{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj8yFP4aDtqvqDmvqYig","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SYoELR7aF7jj2xTz2cqBP3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":753,"cache_read_input_tokens":28653,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":753},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"2c396914-06cc-4924-82cf-c9f213a7a3ee","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:26.149Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj8yF9vyP9LXjXnY8CYn","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01SYoELR7aF7jj2xTz2cqBP3":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/README.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SYoELR7aF7jj2xTz2cqBP3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# my-3D2dge handoff package (v0.88.0)\n2\t\n3\tEverything a coding agent needs to build, run, test and check a game on the full my-3D2dge engine, in one folder of text\n4\tfiles. You don't need the engine's repository, and you don't need to read the engine's source below its header.\n5\t\n6\t**The engine is yours to change.** This copy is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit: `my-3d2dge.js`\n7\tis the engine, readable, with every module, and every page and test here loads it (`modules/` and `my-3d2dge-agent.js`\n8\tare separate copies). If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine\n9\tlacks.\n10\t\n11\t## Start here\n12\t\n13\t1. **Read `API.md`**, the API card: a complete game to start from, what x, y and z mean in each view, the rules, the\n14\t   common mistakes and the API by area. It is the one page to read whole.\n15\t2. **Start from a game, not an empty file.** The kit for your genre (`kits/`): the card's Genre playbook names one for\n16\t   each genre, and `kits/README.md` says which file is which. `kits/<id>.js` is a complete game in that genre, readable\n17\t   (its title, play and end, the modules it rests on, sound, touch controls), `kits/<id>.html` plays it, and\n18\t   `node kits/test.mjs <id>` runs it headless. Or the template, `game/`: a small complete game (climbing, a guard with\n19\t   sight, an HTML HUD, scenes) that `game/index.html` plays and `game/test.mjs` tests without a browser. Copy one into\n20\t   `game/` and make it yours.\n21\t3. **Search `API-INDEX.md`** when you need a name: every public name in the engine, one line each, with its signature,\n22\t   units and defaults, and the height rules at the top. `grep -n \"ledge\\|landing\" API-INDEX.md`. Don't read it whole.\n23\t4. **`AI_GUIDE.md`** has the recipes: genre by genre, the look to aim for, the frame order, heights, recorded sessions.\n24\t5. **Don't build a pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen or a loading screen**: every game has them (`API.md`,\n25\t   \"Standard features\"). Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: { items, settings, controls, quit } })`; heavy setup goes in\n26\t   `game.start({ boot: { steps } })`.\n27\t6. **Objects without drawing code:** read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before you draw a barrel, a table or a tree.\n28\t   `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws one of the core library's in every view; `node tools/asset-find.mjs\n29\t   \"market stall\"` searches it and the store (`src/assets/sets/`: about a thousand objects, materials and sprites,\n30\t   each with where it came from), and `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` draws one you wrote, to look at\n31\t   before you use it.\n32\t7. **A game in real 3D** (first person, a light gun, a racer on banked roads, flight, a dungeon crawled in 3D, fixed\n33\t   cameras): the 2D views have no perspective camera, but the engine has a 3D add-on, `World3D` (three.js r182 and\n34\t   Rapier 0.19.3 under the 2D engine, which keeps the HUD, menus and sound). It is not in this folder: take\n35\t   `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` (its header is its manual), `vendor/` and the 3D kit closest to your game\n36\t   (`src/starter3d/`) from the repository, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge, and see them play at\n37\t   my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d.\n38\t\n39\t## What is here\n40\t\n41\t| File | What it is |\n42\t| --- | --- |\n43\t| `my-3d2dge.js` | the full engine, readable (one file, no dependencies): the core, then every module |\n44\t| `modules/` | each engine module on its own, when there are any (one general system; its header is its manual), to load after the agent edition |\n45\t| `my-3d2dge-agent.js` | the agent edition: the essential engine, whose header is a shorter manual (a game written for it runs on the full engine) |\n46\t| `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` | the card, the index, the guide |\n47\t| `kits/` | the 2D genre kits, readable: each kit's game (`<id>.js`) and the page that plays it (`<id>.html`), the table of them (`README.md`) and their headless test (`test.mjs`) |\n48\t| `game/` | the template game: `index.html`, `game.js`, `test.mjs` |\n49\t| `tools/check.mjs` | plays a game in headless Chromium, switches its views, and reports errors, what is on screen, and `game.debug.snapshot()` |\n50\t| `tools/filmstrip.mjs` | records frames into one picture, to judge motion (`--seed 1` makes it repeatable, `--compare` marks what changed) |\n51\t| `tools/replay.mjs` | replays a recorded play session exactly (`E.session`, a bug seen on a phone) |\n52\t| `docs/ASSETS.md` | the asset card: still objects and materials as data (`r.shape`, `map.solid`, `E.MATERIALS`), the core library, writing one |\n53\t| `tools/asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs` | search the core library and the store; draw an object in every view and lint it; cut the few entries a game uses into a set of its own (`asset-lib.mjs` is theirs) |\n54\t| `src/assets/` | the asset store: sets of objects and materials converted from CC0 libraries (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG), with their catalogs. A game loads one with its script and `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`; the Asset Lab shows them all at my-3d2dge.vercel.app/asset-lab |\n55\t| `package.json` | pins Playwright for the tools |\n56\t\n57\tNot here: the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows. They are on the site\n58\t(my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); here a 3D game is built on the 2D\n59\tengine's views (`kits/README.md`, 3D).\n60\t\n61\t## Run, test, check\n62\t\n63\t```sh\n64\tnpm install                          # Playwright, for the tools (once)\n65\tnpx playwright install chromium      # its browser (once)\n66\tnode game/test.mjs                   # the game's headless test: no browser, a second\n67\tnode tools/check.mjs game/index.html # play it in a browser: errors, screenshots, its snapshot\n68\tnode kits/test.mjs arcade            # a kit, headless: it plays, and its proof gives the same hash twice\n69\tnode tools/check.mjs kits/arcade.html#arcade/maze   # a kit in a browser, at a part (its deep link)\n70\tnode tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html --steps \"wait:800 press:Enter wait:500 rec:16:2 down:KeyD wait:600\" --seed 1\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\tOpen `game/index.html` in a browser to play (or serve the folder: `npx serve`). To hand a single file to a person, inline\n74\tthe two scripts into the page, writing every `</script` inside them as `<\\/script` (the agent edition's header has one,\n75\tand the page would end there).\n76\t\n77\t## Testing a game without a browser\n78\t\n79\t`new E.Game()` is headless in Node (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, sound or DOM, and `draw` is skipped unless\n80\tthe game is made with `draw: true` (the template is): then each `advance` draws a frame on a stand-in, and a draw error\n81\tlands in `game.errors`, as it would on a player's screen.\n82\t\n83\t- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps of 1/120 s at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates.\n84\t- `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step, `hold('right')` holds one down, `hold('right', false)` lets go.\n85\t- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }` is your game's handle: tests read it, and\n86\t  `tools/check.mjs` and `tools/replay.mjs` print it.\n87\t- `game.stop()`, `game.start()` and `game.dispose()` stop, resume and end a game.\n88\t- `game.go('play')` fades for `game.fadeTime` (.22 s) before the new scene enters: advance past it, or set\n89\t  `game.fadeTime = 0` in a test.\n90\t- A scene change clears `game.after` and `game.every` timers (`{ keep: true }` outlives it); those made before the first\n91\t  scene live into it. It ends a hit-stop; `game.timeScale` stays (restore a slow motion with a kept timer). A boot step's `game.go(name)` enters that scene when the boot ends.\n92\t- Draw changes nothing, and nothing your update or input relies on (a click target, what is on screen) may be made only\n93\t  in `draw`: `E.Menu`'s rows come from its update (`menu.rowAt(i)` is entry i's box, for a test's click), and the camera\n94\t  (`game.cam`) says where the screen is in update.\n95\t- `E.store` keeps values for the visit in Node, which has no `localStorage` (`E.store.clear()` between games in one test).\n96\t- A script's `tap` and `hold` are no device: `input.label()` keeps showing the keys or buttons the player used last.\n97\t\n98\tThe same inputs give the same game on every machine and at every refresh rate: the clock is an exact fixed step.\n99\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"8cd88059-3679-4644-b177-7c710addbb0c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:26.156Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/README.md","content":"# my-3D2dge handoff package (v0.88.0)\n\nEverything a coding agent needs to build, run, test and check a game on the full my-3D2dge engine, in one folder of text\nfiles. You don't need the engine's repository, and you don't need to read the engine's source below its header.\n\n**The engine is yours to change.** This copy is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit: `my-3d2dge.js`\nis the engine, readable, with every module, and every page and test here loads it (`modules/` and `my-3d2dge-agent.js`\nare separate copies). If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine\nlacks.\n\n## Start here\n\n1. **Read `API.md`**, the API card: a complete game to start from, what x, y and z mean in each view, the rules, the\n   common mistakes and the API by area. It is the one page to read whole.\n2. **Start from a game, not an empty file.** The kit for your genre (`kits/`): the card's Genre playbook names one for\n   each genre, and `kits/README.md` says which file is which. `kits/<id>.js` is a complete game in that genre, readable\n   (its title, play and end, the modules it rests on, sound, touch controls), `kits/<id>.html` plays it, and\n   `node kits/test.mjs <id>` runs it headless. Or the template, `game/`: a small complete game (climbing, a guard with\n   sight, an HTML HUD, scenes) that `game/index.html` plays and `game/test.mjs` tests without a browser. Copy one into\n   `game/` and make it yours.\n3. **Search `API-INDEX.md`** when you need a name: every public name in the engine, one line each, with its signature,\n   units and defaults, and the height rules at the top. `grep -n \"ledge\\|landing\" API-INDEX.md`. Don't read it whole.\n4. **`AI_GUIDE.md`** has the recipes: genre by genre, the look to aim for, the frame order, heights, recorded sessions.\n5. **Don't build a pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen or a loading screen**: every game has them (`API.md`,\n   \"Standard features\"). Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: { items, settings, controls, quit } })`; heavy setup goes in\n   `game.start({ boot: { steps } })`.\n6. **Objects without drawing code:** read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before you draw a barrel, a table or a tree.\n   `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws one of the core library's in every view; `node tools/asset-find.mjs\n   \"market stall\"` searches it and the store (`src/assets/sets/`: about a thousand objects, materials and sprites,\n   each with where it came from), and `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` draws one you wrote, to look at\n   before you use it.\n7. **A game in real 3D** (first person, a light gun, a racer on banked roads, flight, a dungeon crawled in 3D, fixed\n   cameras): the 2D views have no perspective camera, but the engine has a 3D add-on, `World3D` (three.js r182 and\n   Rapier 0.19.3 under the 2D engine, which keeps the HUD, menus and sound). It is not in this folder: take\n   `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` (its header is its manual), `vendor/` and the 3D kit closest to your game\n   (`src/starter3d/`) from the repository, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge, and see them play at\n   my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d.\n\n## What is here\n\n| File | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `my-3d2dge.js` | the full engine, readable (one file, no dependencies): the core, then every module |\n| `modules/` | each engine module on its own, when there are any (one general system; its header is its manual), to load after the agent edition |\n| `my-3d2dge-agent.js` | the agent edition: the essential engine, whose header is a shorter manual (a game written for it runs on the full engine) |\n| `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` | the card, the index, the guide |\n| `kits/` | the 2D genre kits, readable: each kit's game (`<id>.js`) and the page that plays it (`<id>.html`), the table of them (`README.md`) and their headless test (`test.mjs`) |\n| `game/` | the template game: `index.html`, `game.js`, `test.mjs` |\n| `tools/check.mjs` | plays a game in headless Chromium, switches its views, and reports errors, what is on screen, and `game.debug.snapshot()` |\n| `tools/filmstrip.mjs` | records frames into one picture, to judge motion (`--seed 1` makes it repeatable, `--compare` marks what changed) |\n| `tools/replay.mjs` | replays a recorded play session exactly (`E.session`, a bug seen on a phone) |\n| `docs/ASSETS.md` | the asset card: still objects and materials as data (`r.shape`, `map.solid`, `E.MATERIALS`), the core library, writing one |\n| `tools/asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs` | search the core library and the store; draw an object in every view and lint it; cut the few entries a game uses into a set of its own (`asset-lib.mjs` is theirs) |\n| `src/assets/` | the asset store: sets of objects and materials converted from CC0 libraries (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG), with their catalogs. A game loads one with its script and `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`; the Asset Lab shows them all at my-3d2dge.vercel.app/asset-lab |\n| `package.json` | pins Playwright for the tools |\n\nNot here: the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows. They are on the site\n(my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); here a 3D game is built on the 2D\nengine's views (`kits/README.md`, 3D).\n\n## Run, test, check\n\n```sh\nnpm install                          # Playwright, for the tools (once)\nnpx playwright install chromium      # its browser (once)\nnode game/test.mjs                   # the game's headless test: no browser, a second\nnode tools/check.mjs game/index.html # play it in a browser: errors, screenshots, its snapshot\nnode kits/test.mjs arcade            # a kit, headless: it plays, and its proof gives the same hash twice\nnode tools/check.mjs kits/arcade.html#arcade/maze   # a kit in a browser, at a part (its deep link)\nnode tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html --steps \"wait:800 press:Enter wait:500 rec:16:2 down:KeyD wait:600\" --seed 1\n```\n\nOpen `game/index.html` in a browser to play (or serve the folder: `npx serve`). To hand a single file to a person, inline\nthe two scripts into the page, writing every `</script` inside them as `<\\/script` (the agent edition's header has one,\nand the page would end there).\n\n## Testing a game without a browser\n\n`new E.Game()` is headless in Node (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, sound or DOM, and `draw` is skipped unless\nthe game is made with `draw: true` (the template is): then each `advance` draws a frame on a stand-in, and a draw error\nlands in `game.errors`, as it would on a player's screen.\n\n- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps of 1/120 s at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates.\n- `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step, `hold('right')` holds one down, `hold('right', false)` lets go.\n- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }` is your game's handle: tests read it, and\n  `tools/check.mjs` and `tools/replay.mjs` print it.\n- `game.stop()`, `game.start()` and `game.dispose()` stop, resume and end a game.\n- `game.go('play')` fades for `game.fadeTime` (.22 s) before the new scene enters: advance past it, or set\n  `game.fadeTime = 0` in a test.\n- A scene change clears `game.after` and `game.every` timers (`{ keep: true }` outlives it); those made before the first\n  scene live into it. It ends a hit-stop; `game.timeScale` stays (restore a slow motion with a kept timer). A boot step's `game.go(name)` enters that scene when the boot ends.\n- Draw changes nothing, and nothing your update or input relies on (a click target, what is on screen) may be made only\n  in `draw`: `E.Menu`'s rows come from its update (`menu.rowAt(i)` is entry i's box, for a test's click), and the camera\n  (`game.cam`) says where the screen is in update.\n- `E.store` keeps values for the visit in Node, which has no `localStorage` (`E.store.clear()` between games in one test).\n- A script's `tap` and `hold` are no device: `input.label()` keeps showing the keys or buttons the player used last.\n\nThe same inputs give the same game on every machine and at every refresh rate: the clock is an exact fixed step.\n","numLines":99,"startLine":1,"totalLines":99}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SYoELR7aF7jj2xTz2cqBP3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj8yFP4aDtqvqDmvqYig","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UkPamh3m8iKfAvzn49jbDt","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":753,"cache_read_input_tokens":28653,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":753},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"8f00af6d-973b-4522-8b5e-39dafdfb3012","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:26.850Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj8yF9vyP9LXjXnY8CYn","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01UkPamh3m8iKfAvzn49jbDt":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UkPamh3m8iKfAvzn49jbDt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# my-3D2dge guide for AI models\n2\t\n3\tThis guide is for an AI (or a person) asked to build, port, remaster or remix a game with my-3D2dge. `API.md` is the compact reference, read whole: every single file carries it (a genre kit, `dist/my-3d2dge.html`, the compact one), and so does the handoff package. This guide explains the ideas, the workflow for remaking a classic, recipes for each genre, and the checks to run before handing a game back: search it for what you need. Its paths are the repository's (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); the handoff package's `README.md` says which of them it holds. Everything here matches `engine/my-3d2dge.js` v0.88.0.\n4\t\n5\tA coding agent that reads files gets the handoff package (`dist/handoff/`: the full engine readable, the cards, the tools, every 2D kit's game and a template game) or the agent edition alone (`dist/my-3d2dge-agent.js`: the essential engine in one readable file, its header, about 8k tokens, a complete manual with three example games). Games written for the agent edition run unchanged on the full engine, which adds the lighting, props, backdrops, touch controls and camera tools this guide also covers.\n6\t\n7\t## North star\n8\t\n9\tmy-3D2dge is a general-purpose retro-modern 2D engine built for AI models. It exists so that any model can:\n10\t\n11\t- port, remaster, reimagine and remix games from the 8-bit to 64-bit eras;\n12\t- make spiritual successors that feel like modern remasters, with better visuals, physics and controls;\n13\t- mash up mechanics across titles and genres.\n14\t\n15\tIt aims for **consistent quality**: the engine carries the hard parts (feel, physics, rendering, sound, UI), so each game only has to describe its rules and content.\n16\t\n17\t## The mental model\n18\t\n19\t1. **The world is 3D, the camera is 2D.** Game logic works in world units: x east, y south, z up, one tile = 16 units. A `View` projects world points to screen pixels. Switch the view and the same game renders as isometric, three-quarter, top-down, brawler, side-on or any custom angle.\n20\t2. **Characters are puppets, not sprite sheets.** A `Humanoid` is a 3D skeleton posed by math every step (IK legs and arms, gait from velocity, jumps, attacks, cape cloth). A `Blob` is a squashy sphere. Both project through the current view. Small objects can be string sprites (`E.sprite`).\n21\t3. **Genre kits.** Each kit handles one hard problem and knows nothing about genres, so they combine freely:\n22\t   - `TileMap` + `Body`: ground-plane levels and physics.\n23\t   - `PlatformMap` + `Platformer`: side-scrolling levels and a platformer controller.\n24\t   - `Bullets` + `E.pattern`: projectiles.\n25\t   - `Dialog` + `Menu`: text boxes and menus.\n26\t   - scenes: title, play, game over.\n27\t   - `game.audio`: chip sound.\n28\t\n29\t## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n30\t\n31\t1. **Name the core loop.** Mega Man: run, jump, shoot, reach the boss. Zelda: explore rooms, fight, find keys, open doors. 1942: dodge, shoot, survive waves, beat the boss. Write it as one sentence at the top of the game.\n32\t2. **Pick the view and the kit.** Use the genre playbook in `API.md`. A side-scroller is `PlatformMap` + `Platformer` in `'side'`; a top-down game is `TileMap` + `Body`. Pick a console resolution (`res: 'ps1'`, 320 x 240, or `'snes'`) so pixels are whole and the HUD's are known, and `expand: true` so the game fills any screen: the resolution is then the least it shows, and the HUD anchors to `r.W` and `r.H`.\n33\t3. **Start from the closest kit.** Each genre has one, a complete small game with gameplay, HUD, win and lose, sound and touch controls: the playbook in `API.md` names it (in a single file its slice sits between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; in the handoff package it is `kits/<id>.js`). Change it into your game, with its own title and game-over scenes, and ship only that. Do not start from an empty file.\n34\t4. **Rebuild the content.** Levels are ASCII rows with a legend, enemies are small factories, items are sprites. Keep the original's layout ideas but make your own maps; for a remix, change the rules on purpose.\n35\t5. **Modernize.** The engine gives you most of it for free:\n36\t   - forgiving controls: coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height, input buffering;\n37\t   - hit feedback: hit-stop, shake, sparks, damage numbers;\n38\t   - smooth animation, lighting and glow;\n39\t   - a 2.5D view (`views: ['side', 'brawler']`);\n40\t   - pause, save data, touch controls.\n41\t6. **Tune the feel** with the feel numbers in `API.md`, then check the game with the skeleton overlay (`rig.debug(r)`) and in slow motion (`game.timeScale = .25`).\n42\t7. **Verify** with the checker and look at the screenshots (see the checklist at the end).\n43\t\n44\t## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\n45\tPlayers judge a remake in the first screenshot. The engine's defaults reach SNES quality by themselves; games reach the PS1/N64 bar (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa) when they use every layer of the look kit.\n46\t\n47\t**Why games look cheap, and the fix for each:**\n48\t- *Tiny stick characters.* Use the HD Humanoid (the default) at `size: 1.2-1.4` on a 320x240 `res`, pick a `build` (`'heroic'` for tall heroes, `'bulky'` for brawlers and bosses, `'chibi'` for Mana-style RPGs), and dress every character: outfit, hair, hat, armor, sleeves, cape, and a palette of 4-6 colors per character (skin, hair, cloth, pants, boot, trim). Give enemies different silhouettes (hunch, bulky, blobs with `ears`, `horns`, `wings`, `feet`, `tail`, `mouth: 'fangs'`). Use poses (`pose: 'cheer'` on victory, `'cast'` for spells, `'guard'`, `'kneel'`, `'down'` for the defeated) instead of leaving everyone in the idle stance.\n49\t- *Empty backgrounds.* Side views get an `E.Backdrop` (a preset or custom layers of mountains, castle, forest, city, clouds, fog) with parallax. Ground views get textured floors from `E.tex.*` with floor tags (water, lava shimmer), and walls with a material: `face: 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'`. Never leave every wall the same brick.\n50\t- *Flat colors.* Shade everything with `E.tones(hex)`: `deep` and `sh` on the shadow side, `base`, `lt` on lit edges, a `hi` sparkle. Shadows lean cool, highlights warm; never shade by only darkening.\n51\t- *Bare rooms.* Put 3-8 props on every screen with `r.prop`: candles, chandeliers and stained-glass windows in castles; crates, barrels, signs and lamps in towns; trees, bushes, flowers, rocks and grass outdoors; crystals, bones, skulls, cobwebs and vines in caves; awnings, lanterns, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and fire drums on streets; sandbags, cactus and palms in deserts; mushrooms in forests. Tint props with `color` so they match the palette, and set `E.style.propSize = 1.4` when heroes are size 1.2+ so a door is taller than the hero.\n52\t- *Weak hits.* Every hit gets hit-stop (`game.freeze(.04-.08)`), shake, sparks and a sound. Every death and explosion gets `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` and, for big ones, `game.flash`. Pickups get `particles.glints`.\n53\t- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild, Arkham) wants an HTML HUD instead: `E.Shell` (below), as the STEALTH slice and the template game do; its dialogue stays `E.Dialog` with portraits.\n54\t- *Faceless dialog.* Every talking character gets a portrait: `talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig })`, and `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` on HUDs and RPG party and status screens.\n55\t- *Muddy lighting.* Use lights for mood, not darkness: a few warm lights (`r.prop(..., { light: true })`) over an ambient of .4-.6; GPU lighting in top-down dungeons.\n56\t\n57\t**Art recipes by classic** (combine with the genre recipes below):\n58\t- *Symphony of the Night:* `res: 'ps1'`, the `'castle-night'` backdrop, PlatformMap `stone` tiles with `moss` and `brick` walls, `'back'` tiles for the rear wall, candles and candelabras every few tiles, chandeliers, stained-glass `window` props, banners and statues. The hero is `build: 'heroic'`, `outfit: 'coat'`, `hair: 'long'`, `cape`, pale skin and silver hair, and the sword chops in the screen plane by itself in the side view; enemies are skeletons (`build: 'skeleton'`, add `outfit: 'robe'` and a staff for a lich), bats (`Blob` with `wings: true, mouth: 'fangs'`), cobwebs and vines on the walls.\n59\t- *Metal Slug:* the `'desert'` backdrop, `ground` and `metal` tiles, crates, barrels, sandbags, palms and cactus, `broken` pillars, `particles.explosion` everywhere (with `particles.ground` set so debris bounces on platforms), `bulky` soldiers with `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` while firing, and screen shake on every blast.\n60\t- *Legend of Mana / Secret of Mana:* the `threequarter` view, `E.tex.grass` / `dirt` / `water` floors by floor tag (a river with `block: true` and a plank bridge), houses of `timber` or `plaster` walls under `tiles` roofs, `hedge` walls with `leaves` tops and `rock` cliffs with `grass` tops, trees, bushes, flowers, fences and mushrooms as props, `chibi` characters with `face: { eyes: 'big' }` and bright palettes, rabbites (`Blob` with `ears: 'rabbit', feet: true`), and dialog boxes with portraits.\n61\t- *Klonoa and 2.5D platformers:* PlatformMap in the `brawler` view (tiles show their top faces), a bright `'day'` or `'forest'` backdrop, props in front of and behind the play plane (`y` of -12 and +12), and a soft camera `lead`.\n62\t- *Final Fight / Streets of Rage:* the `brawler` view, a long street TileMap with brick and `plaster` building walls, awnings, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and burning fire drums along the sidewalk, `bulky` heroes and thugs with varied palettes, and big impacts.\n63\t\n64\t## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\n65\tBroken animation ruins a good-looking game faster than flat art. The Humanoid rig animates procedurally, so use its vocabulary instead of moving joints by hand:\n66\t- **Moves, not raw specs.** `new E.Attack('overhead')`, `new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'])`. Every move in `E.MOVES` winds up, strikes with a lunge, step, lean and twist, follows through, holds, and eases back to the stance. It never sweeps backwards through its own arc.\n67\t- **A move set per character.** A knight gets `overhead`, `thrust` and `spin`; a mage `cast`; a rogue `jab`, `thrust` and `backslash`; a monk `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse` and `flyingkick`; a brute `haymaker`, `bash` and `sweep`; a beast `claw`. Two heroes must never share one swing.\n68\t- **Stances and poses between attacks.** Brawlers walk with `stance: 'guard'`, knights with `stance: 'ready'`. Win with `pose: 'cheer'`, cast with `'cast'`, block with `'block'` (weapons) or `'guard'` (fists), duck with `'crouch'`, taunt with `'hips'` or `'wave'`, fall with `down`, die with `'die'` (stagger, knees, topple), and climb ladders with `climb: true`. Faces act too: `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'`, and a Dialog portrait's mouth moves while its line types.\n69\t- **Enemies telegraph.** Give enemy attacks a longer `wind` (.2-.35 s) than the hero's, so players can read and dodge them. Flash and knock back on hit, lie `down` on death.\n70\t- **Check the motion.** `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game.html#scene --steps \"... rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records the swing frame by frame. Look for the wind-up, the strike and a clean settle. Add `--seed 1` for a repeatable strip, and `--compare before.html` (a copy of the page from before your change) to see exactly which pixels your change moved.\n71\t\n72\t## Hard rules\n73\t\n74\t1. **Game logic in world units, `dt` everywhere.** Never store screen pixels. Convert only when drawing (`r.w(x, y, z)`) or reading the mouse (`game.mouseGround()`). `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, so the same inputs play the same at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz.\n75\t2. **Draw only with engine primitives** (`E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props, `r.*` helpers). `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText`, canvas gradients and scaled `drawImage` anti-alias and break the pixel look. Translucency goes through the engine helpers (`px.blend`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc`, `r.shadow`): stepped alpha with blend modes by default (PS1 / N64 / SNES color math), or ordered dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for NES, Game Boy and Genesis looks.\n76\t3. **Anything that overlaps goes through the queue**: `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue`. Only backgrounds are drawn directly (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`, parallax shapes).\n77\t4. **Animate with continuous values** (phases, velocities, eased timers), never frame lists.\n78\t5. **Read input in `update`**: `pressed` for one-shot actions, `down` for held, `buffered` + `consume` for combat.\n79\t6. **A game is one script on the engine:** in a single file, the code between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; with files, `game.js` beside the `index.html` that loads the engine (inline both into the page to hand a person one file). **The engine is yours to change too.** Your copy (a clone of the repository, the handoff package, a single file) is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit. The readable engine is `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `engine/modules/` in the repository (`node tools/build.mjs` rebuilds every page from them), `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package (every page and test there loads it), and the script marked \"inlined from engine/my-3d2dge.js\" in `dist/my-3d2dge.html`; a genre kit's is minified. If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine lacks.\n80\t7. **Don't rebuild the standard features** (below): the pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen and pausing on focus loss come with every game. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`.\n81\t\n82\t## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\n83\tEvery `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and the player's choices are saved per game.\n84\t- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (when your `E.Shell` has one) and Quit to title (it asks first; Keep playing has the focus). Arrows, d-pad and stick move, Enter or A chooses, Escape or B goes back one screen, P or Start closes the menu. The game sees none of those presses.\n85\t- **Settings** (`game.settings`): Display (full screen; pixel scaling for a fixed `res`: Auto, Sharp, Fill; a screen filter: Clean, Scanlines or CRT, drawn as the picture reaches the canvas, never on the game's buffer, and off under a test harness, so screenshots and filmstrips stay clean), Sound (sound, volume, music, effects, the audio offset with its calibration), Comfort (reduce motion, screen shake, screen flashes), and yours. Saved per game with a version (`E.store` key `settings:` + the page's path, or `id`); a bad saved value falls back to its default, and where storage is blocked the game plays on (`game.settings.saved` is false).\n86\t- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow keys and the d-pad stay with the menus, so a player can't lock themselves out), the controller (dead zone, invert aim, button symbols: Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo or positions, vibration) and the touch buttons (always, automatic or hidden; right or left side; size; opacity). Restore defaults resets the controls and never touches a save.\n87\t- **Full screen** (`game.fullscreen`): a phone's first tap goes full screen, the top-right button and the menu toggle it, and once the player leaves it the game never forces it back. Where a browser can't (an iPhone), the settings say to use Share, then Add to Home Screen; the page carries the home-screen app tags.\n88\t- **Pausing by itself**: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden, the window loses focus or the controller in use is unplugged (`game.pauseReason` says which), and only the player resumes.\n89\t- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu stays on a touch screen with no touch buttons; in play they're gone. Keep a title's art clear of the top-right 110 x 60 CSS pixels, or pass `corner: false`.\n90\t- **Phones**: a viewport that reaches the safe areas (pinch zoom left alone), no page scrolling, zooming, selection or callouts on the canvas, touch buttons inside the safe areas, a touch stick with a dead zone that also presses the directions past halfway (menus, dialog choices, ladders), a bottom dialog that keeps clear of the touch buttons, menus picked by a tap or click on an entry and dialogs turned by a tap on the box (a title needs no touch buttons), and a fixed `res` filling a high-density screen (`expand: true` fills every screen).\n91\t- **Boot** (a loading screen and a crash report): heavy setup (a procedural level, a big table, baked caches, data from a promise) goes in boot steps, run one a frame behind a boot screen that shows the system's checks (engine, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage, input), each step, a progress bar and lines of the game's own, like the Sims' \"Reticulating splines\"; then the game draws a warm-up frame under it, so play starts without a hitch. `game.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot: { title: 'VESPER', version: '1.0', steps: [['Raising the harbour', () => { map = buildHarbour(); }], ['Waking the guards', () => loadGuards()]], messages: ['Counting the bells', 'Oiling the hinges'], min: 0 } })`. A step may return a promise; headless (and with `loop: false`) the steps run at once and `await game.booted` waits for any promise. If a step throws, or anything fails before play, the screen becomes a report a player can screenshot: what failed, the error, a short code, the engine and game versions, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage and input, with Try again, Copy report and Continue. `E.diagnose()` gives the same report any time (`.text`, `.short`); after play, errors go to the red box with its short line. `E.bootSplash(title)` is the screen's markup for the very start of `<body>`, so it shows while the page's scripts load (the starter files and the handoff template have it). Keep a boot short: no step should wait on purpose (`min` is for a splash you want seen).\n92\t- **Juice that respects the player**: `game.shake` and `game.flash` follow the comfort settings, `game.hitFx` rumbles a gamepad (the vibration setting; `rumble: false` skips it), and `game.input.rumble(strength, seconds)` does it for your own moments.\n93\t\n94\tAdjust them:\n95\t```js\n96\tconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER',\n97\t  menu: {\n98\t    items: [['Restart level', () => game.go('play', { level })]],                 // pause-menu buttons after Resume\n99\t    settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true },                      // yours, beside the standard ones\n100\t      { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal', help: 'How hard enemies hit' }],\n101\t    controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: 'Magic', debug: false },                  // names on the Controls screen; false hides one\n102\t    quit: 'title'                                                                  // the scene Quit goes to (false: no Quit)\n103\t  } });\n104\tconst hurt = { Easy: .5, Normal: 1, Hard: 1.5 }[game.settings.difficulty];       // read them anywhere, any time\n105\tr.text('PRESS ' + game.input.label('jump') + ' TO JUMP', 8, 8, '#fff');           // 'SPACE', 'A' or '✕', after the player's rebinding\n106\t```\n107\t- Leave parts out: `menu: false` (you draw your own pause: the canvas PAUSED overlay comes back), `fullscreen: 'button'` (no tap) or `false`, `corner: false`, `autoPause: false`, `id: 'mygame'` (the name the settings are saved under).\n108\t- A canvas title menu can offer OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')` opens the settings over the title, which waits; `game.menu('controls')` the controls.\n109\t- From code: `game.settings.set('volume', .5)` (checked, saved, applied), `game.settings.reset('Sound')`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (browsers allow it only from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`.\n110\t- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n111\t- The agent edition has `game.settings`, `game.fullscreen`, the corner, the pausing and `input.label`, but not the menu screens: its corner's menu button pauses and resumes.\n112\t\n113\tWhat stays your game's job, because only the game knows it:\n114\t- Every gameplay scene `pausable: true`, so the menu and the pausing reach it; titles and game overs not.\n115\t- Prompts from `game.input.label(action)` (or `ui.hud({ prompt: { act: 'interact' } })`), never hard-coded keys; touch buttons (`touch: [...]`) for every action a level needs (the stick moves).\n116\t- The assists your mechanics call for, as settings: difficulty, aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle for a sustained action, hints after repeated failure.\n117\t- Saves: `game.saves` (three slots and an autosave with their place, level and time played; versioned; `menu: { saves: true }` for the pause menu's Save and Load and the standard screen, which asks before a save is lost), written at checkpoints and save points (not on page close).\n118\t- Information never in color alone; flashes short (`game.flash` already follows the settings).\n119\t\n120\t## Frame order\n121\t\n122\t`game.start(...)` runs this every animation frame:\n123\t\n124\t1. The clock adds the frame's real time (at most 50 ms) to what is left from the last frame, and runs one step for each whole 1/120 s of it: 2 steps a frame at 60 Hz, 1 at 120 Hz, 1 or 0 at 144 Hz. Each step ticks input, then (except during hit-stop and pause) runs the timers, the current scene's `update(dt)`, particles and the camera. The rest waits for the next frame: `game.alpha` (0..1) is how much of a step it holds, for interpolating drawing.\n125\t   - Slow motion (`game.timeScale = .3`) makes each step shorter (`dt = 1/120 * .3`), never fewer, so it stays smooth. Above 1 a step is split so none is longer than 1/120 s.\n126\t   - Paused, or at `timeScale` 0, the steps still run with `dt` 0, so input keeps ticking.\n127\t2. The renderer clears the buffer and positions the camera.\n128\t3. The scene's `draw(r)`. Draw backgrounds first, then register decals, queue items, actors, sprites, overlays and lights.\n129\t4. The renderer finishes the frame, in this order:\n130\t   1. particles and afterimages are queued;\n131\t   2. decals draw (ground marks, shadows, telegraphs);\n132\t   3. the queue sorts and draws by depth;\n133\t   4. lighting darkens the frame;\n134\t   5. overlays draw on top (HUD, dialog, menus, damage numbers);\n135\t   6. the frame is presented with a sub-pixel camera offset.\n136\t5. With GPU lighting active, every queue item is also drawn into a hidden info image, and WebGPU does the lighting and presentation.\n137\t\n138\tAn error in `update` or `draw` does not stop the loop. It is shown in a red box on screen and logged once to the console (`game.errors` keeps the list).\n139\t\n140\t## Systems in more depth\n141\t\n142\t### Views and resolution\n143\t- Presets: `iso` (yaw 45, pitch 30), `threequarter` (pitch 55), `topdown` (pitch 80), `brawler` (pitch 25), `side` (pitch 0).\n144\t- `overhead` (pitch 90, scale 1) makes world x/y equal screen pixels, for shooters, puzzle and arcade games. It is not in the default view cycle.\n145\t- Make your own with `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost)`.\n146\t- `res` fixes the internal resolution with whole-number scaling. Without it, the size adapts to the window between `minH` and `maxW` × `maxH`; `portrait: { maxH }` lets the view grow on a screen held upright (a phone), so it fills the screen.\n147\t- `res` with `expand: true` (what the starter kits use) fills any window with no bars: `res` is the least the screen shows, and `r.W` × `r.H` grows on the long side (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427×240 of `'ps1'`, an ultrawide 640×270, a phone held upright 320×690). The game's job is what fills the extra space, edge to edge, with the HUD over the world's corners (never a band of nothing). The kits show the ways. A wider screen shows more level (the adventure's neighbouring rooms, dimmed a little: they wait) or widens the play area itself (the shooter's waves spread across the whole screen). A taller one shows more world (the forest round the adventure's rooms, the RPG's sky darkening to stars), zooms a side view in (the platformer, the brawler and the RPG's battle at 1.5x, a scene's `camera.tall`, the battle's two sides closing up to fit) and lifts the level onto a band below it (the platformer's soil, the brawler's foreground wall). A painted backdrop is painted at the screen's size and zoom, its ground meeting the floor's edge. Keep prompts clear of `game.input.touchArea()` and HUDs clear of `game.cornerArea()` (the menu button stays at the top right on a phone). On a phone, make the controls smart: a button there only when it has something to do (`input.touchSet`: TALK beside someone), taps on what you see (the RPG's commands and foes), a thumb that steers from anywhere on the screen (`input.steer = 'drag'`, `input.drag()`: the shooter's ship goes 1.5x as far, so one swipe crosses it), what a phone player shouldn't have to hold done for them (the shooter fires by itself), buttons a thumb hits (44 CSS px and more: the RPG's battle pads, with AUTO), and a camera that never shows ground a fighter can't walk to (the brawler stops at a fight's lock). And keep the frame a phone's: a 120 Hz screen gives 8.3 ms, and frames that miss it (their cost, or the GPU behind them) are drawn every other refresh (`game.drawEvery`), so draw background characters at half rate (`r.actor(..., { every: 2, key })`: the adventure's townsfolk) and leave GPU lighting to a computer (its main pointer fine). Settings, Display, Show frame rate reads it on the device, and Frame rate lets the player fix it (60: every other refresh of a 120 Hz screen; Full: every refresh). `npm run test:phone` checks the kits on an emulated phone.\n148\t- `game.views` limits view cycling (`game.nextView()`) and tells the checker which views to test.\n149\t\n150\t### Scenes\n151\tA scene is `{ enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera }`.\n152\t- `game.go('play', data)` switches before the next step; timers, tweens, scripts and particles are cleared (weather too) and the camera snaps. `{ fade: 'iris', at: hero }` (or `'wipe'`, `'mosaic'`, `'swirl'`, `'dissolve'`, `'white'`; `seconds` each way) picks the transition: an iris closing on the hero for a door, a swirl into a battle, a mosaic into a dream. A world point `at` is placed by each scene's camera; `[x, y]` is a point on the screen. Reduce motion turns each into a plain fade.\n153\t- `pausable: true` lets `pause` (Escape, P, Start, the touch START) toggle `game.paused`: the pause menu opens (the canvas PAUSED overlay with `menu: false`) and the music is ducked. It also lets the page pause the scene by itself when it is hidden or loses focus.\n154\t\n155\t### Camera\n156\t- `game.follow(hero, { z, lead })` once, or `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update.\n157\t- `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` (or `level.bounds`) keeps it inside the level.\n158\t- `cam.room = [w, h]` snaps the target to the center of the current room; the camera then slides, so Zelda- and Metroid-style room transitions come for free. Freeze gameplay while `cam.moving` is true.\n159\t- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n160\t- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggle the screen. `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (its `onGround`) or nears the screen's edge, so a jump doesn't drag the camera up and down. `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin: 24, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps several things in view, centred between them, zooming out (to at least `zoom[0]`) as they part and back in as they meet: two players, a boss and the hero, a race's leaders. `zoom: false` only centres.\n161\t- Screen shake respects the player's reduced-motion setting.\n162\t\n163\t### Players (two to four on one machine)\n164\t- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `pressed()`, `label()`), plus `player` (2 to 4), `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`: blue, red, green, yellow) and `device`.\n165\t- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player 1 WASD with F G H R T Y (`Input.halfKeys(preset, 'left')`), player 2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` and the numpad (`'right'`), each half taking the preset's actions in turn. `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })` adds one directly (tests, a versus menu); `game.removePlayer(p)`.\n166\t- The standard features follow: each player's prompts name its own keys (`input.label`), a pause says who paused (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller names its player, the Controls screen has a page each. Recorded sessions replay every pad and both halves of the keyboard.\n167\t- One shared screen: `cam.frame(heroes, { margin: 90 })` keeps everyone in view. A split screen: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [hero, hero2] })`, `layout: 'auto'` (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one, a grid for 3 or 4), `'columns'`, `'rows'` or `'grid'`. The scene's `draw(r)` runs once per viewport, its camera its own (`game.viewports[i].follow(t)`, `.focus(x, y)`), with `r.viewport` saying which (`{ i, x, y, w, h }`: draw that player's HUD there); `scene.hud(r)` then runs once over the whole screen. GPU lighting is off while split; a scene's start ends the split.\n168\t\n169\t### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\n170\tThe code every game writes by hand, in the engine and on game time: each pauses with the game, stands still in hit-stop, slows with `timeScale` and ends with the scene (unless `keep: true`), and runs the same at every refresh rate.\n171\t- **Tweens** (Phaser's tweens, Unity's DOTween): `game.tween(target, { x: 10, y: 4 }, seconds, { ease, delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases a target's numbers to new values, from where they are when its delay ends; a door sliding, a title dropping in, a coin flying to the HUD, a camera move. `ease` is a name in `E.ease` (Penner's: `linear`, `inSine`, `outBack`, `outElastic`, `outBounce`, ...; `outQuad` by default) or a function of 0..1. It replaces a timer, a start value and a lerp in every update.\n172\t- **Scripts** (Unity's coroutines): `game.script(function* (game) { ... })` runs a generator in the update step and waits at each `yield`: a number of seconds, a condition (`E.until(fn)` or a function), a tween, another script or a generator (run and waited for), `E.all([...])`, `E.any([...])`, or nothing (one step). A wave timeline is a loop (`for (const w of WAVES) { spawn(w); yield E.any([w.time, () => !enemies.length]); }`); a cutscene is its lines in order (`talk.say(...)`, `yield E.until(() => !talk.open)`, `yield game.tween(cam, ...)`), instead of a state machine with a timer per state. It runs at once to its first `yield`; `.cancel()` stops it and runs its `finally`.\n173\t- **Paths:** `new E.Path(points, { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })` measures a line by distance along it: `at(d)` is a point with its heading (a moving platform's place, an enemy's dive, a rail, a camera track: `d = speed * time`), `nearest(x, y)` the distance along of the nearest point (a racer's progress round the lap, for places), `draw(r)` shows it while you build.\n174\t- **Weather:** `game.weather = { kind, amount, wind, lightning }` (rain, snow, ash, leaves, sand): drops fall through the world's height in ground views and in three layers of parallax in side and overhead views, splash or settle on the floor (`particles.ground`, the platforms' height in a side view), and lightning flashes (the player's Screen flashes setting) and thunders. It is cosmetic: its own seeded random stream, never `Math.random`, so replays and gameplay are untouched. `E.WEATHER` holds the kinds; add your own.\n175\t- **Sprites** (frames, turns, swaps): `E.anim([...frames], fps, { loop, pingpong })` (or one sheet cut into `frames`) is played by `r.sprite(x, y, z, anim, { t })`; `rot` turns a sprite in whole pixels, cached in 16 or 32 steps as the era's games pre-rotated theirs (a ship facing its heading, a spinning blade); `scale` grows it nearest-neighbour; `E.recolor(spr, map)` is a palette swap (the second player, a stronger enemy).\n176\t- **Wrap-around:** `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` keeps a position on a world whose edges meet (Asteroids, Pac-Man's tunnel, a looping stage), `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` the short way across the seam (aim and chase across it), and `wrap: [w, h]` on `r.sprite` and `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge a thing straddles.\n177\t\n178\t### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n179\t- ASCII `rows` and a `legend`: a number is a wall type, a string is a spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn }` combines them.\n180\t- Wall `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH }`. `cut` lowers a wall when it stands between the camera and the floor.\n181\t- `floorTex(x, y, floorTag)` colors the floor per pixel; it is baked once per view. Ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`.\n182\t- `map.set(cx, cy, 0)` opens doors or breaks walls; the floor and wall caches rebuild themselves.\n183\t- Collision is height-aware: `collide(body)` ignores walls lower than `body.z + step`, and `groundAt(x, y, r, z)` returns the surface to stand on.\n184\t- Things standing on a block sort after it, and `r.shadow(..., surfaceZ)` draws on top of blocks.\n185\t- `map.block(cx, cy, on)` and `map.set(...)` bump `map.version`; a `FlowField` rebuilds at its next `update` when it changes.\n186\t- Maps built in code: `new E.TileMap({ w, h, types })` starts as open floor; `map.fill(cx, cy, w, h, id)` puts a block of wall type `id` in (0 clears it), and `map.setFloor(cx, cy, tag, block)` tags a floor cell (`'grass'`, `'water'`) and blocks it or not.\n187\t- Facades come from wall-type options: `windows: { w, h, step, from, lit }` (or `true`), `door: { chance, color }`, `shutters: color`, `balcony: { z, color }`, `arch: { chance }`. For anything else, `types[id].decorate(face)` paints each wall face as it is baked (so it costs nothing per frame): `face.side`, `face.u0..u1` along the wall in world units (patterns line up across cells), `face.z0..z1`, `face.at(u, z)`, `face.quad(u0, z0, u1, z1, color)`, `face.line`, `face.dot`, `face.tones`, `face.view`.\n188\t- A big thing over several blocks (a canopy over a tower, a cart across two cells) sorts by its whole base: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { footprint: [x0, y0, x1, y1] })` lifts it over every block under it. `r.prop` and `r.sprite` take `footprint` too.\n189\t\n190\t### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\n191\tOne rule for collision, standing, sight, reach, navigation and drawing, so a game with roofs never writes its own:\n192\t- World units, one tile = 16, z up. A body's `z` is its feet. A cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)`: 0 on the floor, and a wall is solid from 0 up to its `h` (one column per cell: no passages under things yet).\n193\t- A body stands on the highest top within `z + step` under its footprint (`step` 2, `Body.step`), so a kerb of 2 is walked over and a block of 14 is jumped onto.\n194\t- Eyes are at `z + E.HEIGHTS.eye` (30) and a target is seen at `z + E.HEIGHTS.chest` (22), the heights of a size-1.3 Humanoid. A body's own `eye` and `chest` override them: a crouching hero sets `hero.chest = 8`.\n195\t- \"The same level\" is `|dz| <= E.HEIGHTS.level` (8) unless a call says otherwise. `E.dist` is 3D; `E.inArc` is flat unless you pass `dz`, and `E.overlap` is always flat (pair it with `E.sameLevel`).\n196\t\n197\tThe queries on a `TileMap` (`dir` is an angle in radians or a ground vector `[dx, dy]`; `from` is a body or any `{ x, y, z, r }`):\n198\t- `map.ray(a, b, { eye, target })` walks every cell between two points and returns `{ clear, t, x, y, z }`, where it stops. `map.sight(a, b)` is eyes to chest; `los()` only looks across the ground (a 12-unit planter blocks it, not `sight`).\n199\t- `map.standable(x, y, z, r)`: room to stand, held up, not in deep water.\n200\t- `map.ledge(from, dir, { reach: 18, min: 4, rise: 82, spread: .28 })` → `{ x, y, z, rise, facing, edge: [x, y] }`: the nearest top to pull up onto, and where the hands grip.\n201\t- `map.landing(from, dir, { min: 35, max: 83, rise: 11, drop: 18, apex: 20 })` → `{ x, y, z, d }`: a spot across a gap that a jump arc clears.\n202\t- `map.drop(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, fall }`: what lies past the edge ahead (hop-downs, fall warnings, guards that won't walk off roofs).\n203\t- `map.arcClear(a, b, apex, r)`: a parabola from feet to feet, peaking `apex` above the higher end, clears every top (jumps, throws, aerial takedowns).\n204\t\n205\tReach and perception:\n206\t- `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, facing, dz, sight: map, filter })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]`, nearest first. `dz` defaults to the same level; `arc` is a half angle around `facing`. Melee auto-aim, lock-on, takedowns and interaction prompts use it.\n207\t- `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)` and `E.sameLevel(a, b, tol)`.\n208\t\n209\tNavigation: `new E.FlowField(map, { step: 2, climb: 0, drop: 0, jump: 0 })` walks the tops a body can stand on. Next-door cells connect when their tops differ by at most `step`, or by up to `climb` going up and `drop` going down. A guard on a roof gets a field across the roofs; with `climb` and `drop` set, it chases between levels. With `jump` (the widest gap, world units) it also links roofs across a gap a jump clears, and a climb costs more than a leap, so a chase leaps rather than drop to the street and climb back. `flow.next(x, y)` → `{ x, y, z, kind: 'walk' | 'climb' | 'drop' | 'jump', edge }` says where to head: an AI on the ground presses jump for a `climb` or a `jump` once `edge` (the distance to the side of its cell it leaves by) is under its radius plus a little, and `E.Mover` does the climb or the leap.\n210\t\n211\t```js\n212\tconst seen = map.sight(guard, hero) && E.inArc(guard, guard.facing, hero, 120, .7);   // eyes over planters, not through roofs\n213\tif (input.pressed('jump')) {\n214\t  const up = map.ledge(hero, hero.facing);   // a wall to climb in front?\n215\t  const across = !up && map.landing(hero, hero.facing);   // or a gap to clear?\n216\t  if (up) climbTo(up); else if (across) leapTo(across); else hero.jump(210);\n217\t}\n218\tconst prey = E.targets(hero, guards, { range: 22, arc: 1.2, facing: hero.facing, sight: map })[0];   // the same level by default\n219\tif (prey && input.pressed('attack')) takedown(prey.target);\n220\t```\n221\t\n222\t### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n223\t- Tiles live on the x/z plane; the ASCII top row is the highest.\n224\t- Kinds:\n225\t  - `solid`;\n226\t  - `slope`: `dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left, `from` / `to` (0..1) for gentle slopes; bodies walk up and down them and step up onto low ledges;\n227\t  - `oneway`: you stand on it and jump up through it, and down + jump drops through;\n228\t  - `ladder`: climbable, and you can stand on its top;\n229\t  - `hazard`: not solid; test it with `touching(body, 'hazard')`;\n230\t  - `deco`: drawn in the play plane;\n231\t  - `back`: a darker background wall.\n232\t- Styles give each tile its look: `stone` (with `moss`), `brick`, `wood`, `metal`, `ground` (grass top when open to the sky, with `flower`), `block` (bevelled), `bonus` (`?` block, `glyph` option), `pipe`, `plank`, `spikes`, `ladder`, `liquid`, `plain`; `mat` gives a solid tile's face any material (`E.MATERIALS`).\n233\t- In `side` the level is flat like an NES or SNES game. In a tilted view with yaw 0 (`brawler` or custom), tiles show top faces and the level becomes a 2.5D diorama.\n234\t- `move(body, dt, solids)` moves any box `{ x, z, vx, vz, w, h }` with sub-steps. It reports:\n235\t  - `onGround` and `onOneWay`;\n236\t  - `hitWall`: -1 left, 1 right, 0 none;\n237\t  - `hitCeiling`;\n238\t  - `bumped`: the tile hit from below;\n239\t  - `ground`: the moving solid it stands on.\n240\t- `solids` are extra boxes (moving platforms, elevators, crates); a body standing on one is carried by its `vx` / `vz`.\n241\t\n242\t### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n243\t- Defaults feel like a modern NES remaster: run 95, jump 285 (about 3 tiles), gravity 800 rising and 1150 falling, fall cap 330.\n244\t- Forgiving controls: coyote time 0.09 s, jump buffer 0.13 s, jump cut 0.45 when jump is released early.\n245\t- Optional moves: `airJumps` (double jump), `wallJump` (with wall slide), `dash`, `climb` speed.\n246\t- `update(dt, level, input | intent, solids)`. The same controller runs enemies: pass `{ x, jump, jumpPressed }` from your AI.\n247\t- `rigState(extra)` returns the object `Humanoid.update` wants, including the airborne pose.\n248\t\n249\t### Body (ground-plane physics)\n250\t- Velocity with optional `friction` (per second) and `bounce` (off walls and the floor).\n251\t- `push(ix, iy, iz)` adds knockback; `jump(v)` jumps with `gravity`.\n252\t- It lands on the highest walkable surface: the floor, or the top of a block low enough to step onto.\n253\t- Use it for heroes, enemies, bombs, crates, pickups and balls.\n254\t\n255\t### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n256\t`E.Mover` is the ground views' answer to `Platformer`: a `Body` with a third-person game's movement.\n257\t- `const mover = new E.Mover({ x, y, z })` (the index files its members as `mover.`); each step `mover.update(dt, map, game.input)` with `input: 'THIRDPERSON'` (move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch, J attack, K block, L dodge; touch buttons), or an AI's intent `{ move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }`. Screen directions turn into ground ones through the view.\n258\t- Walk 50, run 85, sprint 125 and sneak 32 with acceleration; a jump with coyote time and a buffer, once a press.\n259\t- A jump press is parkour first: a ledge up to `climbMax` (82) is climbed (`climbUp`; crouch while hanging lets go), a wall up to `vault` (14) is vaulted at a run, a gap ahead is leapt (it aims for the far side's landing), a drop up to `hopMax` (48) is hopped down; otherwise it jumps.\n260\t- Climbing any wall (`climbAny: true`, Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild): a jump press at a wall too tall for a ledge latches on, on the ground or in the air (`mover.wall` is the face, `map.wall(from, dir)`'s: its point, normal, top and facing). The stick climbs as the screen shows it (up the screen is up the wall; an AI's intent: `move: [along, -up]`), it turns outer and inner corners, a jump press leaps up (`climbJump`, which ends on the wall) or, pulled away, pushes off; near the top it pulls up (`climbUp`), at the floor it stands, crouch lets go. Lower `climbMax` (30) so roofs are climbed, not jumped onto. The STEALTH slice and the template do this.\n261\t- Gliding (`glide: true`, Breath of the Wild, Arkham): a jump press in the air, `glideMin` (24) over the ground, opens a sail: it sinks at `glideSink`, flies at `glideSpeed`, takes no fall damage, rises over an updraft (`lift`: a speed, or `m => inUpdraft(m) ? 40 : 0`), and closes on a press, on landing, or at a wall (a climber takes hold). `mover.gliding` says so; the rig hangs under it with its hands up, so draw the sail over them (the template does).\n262\t- Stamina (off until you set `staminaMax`; the template's is 100): climbing, climb jumps, gliding and sprinting spend `mover.stamina` (`tire`), standing refills it (`staminaRegen`); at 0 it lets go of the wall, the glide closes and it can't sprint (`mover.exhausted`) until a third is back. Show it with `ui.hud({ bars: [{ label: 'Stamina', value: mover.stamina, max: mover.staminaMax }] })`.\n263\t- Falls: past `rollAt` (26) at speed it rolls, past `hurtAt` (72) it sets `hurt`. `lastSafe` and `respawn()` bring it back from water and pits (`inHazard`).\n264\t- `mover.rigState(extra)` feeds `rig.update`: its crouch (a sneaking gait), sprint lean, air, landing, or the playing action's pose.\n265\t- Actions (`E.ACTIONS`): `climbUp` (played backwards to climb down), `vault`, `hopDown`, `roll` (invulnerable in its middle), `getUp`, and `takedown`, a paired action that moves two bodies: `mover.play('takedown', { other: guard, aerial, map })`. While `mover.action` plays it moves the body instead of physics; `act.beat`, `act.u`, `act.canCancel`, `act.invulnerable`, `act.otherState()` for the victim's rig. Your own: `E.ACTIONS.slide = { beats: [['go', .4]], setup(A) {}, at(A, beat, u) { return { x, y, z, state } } }`.\n266\t\n267\t### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n268\t- `E.sees(guard, hero, { map, range: 120, fov: 1.6, eye })` → `{ seen, d, angle, exposure }`: inside the cone, and three points on the hero (feet, chest, head) checked against every top with `map.ray`. Crouching shows less and lower; `hero.exposure` (0..1) is cover you set: tall grass, shadow.\n269\t- `E.hears(guard, { x, y, z, loud: 80 }, map)`: 1 at the noise, 0 at its radius, halved through a wall.\n270\t- `const mind = new E.Suspicion({ rise, decay, notice, search, alert, forget })`; each step `mind.update(dt, E.sees(...), hero)` → `'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'` (it rises faster the nearer and more exposed the hero is), `mind.last` (where it last saw or heard), `mind.changed`. An alert guard that loses the hero searches for `forget` seconds; `mind.hear(strength, at)` notices a noise.\n271\t- Drawing: `r.sightCone(guard, { map, range, fov, color, alpha })` (cut short by walls) and `r.marker(x, y, z, { label, color })`, an objective marker that becomes an arrow at the screen's edge.\n272\t\n273\t### Attack (melee timing)\n274\t- `new E.Attack(spec)` owns the wind-up, active and recover timers of one move; the spec is also the Humanoid attack spec.\n275\t- `start()` begins a swing (pass `true` to chain from recover into a combo); `update(dt)` returns the phase that just began, which is where swing sounds go.\n276\t- `hits(targets, test, fn)` calls `fn` once per target per swing while active. `state` feeds `rig.update({ attack })`.\n277\t- `E.Combo([spec1, spec2, spec3], { window })` chains attacks: `press()` flows into the next hit during recover (or shortly after), otherwise restarts. `E.knockback(from, target, speed, up)` pushes the target away.\n278\t- **Special moves** (input sequences): `game.input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing })` is true on the step a fireball's motion ends: down, down-forward, forward, then the button (numpad notation, as fighting games write moves: `'623'` a dragon punch, `'41236'` a half circle, `'66'` a dash), mirrored when the fighter faces left; the button may land with the last direction, and the whole move must come within `window` seconds (.45). `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })` reads any run of inputs (a cheat code, a rhythm pattern); a step joins actions with `+`. Both read `input.history`, the last second of directions (numpad digits) and presses, which an input display can draw.\n279\t\n280\t### Bullets and patterns\n281\t- A pool on the `'ground'` plane (top-down) or the `'side'` plane.\n282\t- Bullets die on walls with a small spark; `hit(targets, fn, team)` tests circles (side targets use their box center).\n283\t- `burst(base, E.pattern.spread(...))` fires a pattern: `dir`, `aim`, `aimSide`, `spread`, `ring`.\n284\t- Bullets glow under GPU lighting. Set `sprite` for custom art and `pierce` to pass through targets.\n285\t\n286\t### Humanoid\n287\t- Proportions: `legUpper legLower hipZ torso shoulderHalf headR armUpper armLower limbW torsoW`.\n288\t- Style: `lean hunch stride swing speedRef`.\n289\t- Gear: `weapon` (`'sword'`, `'gun'`, `'staff'`), `hat` (`'cap'`, `'pointed'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`, `'crown'`), `hood`, `cape`, `eyeGlow`, `colors`.\n290\t- `update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, attack })`.\n291\t- Attacks:\n292\t  - You own the timing: move `phase` from `'wind'` to `'active'` to `'recover'` with your own timers, and pass `u` (0 to 1) within the phase.\n293\t  - `spec.a0` / `a1` are hand angles relative to facing; `z0` / `z1` are heights.\n294\t  - `spin: true` swings a full circle, `kick: true` swings the right foot, `blade: 0` hides the smear.\n295\t- `hand()` and `tip()` give world points for spawning bullets or sparks; `worldOffset(p)` gives where any point in the rig's own frame (a joint in `J`) sits from its feet. `debug(r)` draws the skeleton.\n296\t\n297\t### Audio\n298\t- Browsers start sound only after the first key press or click; the engine waits for it.\n299\t- `sfx(name)` ignores the same sound fired twice in 35 ms, and varies the pitch slightly so repeats don't sound robotic.\n300\t- `music(song)` loops tracks of its own step notation. Every track loops on its own length, so short drum or bass loops can run under long melodies.\n301\t- Built-in songs: `title`, `adventure`, `dungeon`, `boss`, `victory` (plays once). The songs module's songbook has more by mood (`E.Songbook.find('town')`) and jingles.\n302\t- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time (`audio.pos`), not the sound's, so beats land on the same update step headless and on any device, and the sound is held to that clock (it moves its notes to meet it). Time gameplay to `audio.pos`, `audio.at(step, fn)` and `audio.onBeat(fn)`, never to the sound. A song with `sync: true` pauses with the game.\n303\t- Dynamic music: one song in layers rather than cuts. Name the tracks, start the extra ones with `on: false`, and bring them in with `audio.layer('drums', 1, .5)` (the stealth slice's chase); `audio.tempo(bpm)` for a hurry-up; `audio.sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` for a jingle on the next bar; `music(song, { fade: 1 })` to crossfade.\n304\t\n305\t### Text and UI\n306\t- `E.font` is a 5x7 proportional pixel font with lower case (the old 3x5 font is `font: 'tiny'`), with `scale`, `align`, `wrap`, outline, drop `shadow` and `gradient`; `E.font.title` draws extruded gradient logos."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"5dc184ed-6201-476a-8d4b-8dda7a9b37d7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:27.024Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md","content":"# my-3D2dge guide for AI models\n\nThis guide is for an AI (or a person) asked to build, port, remaster or remix a game with my-3D2dge. `API.md` is the compact reference, read whole: every single file carries it (a genre kit, `dist/my-3d2dge.html`, the compact one), and so does the handoff package. This guide explains the ideas, the workflow for remaking a classic, recipes for each genre, and the checks to run before handing a game back: search it for what you need. Its paths are the repository's (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); the handoff package's `README.md` says which of them it holds. Everything here matches `engine/my-3d2dge.js` v0.88.0.\n\nA coding agent that reads files gets the handoff package (`dist/handoff/`: the full engine readable, the cards, the tools, every 2D kit's game and a template game) or the agent edition alone (`dist/my-3d2dge-agent.js`: the essential engine in one readable file, its header, about 8k tokens, a complete manual with three example games). Games written for the agent edition run unchanged on the full engine, which adds the lighting, props, backdrops, touch controls and camera tools this guide also covers.\n\n## North star\n\nmy-3D2dge is a general-purpose retro-modern 2D engine built for AI models. It exists so that any model can:\n\n- port, remaster, reimagine and remix games from the 8-bit to 64-bit eras;\n- make spiritual successors that feel like modern remasters, with better visuals, physics and controls;\n- mash up mechanics across titles and genres.\n\nIt aims for **consistent quality**: the engine carries the hard parts (feel, physics, rendering, sound, UI), so each game only has to describe its rules and content.\n\n## The mental model\n\n1. **The world is 3D, the camera is 2D.** Game logic works in world units: x east, y south, z up, one tile = 16 units. A `View` projects world points to screen pixels. Switch the view and the same game renders as isometric, three-quarter, top-down, brawler, side-on or any custom angle.\n2. **Characters are puppets, not sprite sheets.** A `Humanoid` is a 3D skeleton posed by math every step (IK legs and arms, gait from velocity, jumps, attacks, cape cloth). A `Blob` is a squashy sphere. Both project through the current view. Small objects can be string sprites (`E.sprite`).\n3. **Genre kits.** Each kit handles one hard problem and knows nothing about genres, so they combine freely:\n   - `TileMap` + `Body`: ground-plane levels and physics.\n   - `PlatformMap` + `Platformer`: side-scrolling levels and a platformer controller.\n   - `Bullets` + `E.pattern`: projectiles.\n   - `Dialog` + `Menu`: text boxes and menus.\n   - scenes: title, play, game over.\n   - `game.audio`: chip sound.\n\n## Remake workflow (port, remaster, reimagine, remix)\n\n1. **Name the core loop.** Mega Man: run, jump, shoot, reach the boss. Zelda: explore rooms, fight, find keys, open doors. 1942: dodge, shoot, survive waves, beat the boss. Write it as one sentence at the top of the game.\n2. **Pick the view and the kit.** Use the genre playbook in `API.md`. A side-scroller is `PlatformMap` + `Platformer` in `'side'`; a top-down game is `TileMap` + `Body`. Pick a console resolution (`res: 'ps1'`, 320 x 240, or `'snes'`) so pixels are whole and the HUD's are known, and `expand: true` so the game fills any screen: the resolution is then the least it shows, and the HUD anchors to `r.W` and `r.H`.\n3. **Start from the closest kit.** Each genre has one, a complete small game with gameplay, HUD, win and lose, sound and touch controls: the playbook in `API.md` names it (in a single file its slice sits between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; in the handoff package it is `kits/<id>.js`). Change it into your game, with its own title and game-over scenes, and ship only that. Do not start from an empty file.\n4. **Rebuild the content.** Levels are ASCII rows with a legend, enemies are small factories, items are sprites. Keep the original's layout ideas but make your own maps; for a remix, change the rules on purpose.\n5. **Modernize.** The engine gives you most of it for free:\n   - forgiving controls: coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height, input buffering;\n   - hit feedback: hit-stop, shake, sparks, damage numbers;\n   - smooth animation, lighting and glow;\n   - a 2.5D view (`views: ['side', 'brawler']`);\n   - pause, save data, touch controls.\n6. **Tune the feel** with the feel numbers in `API.md`, then check the game with the skeleton overlay (`rig.debug(r)`) and in slow motion (`game.timeScale = .25`).\n7. **Verify** with the checker and look at the screenshots (see the checklist at the end).\n\n## Visual craft: aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES\nPlayers judge a remake in the first screenshot. The engine's defaults reach SNES quality by themselves; games reach the PS1/N64 bar (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa) when they use every layer of the look kit.\n\n**Why games look cheap, and the fix for each:**\n- *Tiny stick characters.* Use the HD Humanoid (the default) at `size: 1.2-1.4` on a 320x240 `res`, pick a `build` (`'heroic'` for tall heroes, `'bulky'` for brawlers and bosses, `'chibi'` for Mana-style RPGs), and dress every character: outfit, hair, hat, armor, sleeves, cape, and a palette of 4-6 colors per character (skin, hair, cloth, pants, boot, trim). Give enemies different silhouettes (hunch, bulky, blobs with `ears`, `horns`, `wings`, `feet`, `tail`, `mouth: 'fangs'`). Use poses (`pose: 'cheer'` on victory, `'cast'` for spells, `'guard'`, `'kneel'`, `'down'` for the defeated) instead of leaving everyone in the idle stance.\n- *Empty backgrounds.* Side views get an `E.Backdrop` (a preset or custom layers of mountains, castle, forest, city, clouds, fog) with parallax. Ground views get textured floors from `E.tex.*` with floor tags (water, lava shimmer), and walls with a material: `face: 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'`. Never leave every wall the same brick.\n- *Flat colors.* Shade everything with `E.tones(hex)`: `deep` and `sh` on the shadow side, `base`, `lt` on lit edges, a `hi` sparkle. Shadows lean cool, highlights warm; never shade by only darkening.\n- *Bare rooms.* Put 3-8 props on every screen with `r.prop`: candles, chandeliers and stained-glass windows in castles; crates, barrels, signs and lamps in towns; trees, bushes, flowers, rocks and grass outdoors; crystals, bones, skulls, cobwebs and vines in caves; awnings, lanterns, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and fire drums on streets; sandbags, cactus and palms in deserts; mushrooms in forests. Tint props with `color` so they match the palette, and set `E.style.propSize = 1.4` when heroes are size 1.2+ so a door is taller than the hero.\n- *Weak hits.* Every hit gets hit-stop (`game.freeze(.04-.08)`), shake, sparks and a sound. Every death and explosion gets `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` and, for big ones, `game.flash`. Pickups get `particles.glints`.\n- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild, Arkham) wants an HTML HUD instead: `E.Shell` (below), as the STEALTH slice and the template game do; its dialogue stays `E.Dialog` with portraits.\n- *Faceless dialog.* Every talking character gets a portrait: `talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig })`, and `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` on HUDs and RPG party and status screens.\n- *Muddy lighting.* Use lights for mood, not darkness: a few warm lights (`r.prop(..., { light: true })`) over an ambient of .4-.6; GPU lighting in top-down dungeons.\n\n**Art recipes by classic** (combine with the genre recipes below):\n- *Symphony of the Night:* `res: 'ps1'`, the `'castle-night'` backdrop, PlatformMap `stone` tiles with `moss` and `brick` walls, `'back'` tiles for the rear wall, candles and candelabras every few tiles, chandeliers, stained-glass `window` props, banners and statues. The hero is `build: 'heroic'`, `outfit: 'coat'`, `hair: 'long'`, `cape`, pale skin and silver hair, and the sword chops in the screen plane by itself in the side view; enemies are skeletons (`build: 'skeleton'`, add `outfit: 'robe'` and a staff for a lich), bats (`Blob` with `wings: true, mouth: 'fangs'`), cobwebs and vines on the walls.\n- *Metal Slug:* the `'desert'` backdrop, `ground` and `metal` tiles, crates, barrels, sandbags, palms and cactus, `broken` pillars, `particles.explosion` everywhere (with `particles.ground` set so debris bounces on platforms), `bulky` soldiers with `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` while firing, and screen shake on every blast.\n- *Legend of Mana / Secret of Mana:* the `threequarter` view, `E.tex.grass` / `dirt` / `water` floors by floor tag (a river with `block: true` and a plank bridge), houses of `timber` or `plaster` walls under `tiles` roofs, `hedge` walls with `leaves` tops and `rock` cliffs with `grass` tops, trees, bushes, flowers, fences and mushrooms as props, `chibi` characters with `face: { eyes: 'big' }` and bright palettes, rabbites (`Blob` with `ears: 'rabbit', feet: true`), and dialog boxes with portraits.\n- *Klonoa and 2.5D platformers:* PlatformMap in the `brawler` view (tiles show their top faces), a bright `'day'` or `'forest'` backdrop, props in front of and behind the play plane (`y` of -12 and +12), and a soft camera `lead`.\n- *Final Fight / Streets of Rage:* the `brawler` view, a long street TileMap with brick and `plaster` building walls, awnings, streetlamps, hydrants, trash cans and burning fire drums along the sidewalk, `bulky` heroes and thugs with varied palettes, and big impacts.\n\n## Animation: fluid, varied, readable\nBroken animation ruins a good-looking game faster than flat art. The Humanoid rig animates procedurally, so use its vocabulary instead of moving joints by hand:\n- **Moves, not raw specs.** `new E.Attack('overhead')`, `new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'])`. Every move in `E.MOVES` winds up, strikes with a lunge, step, lean and twist, follows through, holds, and eases back to the stance. It never sweeps backwards through its own arc.\n- **A move set per character.** A knight gets `overhead`, `thrust` and `spin`; a mage `cast`; a rogue `jab`, `thrust` and `backslash`; a monk `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse` and `flyingkick`; a brute `haymaker`, `bash` and `sweep`; a beast `claw`. Two heroes must never share one swing.\n- **Stances and poses between attacks.** Brawlers walk with `stance: 'guard'`, knights with `stance: 'ready'`. Win with `pose: 'cheer'`, cast with `'cast'`, block with `'block'` (weapons) or `'guard'` (fists), duck with `'crouch'`, taunt with `'hips'` or `'wave'`, fall with `down`, die with `'die'` (stagger, knees, topple), and climb ladders with `climb: true`. Faces act too: `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'`, and a Dialog portrait's mouth moves while its line types.\n- **Enemies telegraph.** Give enemy attacks a longer `wind` (.2-.35 s) than the hero's, so players can read and dodge them. Flash and knock back on hit, lie `down` on death.\n- **Check the motion.** `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game.html#scene --steps \"... rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records the swing frame by frame. Look for the wind-up, the strike and a clean settle. Add `--seed 1` for a repeatable strip, and `--compare before.html` (a copy of the page from before your change) to see exactly which pixels your change moved.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n1. **Game logic in world units, `dt` everywhere.** Never store screen pixels. Convert only when drawing (`r.w(x, y, z)`) or reading the mouse (`game.mouseGround()`). `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, so the same inputs play the same at 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz.\n2. **Draw only with engine primitives** (`E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props, `r.*` helpers). `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText`, canvas gradients and scaled `drawImage` anti-alias and break the pixel look. Translucency goes through the engine helpers (`px.blend`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc`, `r.shadow`): stepped alpha with blend modes by default (PS1 / N64 / SNES color math), or ordered dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for NES, Game Boy and Genesis looks.\n3. **Anything that overlaps goes through the queue**: `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue`. Only backgrounds are drawn directly (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`, parallax shapes).\n4. **Animate with continuous values** (phases, velocities, eased timers), never frame lists.\n5. **Read input in `update`**: `pressed` for one-shot actions, `down` for held, `buffered` + `consume` for combat.\n6. **A game is one script on the engine:** in a single file, the code between `GAME START` and `GAME END`; with files, `game.js` beside the `index.html` that loads the engine (inline both into the page to hand a person one file). **The engine is yours to change too.** Your copy (a clone of the repository, the handoff package, a single file) is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit. The readable engine is `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `engine/modules/` in the repository (`node tools/build.mjs` rebuilds every page from them), `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package (every page and test there loads it), and the script marked \"inlined from engine/my-3d2dge.js\" in `dist/my-3d2dge.html`; a genre kit's is minified. If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine lacks.\n7. **Don't rebuild the standard features** (below): the pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen and pausing on focus loss come with every game. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`.\n\n## Standard features (built in: adjust them, don't rebuild them)\nEvery `new E.Game(...)` comes with what players expect of a modern release, so your code goes to the game itself. They're tested on both editions, they work with keys, mouse, gamepad and touch, and the player's choices are saved per game.\n- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (when your `E.Shell` has one) and Quit to title (it asks first; Keep playing has the focus). Arrows, d-pad and stick move, Enter or A chooses, Escape or B goes back one screen, P or Start closes the menu. The game sees none of those presses.\n- **Settings** (`game.settings`): Display (full screen; pixel scaling for a fixed `res`: Auto, Sharp, Fill; a screen filter: Clean, Scanlines or CRT, drawn as the picture reaches the canvas, never on the game's buffer, and off under a test harness, so screenshots and filmstrips stay clean), Sound (sound, volume, music, effects, the audio offset with its calibration), Comfort (reduce motion, screen shake, screen flashes), and yours. Saved per game with a version (`E.store` key `settings:` + the page's path, or `id`); a bad saved value falls back to its default, and where storage is blocked the game plays on (`game.settings.saved` is false).\n- **Controls**: every action of the current preset, rebound by pressing the new key, mouse button or gamepad button (a conflict is said and nothing changes; Escape, Enter, the F keys, Start, the arrow keys and the d-pad stay with the menus, so a player can't lock themselves out), the controller (dead zone, invert aim, button symbols: Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo or positions, vibration) and the touch buttons (always, automatic or hidden; right or left side; size; opacity). Restore defaults resets the controls and never touches a save.\n- **Full screen** (`game.fullscreen`): a phone's first tap goes full screen, the top-right button and the menu toggle it, and once the player leaves it the game never forces it back. Where a browser can't (an iPhone), the settings say to use Share, then Add to Home Screen; the page carries the home-screen app tags.\n- **Pausing by itself**: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden, the window loses focus or the controller in use is unplugged (`game.pauseReason` says which), and only the player resumes.\n- **The corner**: a menu button and a full-screen button at the top right, inside the safe area. They show on titles, game overs and pauses, for a moment when the mouse nears the top, and the menu stays on a touch screen with no touch buttons; in play they're gone. Keep a title's art clear of the top-right 110 x 60 CSS pixels, or pass `corner: false`.\n- **Phones**: a viewport that reaches the safe areas (pinch zoom left alone), no page scrolling, zooming, selection or callouts on the canvas, touch buttons inside the safe areas, a touch stick with a dead zone that also presses the directions past halfway (menus, dialog choices, ladders), a bottom dialog that keeps clear of the touch buttons, menus picked by a tap or click on an entry and dialogs turned by a tap on the box (a title needs no touch buttons), and a fixed `res` filling a high-density screen (`expand: true` fills every screen).\n- **Boot** (a loading screen and a crash report): heavy setup (a procedural level, a big table, baked caches, data from a promise) goes in boot steps, run one a frame behind a boot screen that shows the system's checks (engine, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage, input), each step, a progress bar and lines of the game's own, like the Sims' \"Reticulating splines\"; then the game draws a warm-up frame under it, so play starts without a hitch. `game.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot: { title: 'VESPER', version: '1.0', steps: [['Raising the harbour', () => { map = buildHarbour(); }], ['Waking the guards', () => loadGuards()]], messages: ['Counting the bells', 'Oiling the hinges'], min: 0 } })`. A step may return a promise; headless (and with `loop: false`) the steps run at once and `await game.booted` waits for any promise. If a step throws, or anything fails before play, the screen becomes a report a player can screenshot: what failed, the error, a short code, the engine and game versions, browser, screen, canvas, GPU, sound, storage and input, with Try again, Copy report and Continue. `E.diagnose()` gives the same report any time (`.text`, `.short`); after play, errors go to the red box with its short line. `E.bootSplash(title)` is the screen's markup for the very start of `<body>`, so it shows while the page's scripts load (the starter files and the handoff template have it). Keep a boot short: no step should wait on purpose (`min` is for a splash you want seen).\n- **Juice that respects the player**: `game.shake` and `game.flash` follow the comfort settings, `game.hitFx` rumbles a gamepad (the vibration setting; `rumble: false` skips it), and `game.input.rumble(strength, seconds)` does it for your own moments.\n\nAdjust them:\n```js\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER',\n  menu: {\n    items: [['Restart level', () => game.go('play', { level })]],                 // pause-menu buttons after Resume\n    settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true },                      // yours, beside the standard ones\n      { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal', help: 'How hard enemies hit' }],\n    controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: 'Magic', debug: false },                  // names on the Controls screen; false hides one\n    quit: 'title'                                                                  // the scene Quit goes to (false: no Quit)\n  } });\nconst hurt = { Easy: .5, Normal: 1, Hard: 1.5 }[game.settings.difficulty];       // read them anywhere, any time\nr.text('PRESS ' + game.input.label('jump') + ' TO JUMP', 8, 8, '#fff');           // 'SPACE', 'A' or '✕', after the player's rebinding\n```\n- Leave parts out: `menu: false` (you draw your own pause: the canvas PAUSED overlay comes back), `fullscreen: 'button'` (no tap) or `false`, `corner: false`, `autoPause: false`, `id: 'mygame'` (the name the settings are saved under).\n- A canvas title menu can offer OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')` opens the settings over the title, which waits; `game.menu('controls')` the controls.\n- From code: `game.settings.set('volume', .5)` (checked, saved, applied), `game.settings.reset('Sound')`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (browsers allow it only from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`.\n- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n- The agent edition has `game.settings`, `game.fullscreen`, the corner, the pausing and `input.label`, but not the menu screens: its corner's menu button pauses and resumes.\n\nWhat stays your game's job, because only the game knows it:\n- Every gameplay scene `pausable: true`, so the menu and the pausing reach it; titles and game overs not.\n- Prompts from `game.input.label(action)` (or `ui.hud({ prompt: { act: 'interact' } })`), never hard-coded keys; touch buttons (`touch: [...]`) for every action a level needs (the stick moves).\n- The assists your mechanics call for, as settings: difficulty, aim assist, auto-fire, hold or toggle for a sustained action, hints after repeated failure.\n- Saves: `game.saves` (three slots and an autosave with their place, level and time played; versioned; `menu: { saves: true }` for the pause menu's Save and Load and the standard screen, which asks before a save is lost), written at checkpoints and save points (not on page close).\n- Information never in color alone; flashes short (`game.flash` already follows the settings).\n\n## Frame order\n\n`game.start(...)` runs this every animation frame:\n\n1. The clock adds the frame's real time (at most 50 ms) to what is left from the last frame, and runs one step for each whole 1/120 s of it: 2 steps a frame at 60 Hz, 1 at 120 Hz, 1 or 0 at 144 Hz. Each step ticks input, then (except during hit-stop and pause) runs the timers, the current scene's `update(dt)`, particles and the camera. The rest waits for the next frame: `game.alpha` (0..1) is how much of a step it holds, for interpolating drawing.\n   - Slow motion (`game.timeScale = .3`) makes each step shorter (`dt = 1/120 * .3`), never fewer, so it stays smooth. Above 1 a step is split so none is longer than 1/120 s.\n   - Paused, or at `timeScale` 0, the steps still run with `dt` 0, so input keeps ticking.\n2. The renderer clears the buffer and positions the camera.\n3. The scene's `draw(r)`. Draw backgrounds first, then register decals, queue items, actors, sprites, overlays and lights.\n4. The renderer finishes the frame, in this order:\n   1. particles and afterimages are queued;\n   2. decals draw (ground marks, shadows, telegraphs);\n   3. the queue sorts and draws by depth;\n   4. lighting darkens the frame;\n   5. overlays draw on top (HUD, dialog, menus, damage numbers);\n   6. the frame is presented with a sub-pixel camera offset.\n5. With GPU lighting active, every queue item is also drawn into a hidden info image, and WebGPU does the lighting and presentation.\n\nAn error in `update` or `draw` does not stop the loop. It is shown in a red box on screen and logged once to the console (`game.errors` keeps the list).\n\n## Systems in more depth\n\n### Views and resolution\n- Presets: `iso` (yaw 45, pitch 30), `threequarter` (pitch 55), `topdown` (pitch 80), `brawler` (pitch 25), `side` (pitch 0).\n- `overhead` (pitch 90, scale 1) makes world x/y equal screen pixels, for shooters, puzzle and arcade games. It is not in the default view cycle.\n- Make your own with `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost)`.\n- `res` fixes the internal resolution with whole-number scaling. Without it, the size adapts to the window between `minH` and `maxW` × `maxH`; `portrait: { maxH }` lets the view grow on a screen held upright (a phone), so it fills the screen.\n- `res` with `expand: true` (what the starter kits use) fills any window with no bars: `res` is the least the screen shows, and `r.W` × `r.H` grows on the long side (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427×240 of `'ps1'`, an ultrawide 640×270, a phone held upright 320×690). The game's job is what fills the extra space, edge to edge, with the HUD over the world's corners (never a band of nothing). The kits show the ways. A wider screen shows more level (the adventure's neighbouring rooms, dimmed a little: they wait) or widens the play area itself (the shooter's waves spread across the whole screen). A taller one shows more world (the forest round the adventure's rooms, the RPG's sky darkening to stars), zooms a side view in (the platformer, the brawler and the RPG's battle at 1.5x, a scene's `camera.tall`, the battle's two sides closing up to fit) and lifts the level onto a band below it (the platformer's soil, the brawler's foreground wall). A painted backdrop is painted at the screen's size and zoom, its ground meeting the floor's edge. Keep prompts clear of `game.input.touchArea()` and HUDs clear of `game.cornerArea()` (the menu button stays at the top right on a phone). On a phone, make the controls smart: a button there only when it has something to do (`input.touchSet`: TALK beside someone), taps on what you see (the RPG's commands and foes), a thumb that steers from anywhere on the screen (`input.steer = 'drag'`, `input.drag()`: the shooter's ship goes 1.5x as far, so one swipe crosses it), what a phone player shouldn't have to hold done for them (the shooter fires by itself), buttons a thumb hits (44 CSS px and more: the RPG's battle pads, with AUTO), and a camera that never shows ground a fighter can't walk to (the brawler stops at a fight's lock). And keep the frame a phone's: a 120 Hz screen gives 8.3 ms, and frames that miss it (their cost, or the GPU behind them) are drawn every other refresh (`game.drawEvery`), so draw background characters at half rate (`r.actor(..., { every: 2, key })`: the adventure's townsfolk) and leave GPU lighting to a computer (its main pointer fine). Settings, Display, Show frame rate reads it on the device, and Frame rate lets the player fix it (60: every other refresh of a 120 Hz screen; Full: every refresh). `npm run test:phone` checks the kits on an emulated phone.\n- `game.views` limits view cycling (`game.nextView()`) and tells the checker which views to test.\n\n### Scenes\nA scene is `{ enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera }`.\n- `game.go('play', data)` switches before the next step; timers, tweens, scripts and particles are cleared (weather too) and the camera snaps. `{ fade: 'iris', at: hero }` (or `'wipe'`, `'mosaic'`, `'swirl'`, `'dissolve'`, `'white'`; `seconds` each way) picks the transition: an iris closing on the hero for a door, a swirl into a battle, a mosaic into a dream. A world point `at` is placed by each scene's camera; `[x, y]` is a point on the screen. Reduce motion turns each into a plain fade.\n- `pausable: true` lets `pause` (Escape, P, Start, the touch START) toggle `game.paused`: the pause menu opens (the canvas PAUSED overlay with `menu: false`) and the music is ducked. It also lets the page pause the scene by itself when it is hidden or loses focus.\n\n### Camera\n- `game.follow(hero, { z, lead })` once, or `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update.\n- `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` (or `level.bounds`) keeps it inside the level.\n- `cam.room = [w, h]` snaps the target to the center of the current room; the camera then slides, so Zelda- and Metroid-style room transitions come for free. Freeze gameplay while `cam.moving` is true.\n- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggle the screen. `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (its `onGround`) or nears the screen's edge, so a jump doesn't drag the camera up and down. `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin: 24, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps several things in view, centred between them, zooming out (to at least `zoom[0]`) as they part and back in as they meet: two players, a boss and the hero, a race's leaders. `zoom: false` only centres.\n- Screen shake respects the player's reduced-motion setting.\n\n### Players (two to four on one machine)\n- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `pressed()`, `label()`), plus `player` (2 to 4), `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`: blue, red, green, yellow) and `device`.\n- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player 1 WASD with F G H R T Y (`Input.halfKeys(preset, 'left')`), player 2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` and the numpad (`'right'`), each half taking the preset's actions in turn. `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })` adds one directly (tests, a versus menu); `game.removePlayer(p)`.\n- The standard features follow: each player's prompts name its own keys (`input.label`), a pause says who paused (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller names its player, the Controls screen has a page each. Recorded sessions replay every pad and both halves of the keyboard.\n- One shared screen: `cam.frame(heroes, { margin: 90 })` keeps everyone in view. A split screen: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [hero, hero2] })`, `layout: 'auto'` (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one, a grid for 3 or 4), `'columns'`, `'rows'` or `'grid'`. The scene's `draw(r)` runs once per viewport, its camera its own (`game.viewports[i].follow(t)`, `.focus(x, y)`), with `r.viewport` saying which (`{ i, x, y, w, h }`: draw that player's HUD there); `scene.hud(r)` then runs once over the whole screen. GPU lighting is off while split; a scene's start ends the split.\n\n### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\nThe code every game writes by hand, in the engine and on game time: each pauses with the game, stands still in hit-stop, slows with `timeScale` and ends with the scene (unless `keep: true`), and runs the same at every refresh rate.\n- **Tweens** (Phaser's tweens, Unity's DOTween): `game.tween(target, { x: 10, y: 4 }, seconds, { ease, delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases a target's numbers to new values, from where they are when its delay ends; a door sliding, a title dropping in, a coin flying to the HUD, a camera move. `ease` is a name in `E.ease` (Penner's: `linear`, `inSine`, `outBack`, `outElastic`, `outBounce`, ...; `outQuad` by default) or a function of 0..1. It replaces a timer, a start value and a lerp in every update.\n- **Scripts** (Unity's coroutines): `game.script(function* (game) { ... })` runs a generator in the update step and waits at each `yield`: a number of seconds, a condition (`E.until(fn)` or a function), a tween, another script or a generator (run and waited for), `E.all([...])`, `E.any([...])`, or nothing (one step). A wave timeline is a loop (`for (const w of WAVES) { spawn(w); yield E.any([w.time, () => !enemies.length]); }`); a cutscene is its lines in order (`talk.say(...)`, `yield E.until(() => !talk.open)`, `yield game.tween(cam, ...)`), instead of a state machine with a timer per state. It runs at once to its first `yield`; `.cancel()` stops it and runs its `finally`.\n- **Paths:** `new E.Path(points, { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })` measures a line by distance along it: `at(d)` is a point with its heading (a moving platform's place, an enemy's dive, a rail, a camera track: `d = speed * time`), `nearest(x, y)` the distance along of the nearest point (a racer's progress round the lap, for places), `draw(r)` shows it while you build.\n- **Weather:** `game.weather = { kind, amount, wind, lightning }` (rain, snow, ash, leaves, sand): drops fall through the world's height in ground views and in three layers of parallax in side and overhead views, splash or settle on the floor (`particles.ground`, the platforms' height in a side view), and lightning flashes (the player's Screen flashes setting) and thunders. It is cosmetic: its own seeded random stream, never `Math.random`, so replays and gameplay are untouched. `E.WEATHER` holds the kinds; add your own.\n- **Sprites** (frames, turns, swaps): `E.anim([...frames], fps, { loop, pingpong })` (or one sheet cut into `frames`) is played by `r.sprite(x, y, z, anim, { t })`; `rot` turns a sprite in whole pixels, cached in 16 or 32 steps as the era's games pre-rotated theirs (a ship facing its heading, a spinning blade); `scale` grows it nearest-neighbour; `E.recolor(spr, map)` is a palette swap (the second player, a stronger enemy).\n- **Wrap-around:** `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` keeps a position on a world whose edges meet (Asteroids, Pac-Man's tunnel, a looping stage), `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` the short way across the seam (aim and chase across it), and `wrap: [w, h]` on `r.sprite` and `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge a thing straddles.\n\n### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n- ASCII `rows` and a `legend`: a number is a wall type, a string is a spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn }` combines them.\n- Wall `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH }`. `cut` lowers a wall when it stands between the camera and the floor.\n- `floorTex(x, y, floorTag)` colors the floor per pixel; it is baked once per view. Ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`.\n- `map.set(cx, cy, 0)` opens doors or breaks walls; the floor and wall caches rebuild themselves.\n- Collision is height-aware: `collide(body)` ignores walls lower than `body.z + step`, and `groundAt(x, y, r, z)` returns the surface to stand on.\n- Things standing on a block sort after it, and `r.shadow(..., surfaceZ)` draws on top of blocks.\n- `map.block(cx, cy, on)` and `map.set(...)` bump `map.version`; a `FlowField` rebuilds at its next `update` when it changes.\n- Maps built in code: `new E.TileMap({ w, h, types })` starts as open floor; `map.fill(cx, cy, w, h, id)` puts a block of wall type `id` in (0 clears it), and `map.setFloor(cx, cy, tag, block)` tags a floor cell (`'grass'`, `'water'`) and blocks it or not.\n- Facades come from wall-type options: `windows: { w, h, step, from, lit }` (or `true`), `door: { chance, color }`, `shutters: color`, `balcony: { z, color }`, `arch: { chance }`. For anything else, `types[id].decorate(face)` paints each wall face as it is baked (so it costs nothing per frame): `face.side`, `face.u0..u1` along the wall in world units (patterns line up across cells), `face.z0..z1`, `face.at(u, z)`, `face.quad(u0, z0, u1, z1, color)`, `face.line`, `face.dot`, `face.tones`, `face.view`.\n- A big thing over several blocks (a canopy over a tower, a cart across two cells) sorts by its whole base: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { footprint: [x0, y0, x1, y1] })` lifts it over every block under it. `r.prop` and `r.sprite` take `footprint` too.\n\n### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\nOne rule for collision, standing, sight, reach, navigation and drawing, so a game with roofs never writes its own:\n- World units, one tile = 16, z up. A body's `z` is its feet. A cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)`: 0 on the floor, and a wall is solid from 0 up to its `h` (one column per cell: no passages under things yet).\n- A body stands on the highest top within `z + step` under its footprint (`step` 2, `Body.step`), so a kerb of 2 is walked over and a block of 14 is jumped onto.\n- Eyes are at `z + E.HEIGHTS.eye` (30) and a target is seen at `z + E.HEIGHTS.chest` (22), the heights of a size-1.3 Humanoid. A body's own `eye` and `chest` override them: a crouching hero sets `hero.chest = 8`.\n- \"The same level\" is `|dz| <= E.HEIGHTS.level` (8) unless a call says otherwise. `E.dist` is 3D; `E.inArc` is flat unless you pass `dz`, and `E.overlap` is always flat (pair it with `E.sameLevel`).\n\nThe queries on a `TileMap` (`dir` is an angle in radians or a ground vector `[dx, dy]`; `from` is a body or any `{ x, y, z, r }`):\n- `map.ray(a, b, { eye, target })` walks every cell between two points and returns `{ clear, t, x, y, z }`, where it stops. `map.sight(a, b)` is eyes to chest; `los()` only looks across the ground (a 12-unit planter blocks it, not `sight`).\n- `map.standable(x, y, z, r)`: room to stand, held up, not in deep water.\n- `map.ledge(from, dir, { reach: 18, min: 4, rise: 82, spread: .28 })` → `{ x, y, z, rise, facing, edge: [x, y] }`: the nearest top to pull up onto, and where the hands grip.\n- `map.landing(from, dir, { min: 35, max: 83, rise: 11, drop: 18, apex: 20 })` → `{ x, y, z, d }`: a spot across a gap that a jump arc clears.\n- `map.drop(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, fall }`: what lies past the edge ahead (hop-downs, fall warnings, guards that won't walk off roofs).\n- `map.arcClear(a, b, apex, r)`: a parabola from feet to feet, peaking `apex` above the higher end, clears every top (jumps, throws, aerial takedowns).\n\nReach and perception:\n- `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, facing, dz, sight: map, filter })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]`, nearest first. `dz` defaults to the same level; `arc` is a half angle around `facing`. Melee auto-aim, lock-on, takedowns and interaction prompts use it.\n- `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)` and `E.sameLevel(a, b, tol)`.\n\nNavigation: `new E.FlowField(map, { step: 2, climb: 0, drop: 0, jump: 0 })` walks the tops a body can stand on. Next-door cells connect when their tops differ by at most `step`, or by up to `climb` going up and `drop` going down. A guard on a roof gets a field across the roofs; with `climb` and `drop` set, it chases between levels. With `jump` (the widest gap, world units) it also links roofs across a gap a jump clears, and a climb costs more than a leap, so a chase leaps rather than drop to the street and climb back. `flow.next(x, y)` → `{ x, y, z, kind: 'walk' | 'climb' | 'drop' | 'jump', edge }` says where to head: an AI on the ground presses jump for a `climb` or a `jump` once `edge` (the distance to the side of its cell it leaves by) is under its radius plus a little, and `E.Mover` does the climb or the leap.\n\n```js\nconst seen = map.sight(guard, hero) && E.inArc(guard, guard.facing, hero, 120, .7);   // eyes over planters, not through roofs\nif (input.pressed('jump')) {\n  const up = map.ledge(hero, hero.facing);   // a wall to climb in front?\n  const across = !up && map.landing(hero, hero.facing);   // or a gap to clear?\n  if (up) climbTo(up); else if (across) leapTo(across); else hero.jump(210);\n}\nconst prey = E.targets(hero, guards, { range: 22, arc: 1.2, facing: hero.facing, sight: map })[0];   // the same level by default\nif (prey && input.pressed('attack')) takedown(prey.target);\n```\n\n### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n- Tiles live on the x/z plane; the ASCII top row is the highest.\n- Kinds:\n  - `solid`;\n  - `slope`: `dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left, `from` / `to` (0..1) for gentle slopes; bodies walk up and down them and step up onto low ledges;\n  - `oneway`: you stand on it and jump up through it, and down + jump drops through;\n  - `ladder`: climbable, and you can stand on its top;\n  - `hazard`: not solid; test it with `touching(body, 'hazard')`;\n  - `deco`: drawn in the play plane;\n  - `back`: a darker background wall.\n- Styles give each tile its look: `stone` (with `moss`), `brick`, `wood`, `metal`, `ground` (grass top when open to the sky, with `flower`), `block` (bevelled), `bonus` (`?` block, `glyph` option), `pipe`, `plank`, `spikes`, `ladder`, `liquid`, `plain`; `mat` gives a solid tile's face any material (`E.MATERIALS`).\n- In `side` the level is flat like an NES or SNES game. In a tilted view with yaw 0 (`brawler` or custom), tiles show top faces and the level becomes a 2.5D diorama.\n- `move(body, dt, solids)` moves any box `{ x, z, vx, vz, w, h }` with sub-steps. It reports:\n  - `onGround` and `onOneWay`;\n  - `hitWall`: -1 left, 1 right, 0 none;\n  - `hitCeiling`;\n  - `bumped`: the tile hit from below;\n  - `ground`: the moving solid it stands on.\n- `solids` are extra boxes (moving platforms, elevators, crates); a body standing on one is carried by its `vx` / `vz`.\n\n### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n- Defaults feel like a modern NES remaster: run 95, jump 285 (about 3 tiles), gravity 800 rising and 1150 falling, fall cap 330.\n- Forgiving controls: coyote time 0.09 s, jump buffer 0.13 s, jump cut 0.45 when jump is released early.\n- Optional moves: `airJumps` (double jump), `wallJump` (with wall slide), `dash`, `climb` speed.\n- `update(dt, level, input | intent, solids)`. The same controller runs enemies: pass `{ x, jump, jumpPressed }` from your AI.\n- `rigState(extra)` returns the object `Humanoid.update` wants, including the airborne pose.\n\n### Body (ground-plane physics)\n- Velocity with optional `friction` (per second) and `bounce` (off walls and the floor).\n- `push(ix, iy, iz)` adds knockback; `jump(v)` jumps with `gravity`.\n- It lands on the highest walkable surface: the floor, or the top of a block low enough to step onto.\n- Use it for heroes, enemies, bombs, crates, pickups and balls.\n\n### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n`E.Mover` is the ground views' answer to `Platformer`: a `Body` with a third-person game's movement.\n- `const mover = new E.Mover({ x, y, z })` (the index files its members as `mover.`); each step `mover.update(dt, map, game.input)` with `input: 'THIRDPERSON'` (move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch, J attack, K block, L dodge; touch buttons), or an AI's intent `{ move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }`. Screen directions turn into ground ones through the view.\n- Walk 50, run 85, sprint 125 and sneak 32 with acceleration; a jump with coyote time and a buffer, once a press.\n- A jump press is parkour first: a ledge up to `climbMax` (82) is climbed (`climbUp`; crouch while hanging lets go), a wall up to `vault` (14) is vaulted at a run, a gap ahead is leapt (it aims for the far side's landing), a drop up to `hopMax` (48) is hopped down; otherwise it jumps.\n- Climbing any wall (`climbAny: true`, Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild): a jump press at a wall too tall for a ledge latches on, on the ground or in the air (`mover.wall` is the face, `map.wall(from, dir)`'s: its point, normal, top and facing). The stick climbs as the screen shows it (up the screen is up the wall; an AI's intent: `move: [along, -up]`), it turns outer and inner corners, a jump press leaps up (`climbJump`, which ends on the wall) or, pulled away, pushes off; near the top it pulls up (`climbUp`), at the floor it stands, crouch lets go. Lower `climbMax` (30) so roofs are climbed, not jumped onto. The STEALTH slice and the template do this.\n- Gliding (`glide: true`, Breath of the Wild, Arkham): a jump press in the air, `glideMin` (24) over the ground, opens a sail: it sinks at `glideSink`, flies at `glideSpeed`, takes no fall damage, rises over an updraft (`lift`: a speed, or `m => inUpdraft(m) ? 40 : 0`), and closes on a press, on landing, or at a wall (a climber takes hold). `mover.gliding` says so; the rig hangs under it with its hands up, so draw the sail over them (the template does).\n- Stamina (off until you set `staminaMax`; the template's is 100): climbing, climb jumps, gliding and sprinting spend `mover.stamina` (`tire`), standing refills it (`staminaRegen`); at 0 it lets go of the wall, the glide closes and it can't sprint (`mover.exhausted`) until a third is back. Show it with `ui.hud({ bars: [{ label: 'Stamina', value: mover.stamina, max: mover.staminaMax }] })`.\n- Falls: past `rollAt` (26) at speed it rolls, past `hurtAt` (72) it sets `hurt`. `lastSafe` and `respawn()` bring it back from water and pits (`inHazard`).\n- `mover.rigState(extra)` feeds `rig.update`: its crouch (a sneaking gait), sprint lean, air, landing, or the playing action's pose.\n- Actions (`E.ACTIONS`): `climbUp` (played backwards to climb down), `vault`, `hopDown`, `roll` (invulnerable in its middle), `getUp`, and `takedown`, a paired action that moves two bodies: `mover.play('takedown', { other: guard, aerial, map })`. While `mover.action` plays it moves the body instead of physics; `act.beat`, `act.u`, `act.canCancel`, `act.invulnerable`, `act.otherState()` for the victim's rig. Your own: `E.ACTIONS.slide = { beats: [['go', .4]], setup(A) {}, at(A, beat, u) { return { x, y, z, state } } }`.\n\n### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n- `E.sees(guard, hero, { map, range: 120, fov: 1.6, eye })` → `{ seen, d, angle, exposure }`: inside the cone, and three points on the hero (feet, chest, head) checked against every top with `map.ray`. Crouching shows less and lower; `hero.exposure` (0..1) is cover you set: tall grass, shadow.\n- `E.hears(guard, { x, y, z, loud: 80 }, map)`: 1 at the noise, 0 at its radius, halved through a wall.\n- `const mind = new E.Suspicion({ rise, decay, notice, search, alert, forget })`; each step `mind.update(dt, E.sees(...), hero)` → `'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'` (it rises faster the nearer and more exposed the hero is), `mind.last` (where it last saw or heard), `mind.changed`. An alert guard that loses the hero searches for `forget` seconds; `mind.hear(strength, at)` notices a noise.\n- Drawing: `r.sightCone(guard, { map, range, fov, color, alpha })` (cut short by walls) and `r.marker(x, y, z, { label, color })`, an objective marker that becomes an arrow at the screen's edge.\n\n### Attack (melee timing)\n- `new E.Attack(spec)` owns the wind-up, active and recover timers of one move; the spec is also the Humanoid attack spec.\n- `start()` begins a swing (pass `true` to chain from recover into a combo); `update(dt)` returns the phase that just began, which is where swing sounds go.\n- `hits(targets, test, fn)` calls `fn` once per target per swing while active. `state` feeds `rig.update({ attack })`.\n- `E.Combo([spec1, spec2, spec3], { window })` chains attacks: `press()` flows into the next hit during recover (or shortly after), otherwise restarts. `E.knockback(from, target, speed, up)` pushes the target away.\n- **Special moves** (input sequences): `game.input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing })` is true on the step a fireball's motion ends: down, down-forward, forward, then the button (numpad notation, as fighting games write moves: `'623'` a dragon punch, `'41236'` a half circle, `'66'` a dash), mirrored when the fighter faces left; the button may land with the last direction, and the whole move must come within `window` seconds (.45). `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })` reads any run of inputs (a cheat code, a rhythm pattern); a step joins actions with `+`. Both read `input.history`, the last second of directions (numpad digits) and presses, which an input display can draw.\n\n### Bullets and patterns\n- A pool on the `'ground'` plane (top-down) or the `'side'` plane.\n- Bullets die on walls with a small spark; `hit(targets, fn, team)` tests circles (side targets use their box center).\n- `burst(base, E.pattern.spread(...))` fires a pattern: `dir`, `aim`, `aimSide`, `spread`, `ring`.\n- Bullets glow under GPU lighting. Set `sprite` for custom art and `pierce` to pass through targets.\n\n### Humanoid\n- Proportions: `legUpper legLower hipZ torso shoulderHalf headR armUpper armLower limbW torsoW`.\n- Style: `lean hunch stride swing speedRef`.\n- Gear: `weapon` (`'sword'`, `'gun'`, `'staff'`), `hat` (`'cap'`, `'pointed'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`, `'crown'`), `hood`, `cape`, `eyeGlow`, `colors`.\n- `update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, attack })`.\n- Attacks:\n  - You own the timing: move `phase` from `'wind'` to `'active'` to `'recover'` with your own timers, and pass `u` (0 to 1) within the phase.\n  - `spec.a0` / `a1` are hand angles relative to facing; `z0` / `z1` are heights.\n  - `spin: true` swings a full circle, `kick: true` swings the right foot, `blade: 0` hides the smear.\n- `hand()` and `tip()` give world points for spawning bullets or sparks; `worldOffset(p)` gives where any point in the rig's own frame (a joint in `J`) sits from its feet. `debug(r)` draws the skeleton.\n\n### Audio\n- Browsers start sound only after the first key press or click; the engine waits for it.\n- `sfx(name)` ignores the same sound fired twice in 35 ms, and varies the pitch slightly so repeats don't sound robotic.\n- `music(song)` loops tracks of its own step notation. Every track loops on its own length, so short drum or bass loops can run under long melodies.\n- Built-in songs: `title`, `adventure`, `dungeon`, `boss`, `victory` (plays once). The songs module's songbook has more by mood (`E.Songbook.find('town')`) and jingles.\n- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time (`audio.pos`), not the sound's, so beats land on the same update step headless and on any device, and the sound is held to that clock (it moves its notes to meet it). Time gameplay to `audio.pos`, `audio.at(step, fn)` and `audio.onBeat(fn)`, never to the sound. A song with `sync: true` pauses with the game.\n- Dynamic music: one song in layers rather than cuts. Name the tracks, start the extra ones with `on: false`, and bring them in with `audio.layer('drums', 1, .5)` (the stealth slice's chase); `audio.tempo(bpm)` for a hurry-up; `audio.sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` for a jingle on the next bar; `music(song, { fade: 1 })` to crossfade.\n\n### Text and UI\n- `E.font` is a 5x7 proportional pixel font with lower case (the old 3x5 font is `font: 'tiny'`), with `scale`, `align`, `wrap`, outline, drop `shadow` and `gradient`; `E.font.title` draws extruded gradient logos.","numLines":306,"startLine":1,"totalLines":665,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UkPamh3m8iKfAvzn49jbDt","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj8yFP4aDtqvqDmvqYig","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SYF3j48YnJgSKCGZYhAaeY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/_research/hub/lib/hub/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":753,"cache_read_input_tokens":28653,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":753},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"aa69ae8f-7f5f-4a5a-808d-5c1215fe4737","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:27.548Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj8yF9vyP9LXjXnY8CYn","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01SYF3j48YnJgSKCGZYhAaeY":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/_research/hub/lib/hub/README.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SYF3j48YnJgSKCGZYhAaeY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n2\t\n3\tAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\n4\tplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\n5\tround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\n6\tscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\n7\tround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n8\t\n9\t**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n10\t\n11\tThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\n12\tdescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n13\t\n14\t- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n15\t- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n16\t\n17\tTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n18\t\n19\t> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n20\t> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n21\t> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n22\t\n23\t---\n24\t\n25\t## Modes\n26\t\n27\tA game declares one mode.\n28\t\n29\t| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n30\t|---|---|---|\n31\t| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n32\t| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n33\t| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n34\t\n35\t- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n36\t  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n37\t- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n38\t  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n39\t  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n40\t- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n41\t  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n42\t  world the host sends them.\n43\t\n44\tGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n45\t`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n46\t\n47\t---\n48\t\n49\t## A round\n50\t\n51\t1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n52\t   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n53\t   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n54\t   (*The card*).\n55\t2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n56\t3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n57\t4. The game reports how it goes.\n58\t5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n59\t   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n60\t   over the game, then removes it.\n61\t\n62\t### Tags\n63\t\n64\tIn the game's `<head>`:\n65\t\n66\t```html\n67\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n68\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n69\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n70\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n71\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n72\t```\n73\t\n74\t- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n75\t  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n76\t  is in that range.\n77\t- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n78\t- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n79\t  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n80\t\n81\tOptional:\n82\t\n83\t- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n84\t  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n85\t- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n86\t  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n87\t\n88\t### The client\n89\t\n90\t[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\n91\tis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\n92\tafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n93\t\n94\t- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n95\t  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n96\t- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n97\t  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n98\t  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n99\t- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n100\t  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n101\t  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n102\t\n103\tThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\n104\tedited copy is not replaced.\n105\t\n106\t### Joining\n107\t\n108\t- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n109\t- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n110\t  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n111\t- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n112\t  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n113\t  the game runs as it does on its own.\n114\t- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n115\t  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n116\t- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n117\t  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n118\t  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n119\t\n120\t`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n121\t\n122\t| Field | |\n123\t|---|---|\n124\t| `me` | this copy's player id |\n125\t| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n126\t| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n127\t| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n128\t| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n129\t| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n130\t\n131\tNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\n132\tfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n133\t\n134\t### The card\n135\t\n136\tBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\n137\tnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\n138\tthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n139\t\n140\t```js\n141\tAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n142\t```\n143\t\n144\tThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\n145\tscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\n146\ttitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\n147\tit deserves.\n148\t\n149\tWhile it is on, the card shows:\n150\t\n151\t- the game's name;\n152\t- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n153\t- how to win the round;\n154\t- the controls this player will use;\n155\t- the time left, from the `card` event.\n156\t\n157\tAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\n158\ton the card:\n159\t\n160\t- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n161\t  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n162\t- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n163\t  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n164\t\n165\tNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\n166\tchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\n167\tnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n168\t`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n169\t\n170\t`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n171\t\n172\t| Field | |\n173\t|---|---|\n174\t| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n175\t| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n176\t| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n177\t| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n178\t| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n179\t| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n180\t\n181\t### The start\n182\t\n183\t`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n184\t`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\n185\tgive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n186\t`start`.\n187\t\n188\t### Reporting\n189\t\n190\t- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n191\t  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n192\t  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n193\t- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n194\t  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n195\t  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n196\t- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n197\t  by the host only:\n198\t  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n199\t    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n200\t    placed last.\n201\t  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n202\t  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n203\t\n204\t### Time-up and the end\n205\t\n206\t- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n207\t  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n208\t  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n209\t  `finish()` by then is void.\n210\t- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n211\t  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n212\t  game, then removes it.\n213\t\n214\t| `end` reason | |\n215\t|---|---|\n216\t| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n217\t| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n218\t| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n219\t| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n220\t| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n221\t\n222\t### Leaving\n223\t\n224\t- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n225\t  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n226\t- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n227\t- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n228\t  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n229\t- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n230\t  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n231\t  that player leaving.\n232\t\n233\t### Scoring\n234\t\n235\t- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n236\t  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n237\t  progress.\n238\t- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n239\t  result instead of medals.\n240\t- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n241\t\n242\t`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\n243\tresults:\n244\t\n245\t- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n246\t  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n247\t- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n248\t  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n249\t\n250\tPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n251\t\n252\t---\n253\t\n254\t## Sending data between copies\n255\t\n256\t### Parallel\n257\t\n258\tThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n259\t\n260\t### Ghost\n261\t\n262\tEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\n263\tthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\n264\tonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\n265\tcharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n266\t\n267\t### Shared world\n268\t\n269\t- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n270\t  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n271\t- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n272\t  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n273\t  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n274\t- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n275\t  every copy.\n276\t\n277\t### `send()` and `message`\n278\t\n279\t- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n280\t  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n281\t- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n282\t  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n283\t  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n284\t- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n285\t- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n286\t  client drops the rest.\n287\t- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n288\t  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n289\t  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n290\t  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n291\t\n292\t---\n293\t\n294\t## Rules\n295\t\n296\tIn a session:\n297\t\n298\t- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n299\t  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n300\t- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n301\t  names. Names come from the hub.\n302\t- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n303\t  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n304\t  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n305\t- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n306\t  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n307\t  is held.\n308\t\n309\tEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\n310\tone, the game is not affected.\n311\t\n312\t---\n313\t\n314\t## Test it\n315\t\n316\tThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\n317\tand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\n318\tto the game:\n319\t\n320\t```bash\n321\tcurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n322\tcurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\n323\tcurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\n324\tpython3 -m http.server 8000\n325\t```\n326\t\n327\tThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\n328\tThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n329\t`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n330\t`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n331\t`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n332\t\n333\tThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n334\t`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n335\t`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n336\t\n337\t- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n338\t- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n339\t- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n340\t  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n341\t- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n342\t  player of its own;\n343\t- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n344\t  told the round ended;\n345\t- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n346\t\n347\t**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\n348\tcases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n349\t`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\n350\tgame is served from the harness's own origin.\n351\t\n352\tIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\n353\tchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\n354\truns there.\n355\t\n356\t---\n357\t\n358\t## Reference\n359\t\n360\t### Tags\n361\t\n362\t| Tag | |\n363\t|---|---|\n364\t| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n365\t| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n366\t| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n367\t| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n368\t| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n369\t| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n370\t| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n371\t\n372\t### API\n373\t\n374\t| | |\n375\t|---|---|\n376\t| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n377\t| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n378\t| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n379\t| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n380\t| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n381\t| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n382\t| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n383\t| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n384\t| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n385\t| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n386\t| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n387\t| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n388\t| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n389\t\n390\t`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\n391\tread them.\n392\t\n393\t| Event | Arguments |\n394\t|---|---|\n395\t| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n396\t| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n397\t| `message` | `data, from` |\n398\t| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n399\t| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n400\t| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n401\t\n402\tEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\n403\torder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\n404\treach the game through the client unchanged.\n405\t\n406\t### Versions\n407\t\n408\tThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\n409\tgames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\n410\tprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\n411\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"fc8fdcf5-fda9-4a73-8206-158f05dfc8d2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:27.554Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/_research/hub/lib/hub/README.md","content":"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n\n---\n\n## Modes\n\nA game declares one mode.\n\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n  world the host sends them.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n   (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\n\n### Versions\n\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\n","numLines":411,"startLine":1,"totalLines":411}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SYF3j48YnJgSKCGZYhAaeY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj8ykajyBHYXD7SzzWZ3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KtfGkDVB8tghaLdMNAAfxW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/API.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":33866,"cache_read_input_tokens":29406,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":33866},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"2339931d-4ad0-4770-8fb1-44e65e15be9d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:29.966Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj8ykK83NB45vD6u5dMZ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KtfGkDVB8tghaLdMNAAfxW":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/API.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KtfGkDVB8tghaLdMNAAfxW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# my-3D2dge API card (v0.88.0)\n2\t\n3\tmy-3D2dge (\"My 3D 2D Game Engine\") is a general-purpose retro-modern 2D game engine built for AI models. Use it to port, remaster, reimagine and remix games from the 8-bit to 64-bit eras (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1), or to make spiritual successors to them. Everything is drawn by code: no image or sound files. Characters are 3D skeletons drawn as pixel art, and the camera can be any classic 2D view.\n4\t\n5\t**Start from a kit:** each genre has one, a complete small game written to be copied (the Genre playbook below names it). In a single file it sits between `GAME START` and `GAME END`: the starter's shell (the game, its title menu, the proof), the slice and the start; a genre kit holds one slice, `dist/my-3d2dge.html` every one and the ANIMATION LAB (how each animation is driven). In the handoff package each kit's game is `kits/<id>.js`, readable. **To make a game, change the slice into yours, with its own title, play and game-over scenes, and delete the starter's menu and the slices you do not use. The engine is yours to change too:** fix, extend or rewrite any of it as you see fit.\n6\t\n7\t**Looking a name up:** grep `API-INDEX.md` (every public name, one line; members are filed under instance names: `rig.`, `talk.`, `mover.`, `flow.`, `map.`), not the engine's source. It, the guide (`AI_GUIDE.md`) and the tools are in the handoff package (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/handoff/) and the repository.\n8\t\n9\t## Quick start (a complete game)\n10\t```js\n11\t(() => {\n12\tconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp } = E;            // always start like this\n13\tconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'snes', view: 'threequarter' });\n14\tconst map = new E.TileMap({\n15\t  rows: ['##########', '#@...s...#', '#..##....#', '#........#', '##########'],\n16\t  legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero', 's': 'slime' },  // number = wall type, string = spawn tag\n17\t  types: { 1: { h: 30, top: '#57506a', side: '#3d3750' } },\n18\t  floorTex: (x, y) => E.tex.flagstone(x, y, { stones: ['#4b4559', '#554f64'].map(E.hex), mortar: E.hex('#221e2b'), hi: E.hex('#6f6782'), lo: E.hex('#322d3e'), speck: E.hex('#2a2634') })\n19\t});\n20\tgame.cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v);\n21\tconst s = map.find('hero'), hero = new E.Body({ x: s.x, y: s.y, r: 5 });\n22\tconst rig = new E.Humanoid({ hat: 'pointed' });\n23\tgame.start({\n24\t  update(dt) {\n25\t    const m = game.input.move(), d = game.view.screenDirToGround(m[0], m[1]);\n26\t    hero.vx = d[0] * 80; hero.vy = d[1] * 80; hero.update(dt, map);\n27\t    if (Math.hypot(d[0], d[1]) > .1) hero.facing = Math.atan2(d[1], d[0]);\n28\t    rig.update(dt, { x: hero.x, y: hero.y, z: hero.z, vx: hero.vx, vy: hero.vy, facing: hero.facing || 0 });\n29\t    game.focus(hero.x, hero.y, 10);                // the camera looks here: call every update\n30\t  },\n31\t  draw(r) {\n32\t    map.drawFloor(r); r.shadow(hero.x, hero.y, 5); map.queueWalls(r);\n33\t    r.actor(hero.x, hero.y, hero.z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view));\n34\t    r.text('HELLO', 4, 4, '#ffffff');              // HUD text in screen pixels\n35\t  }\n36\t});\n37\t})();\n38\t```\n39\t\n40\t## Look target (read this before drawing anything)\n41\tAim above SNES, level with the best PS1 / N64 2D games (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa). Games that skip these steps look like Atari games. **Every game should use all of them:**\n42\t1. **Resolution** `res: 'ps1'` (320x240) or `'snes'`, with `expand: true` so it fills any screen (anchor the HUD to `r.W` / `r.H`), and characters 40-60 px tall: `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3 })` (HD style is the default: shaded volumetric limbs, outfits, hair).\n43\t2. **Dress the character**: an `outfit`, `hair`, a `hat`, `armor`, a `cape`, `sleeves` (Characters), and a real palette in `colors` (never leave the defaults on everyone).\n44\t3. **Backdrop behind every side-view level**: `const bg = new E.Backdrop('castle-night')` then `bg.draw(r)` first in `draw` (a ground view's floor runs on to the screen's edges: `map.outside`). Presets: `day dusk night castle-night city-night desert forest ocean cave space`, or your own `{ sky, sun | moon, stars, starsY: .7, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains' | 'hills' | 'forest' | 'city' | 'castle' | 'clouds' | 'sea' | 'fog' | 'stalactites', color, y, height, parallax, fill }] }` (`starsY` = how far down the stars reach; a layer but clouds, fog and stalactites fills the screen below its skyline unless `fill: false`).\n45\t4. **Textured tiles**: PlatformMap styles `stone` (with `moss`), `brick`, `wood`, `metal`, `ground` (with `flower`), `block`, `pipe`, `bonus`; TileMap floors from `E.tex.*`, TileMap walls with `face: 'brick' | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and tops with `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'` (a village = timber and plaster houses with tile roofs, hedges, rock cliffs).\n46\t5. **Props in every room** (3-8 per screen): `r.prop(name, x, y, z, { size, color, light: true })` with `torch candle candelabra chandelier brazier window banner pillar statue crate barrel chest pot sign fence lamp door tree pine bush flowers grass rock crystal bones skull vines cobweb lantern streetlamp awning hydrant trashcan firedrum sandbags cactus palm mushroom`. Flames animate, glow and can light the scene. `chest` / `door` take `{ open: true }`, `pillar` takes `{ broken: true }`, `window` is a gothic rose window with lead cames (`colors: [...]` for the glass, `size: 2-3` for a cathedral); hang `vines`, `cobweb`, `lantern` with `{ anchor: 'top' }`. With heroes at size 1.2+, give the scene `propSize: 1.4` (or set `E.style.propSize`) so props match them.\n47\t6. **Effects on every hit**: `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)`, `smoke`, `fire`, `glints`, `sparks`, `game.flash(color, s)`, `game.freeze`, `game.shake`.\n48\t7. **Color**: `E.tones(hex)` gives `{ deep, sh, base, lt, hi }` hue-shifted shades and `E.ramp(hex, n)` a palette ramp. Shade everything you draw yourself with them (shadow side `sh`, lit edge `lt`, a `hi` sparkle).\n49\t8. **Typography**: the default font is a 5x7 proportional font with lower case. Titles: `E.font.title(g, 'NAME', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: [...], align: 'center' })`. HUD text: `{ shadow: '#000', outline: false }` or `gradient: [top, bottom]`. `font: 'tiny'` is the old 3x5 font.\n50\t9. **Light every scene**: `game.lights.enabled = true; game.lights.ambient = .5` in a scene's `enter`, then each draw a few lights where light comes from (`r.prop('lamp', x, y, 0, { light: true })`, `game.lights.add(x, y, z, 60, .9, { color })`): dusk, torchlight and glows give depth. It looks the same in every browser and check; `game.enableGPU({ map })` adds shadows in ground views, and falls back to it.\n51\t10. **Faces in dialog**: `talk.say(lines, { name: 'MIRA', portrait: miraRig })` puts a live close-up of that Humanoid (blinking, hair, hat) beside every line. `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` draws one anywhere (HUD, RPG party windows).\n52\t\n53\t## Coordinates: what x, y, z mean in each view\n54\tWorld units: one tile = 16. **z is always up.** Game logic never stores screen pixels.\n55\t\n56\t| View | Looks like | Ground plane | Use for levels | Move with |\n57\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n58\t| `'iso'` (yaw 45, pitch 30) | Diablo, Landstalker | x/y ground, z up | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n59\t| `'threequarter'` (pitch 55) | Zelda LttP, Stardew | x right, y down the screen | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n60\t| `'topdown'` (pitch 80) | GTA 1, Hotline Miami | x right, y down | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n61\t| `'brawler'` (pitch 25) | Final Fight, 2.5D | x right, y into the screen | `E.TileMap` or `E.PlatformMap` | `E.Body` or `E.Platformer` |\n62\t| `'side'` (pitch 0) | Mario, Mega Man | **x right, z up, y = 0 (depth)** | `E.PlatformMap` | `E.Platformer` |\n63\t| `'overhead'` (pitch 90, scale 1) | 1942, Galaga, Pac-Man | x = screen x px, y = screen y px | none, or a TileMap | your own x/y, or `E.Body` |\n64\t\n65\t- A unit is `r.ppu` pixels: at zoom 1 iso 1.41, overhead 1, the others 1.5 (a tile about 24). A sprite's pixel is a screen pixel (`{ world: true }`: it grows with the view, as rigs and maps do).\n66\t- In `'side'` the screen shows x (right) and z (up). **y is depth and is normally 0.** Focus the camera with `game.focus(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 24)`.\n67\t- In ground views, the screen direction the player pushes becomes a world direction through `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`.\n68\t- `r.actor(x, y, z, (g, ox, oy) => ...)` calls your function with `(ox, oy)` = the buffer pixel where world point (x, y, z) lands. Draw the character around that point.\n69\t\n70\t## Rules\n71\t1. Keep game logic in world units, and use `dt` everywhere. `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, the same at any refresh rate (`game.alpha` = the part of a step left over).\n72\t2. Draw only with `E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props and the `r.*` helpers. Never use `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText` or canvas gradients on the game buffer (they blur pixels). For translucency and glow use `px.blend(g, alpha, 'add' | 'multiply' | 'screen' | 'normal', () => {...})`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc` and `r.shadow`: they use stepped alpha and blend modes like PS1 / SNES hardware (or dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for an NES / Genesis look).\n73\t3. Anything that can overlap anything else goes through `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue` (depth sorted). Backgrounds (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`) come first in `draw`.\n74\t4. Read input only in `update`. Use `pressed(a)` for one-shot actions (menus, jumps, shots), `down(a)` for held actions, and `buffered(a)` + `consume(a)` for forgiving combat input.\n75\t5. Animate characters from continuous values (velocity, timers, easing): the rigs, not frame lists.\n76\t6. Colors are `'#rrggbb'` (or `'#rgb'`) strings.\n77\t7. Put the whole game in one IIFE `(() => { ... })();` between the markers, and start it with `game.start(...)`.\n78\t\n79\t## Common mistakes (check these first)\n80\t- Nothing on screen: the camera looks at the wrong place. Use `game.follow(hero)` once (in the scene's `enter`), or call `game.focus(...)` every update. In side view the hero has y = 0.\n81\t- Walls or level missing: you forgot `map.drawFloor(r)` + `map.queueWalls(r)` (TileMap) or `level.draw(r)` (PlatformMap).\n82\t- Using a TileMap for a side-scroller. Side-scrollers use `E.PlatformMap` + `E.Platformer`.\n83\t- HUD drawn in world coordinates. Use `r.text(...)` or `r.overlay(g => ...)` with screen pixels (`r.W`, `r.H`).\n84\t- An old key layout: platformers want `input: 'PLATFORMER'` (Space jumps), shooters `input: 'SHMUP'` (Space fires).\n85\t- Errors show in a red box on screen and in the console. Fix the first one first.\n86\t- Colours are `'#rrggbb'`, `rgb()`, `hsl()` or a basic name ('orange'); any other draws magenta.\n87\t- `mouseGround()` is `[x, y]`; `settings.reset(group)` resets a group ('Sound'; none: all), not a key; `particles.explosion()` adds a hit-stop and a shake (`{ freeze: false, shake: 0 }`); `E.font.text` outlines unless `outline: false`.\n88\t- A frame past 50 ms gives up its time (no catch-up): a test in a slow browser waits on the game's state, not the clock.\n89\t\n90\t## Game\n91\t`new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', view, views, res, expand, input, bg })`\n92\t- `res`: `'nes'` 256x240, `'snes'` 256x224, `'genesis'` 320x224, `'gb'` 160x144, `'gba'` 240x160, `'ps1'` / `'n64'` 320x240, `'wide'` 400x225, or `[w, h]`. Fixed pixels: whole-number scaling on a desktop screen, the largest fit on a phone (Settings, Pixel scaling). Leave it out for an adaptive size.\n93\t- `expand: true`: full screen at any shape, no bars. `res` becomes the least the screen shows and `r.W` x `r.H` grows to fill the window (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427x240 of `'ps1'`, a phone held upright about 320x690). Anchor the HUD to `r.W` and `r.H`, and give the level something to show in the extra space (more of it, a backdrop, a HUD deck under it on a tall screen). `expand: { maxW, maxH }` caps the growth. A scene may set it too.\n94\t- Adaptive size: `minW` / `minH` set the scale (the screen shows at least that many pixels), `maxW` / `maxH` cap the view (the rest is letterboxed). `portrait: { maxH: 1000 }` overrides them while the screen is taller than wide, so a phone held upright (9:16) is filled instead of showing a band across its middle. `game.screen.portrait` says which way it is.\n95\t- `input`: `'DEFAULT'` (top-down action), `'PLATFORMER'`, `'SHMUP'`, `'THIRDPERSON'`, or your own `{ action: ['KeyQ', 'Pad0'] }`.\n96\t- `views`: the views your game supports; `game.nextView()` cycles them (bind it to a key).\n97\t- Start: `game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) })`, or with scenes (below).\n98\t- Methods: `follow(obj, { z: 16, lead: 20 })` (camera tracks it automatically), `focus(x, y, z)` (or aim it yourself every update), `setView(id)`, `nextView()`, `freeze(s)` (hit-stop: `update` waits; `game.hitstop` is the time left), `shake(n)`, `after(s, fn)`, `every(s, fn)` (timers in game time, return `{ cancel() }`), `hook('input' | 'step' | 'enter', fn)` (your own each step, paused too for input; returns its removal), `mouseGround()`, `go(scene, data)`, `enableGPU({ map })`.\n99\t- Fields: `W`, `H` (screen pixels), `time`, `real`, `timeScale` (0.25 = slow motion), `paused`, `input`, `audio`, `particles`, `lights`, `view`, `errors`, `cam`. `game.fail('my power', err)` reports an error in your code (logged, in `errors`, on the error panel) and plays on.\n100\t- Camera zoom and turn: `game.setZoom(1.5)` (.5 .. 3; everything drawn by the engine re-rasterizes crisply, the HUD stays), `game.rotateView(45)` (ground views only), `game.resetCamera()` (to the scene's zoom), `game.zoom`, `game.yaw`, `game.note('ZOOM 2x')` (a short notice at the top of the screen; `game.shownNote` is the one showing). Custom painters can read `r.view.zoom` (undefined at 1x) to scale their own art.\n101\t- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` lets the player zoom within `zoom` from `start` (wheel, `-` `=`, pinch, Settings; `false`: none; `AI_GUIDE.md`, Camera).\n102\t- Tests and tools: `new E.Game({ headless: true })` (the default in Node: no screen, sound or DOM; `draw` is skipped, or with `draw: true` run on a stand-in, its errors in `game.errors`) or `{ loop: false }` runs only by `game.advance(seconds, { draw })` (exact fixed steps at once); `input.tap(action)` presses for one step, `input.hold(action, on)`; `game.debug = { snapshot() {...} }` is your game's handle (`tools/check.mjs` prints it); `game.stop()`, `start()`, `dispose()`.\n103\t- Sessions: `?record=1`, then the pause menu's Save session; `node tools/replay.mjs session.json` replays it exactly, headless (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Sessions).\n104\t- Camera: `cam.offset = [dx, dy]` sits the focus that many screen pixels from the centre (above a phone's touch buttons: `[0, -60]`); `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` keeps it inside the level (in side views a level shorter than the screen sits on the bottom edge; `cam.align = 'center'` to change); `cam.room = [160, 128]` moves screen by screen (Zelda, Metroid) and `cam.moving` is true while it slides; `cam.smooth` = lag in seconds (0: locked to the target); `cam.window = [48, 24]` a dead zone the focus moves in before the camera follows; `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (side-scrollers); `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps them all in view, zooming out as they part (`frame(null)` stops); `cam.free = { keys, edge: 10, drag, speed: 300, bounds: [x0, y0, x1, y1] }` a free camera (strategy, sims): keys, a stick, the screen's edge or a drag pan it, a zoom goes toward the pointer or the pinch.\n105\t\n106\t## Scenes (title, play, game over)\n107\t```js\n108\tgame.start({ scene: 'title', scenes: {\n109\t  title: { update(dt) { if (game.input.pressed('start')) game.go('play', { level: 1 }); },\n110\t           draw(r) { r.sky(['#101848', '#402a70']); r.text('PRESS START', r.W / 2, 100, '#fff', { align: 'center', scale: 2 }); } },\n111\t  play: { pausable: true, view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER', res: 'snes', views: ['side', 'brawler'], touch: ['jump', 'attack'],\n112\t          enter(data) { /* build the level */ }, exit() {}, update(dt) {}, draw(r) {} }\n113\t} });\n114\t```\n115\t`game.go(name, data)` switches after a short dithered fade (`game.fadeTime`: a test advances past it; `{ fade: 0 }` cuts; `{ fade: 'wipe' | 'iris' | 'mosaic' | 'swirl' | 'dissolve' | 'white', seconds: .4, at: hero }` a transition, a plain fade under reduce motion); timers (but `after(s, fn, { keep: true })`) and particles are cleared, a hit-stop ends (`timeScale` stays). `pausable: true`: Escape / P / Start pauses it, with the pause menu (below). `view`, `views`, `input`, `res`, `expand`, `touch`, `camera`, `propSize` are options applied on entry (a method so named warns). A scene is one object every visit: reset its state in `enter`. `back()` takes a phone's back gesture first (true if it went back: a sub-menu closed).\n116\t\n117\t## Motion and timing: tweens, scripts, paths, weather\n118\t- Tweens (Phaser's): `game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases numbers on game time: paused with the game, slowed by `timeScale`, ended by a scene change unless `keep`. Returns `{ done, cancel() }`. `E.ease` has Penner's set: `linear`, `inSine` `outSine` `inOutSine`, `inQuad` `outQuad` `inOutQuad`, the same for `Cubic`, `Back`, `Elastic`, `Bounce`.\n119\t- Scripts (Unity's coroutines) for cutscenes, boss patterns, wave timelines, tutorials:\n120\t```js full-script\n121\tgame.script(function* () {\n122\t  yield 1;                                   // seconds of game time\n123\t  yield E.until(() => door.open);            // a condition\n124\t  yield game.tween(boss, { y: 80 }, 1.5);    // a tween (or another script, or a generator) finishing\n125\t  for (let w = 0; w < 3; w++) { spawnWave(w); yield E.any([8, () => enemies.length === 0]); }\n126\t});\n127\t```\n128\t  `E.all([...])` waits for all; a bare `yield` waits one step. It runs at once to its first `yield`, the same at any refresh rate; `{ keep: true }` outlives the scene; `.cancel()`, `.done`, `.value`.\n129\t- Paths: `const p = new E.Path([[x, y, z], ...], { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })`: `p.length`, `p.at(d)` (`{ x, y, z, angle }` by distance along: moving platforms, dives, rails), `p.nearest(x, y)` (a racer's progress), `p.draw(r)` to see it.\n130\t- Weather: `game.weather = { kind: 'rain' | 'snow' | 'ash' | 'leaves' | 'sand', amount: .8, wind: .3, lightning: .2 }` (null: clear) falls through the world in every view, settles or splashes, flashes and thunders; cosmetic only (its own random stream), and it ends with the scene: set it in `enter`.\n131\t- Wrap-around (Asteroids): `x = E.wrap(x, 0, W)`, `E.wrapDiff(a, b, W)` (the short way across the seam), and `wrap: [W, H]` on `r.sprite` or `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge.\n132\t\n133\t## Standard features (every game has them: don't build your own)\n134\tWith no code: a **pause menu** on pausable scenes (Resume, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Quit to title; keys, mouse, gamepad and touch), **settings** saved per game (`game.settings`: volume, music, effects, mute, reduce motion, shake and flashes, pixel scaling, a screen filter, the frame rate and its readout), **controls** the player rebinds (conflicts said first; dead zone, invert aim and move, button symbols, vibration; touch button side, size, opacity), **full screen** (a phone's first tap, a top-right button, the menu; turning it off is remembered), a **menu button** at the top right (on a touch screen it stays in play: it is the touch START), a **way out** of every menu screen, the **back gesture** stepping back in the game (`game.back()`) before it leaves the page, **pausing** when the page is hidden or the controller unplugs, and a page set up for phones (safe areas, no scrolling, zooming or pull to refresh on the canvas).\n135\t- Adjust: `new E.Game({ menu: { items: [['Restart', fn]], settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true }, { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal' }], controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: false }, quit: 'title' } })`, then read `game.settings.hints`. Leave parts out: `menu: false` (your own pause), `fullscreen: 'button' | false`, `corner: false`, `backGesture: false`, `autoPause: false`, `touch: 'never'` (a phone is told a controller or keyboard is needed). `game.cornerArea()`: where the corner's buttons cover the screen (or null): keep a HUD clear of it.\n136\t- Save slots (`game.saves`): three slots and an autosave, each with its place, level and time played. `game.saves.write = () => ({ data, place, level })` and `read = save => {...}`; then `save(slot)` (0, 1, 2 or `'auto'`), `load(slot)`, `copy(a, b)`, `erase(slot)`, `autosave()`, `list()`, `latest()` (a title's Continue). `menu: { saves: true }` (or `{ slots, version, migrate(data, from) }`) puts Save (while `saves.canSave`: a save point) and Load in the pause menu, and `saves.show('save' | 'load')` opens the screen from your own menu. Blocked storage keeps them for the visit.\n137\t- Prompts: `game.input.label('jump')` is that action's key or button on the player's device, after rebinding ('Space', 'A', '✕'). PlayStation symbols by default: `settings: [{ key: 'glyphs', value: 'playstation' }]` (a standard key without a label sets its default). A canvas title's OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')`. From code: `game.settings.set(key, v)`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`. `live: true` (or `game.menu(name, { pause: false })`): menus over a world that keeps stepping (online play).\n138\t- **Boot**: heavy setup in steps behind a boot screen, and a report for any error before play: `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { title, steps: [['Building the city', () => { map = build(); }]] } })` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Boot).\n139\t\n140\t## Input: `game.input`\n141\t- Actions in every preset: `up down left right` (WASD, arrows, d-pad), `start` (Enter, Start), `pause` (Escape, P, Start), `confirm` (Enter, Space, J, Z, pad A), `cancel` (Escape, Backspace, X, K, pad B).\n142\t- DEFAULT adds `attack` (J, X, click), `dash` (Space, Shift, K), `skill` (L, E, right click), `jump` (Z). PLATFORMER: `jump` (Space, Z, K, pad A), `attack` (X, J, click), `dash` (Shift, C, L), `skill`. SHMUP: `fire` (Space, Z, J, click), `bomb` (X, K). THIRDPERSON: `sprint` (Shift), `jump` (Space), `crouch` (C, Ctrl), `interact` (E, F), `attack` (J), `block` (K), `dodge` (L), `use` (Q), `map` (M, Tab).\n143\t- `down(a)`, `pressed(a)`, `released(a)`, `repeat(a, delay, rate)` (menus), `buffered(a, win)` + `consume(a)`, `consumeAll()`, `anyPressed()`, `move()` (screen direction `[x, y]`, length up to 1), `use(preset)`, `touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'])`, `touchSet('confirm', 'TALK', near)` (a context button: relabel it, or hide it while it has nothing to do).\n144\t- Moves: `input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing })` is true on the step a quarter circle forward and punch ends (numpad notation, mirrored when `facing` is -1: `'623'`, `'41236'`); `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down+right', 'attack'], { window: .5 })` any run of inputs (a cheat code); `input.history` the last second of them (`{ t, dir, pressed }`, for an input display).\n145\t- Players (two to four on one machine): `game.players` is `[game.input, ...]`, each an input with this API, its `player` number, `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`) and device. `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => {} })` lets them in: Start on a free gamepad, or 2 on the keyboard (player 2 takes its right half: arrows, `.` `/` `,` and the numpad; player 1 keeps WASD with F G H; a preset's `halves: { left: { pass: ['KeyR'] }, right: {...} }` its own); `game.addPlayer({ pad: 0 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })`, `game.removePlayer(p)`. Prompts, the pause (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller and the Controls screen are per player. One screen: `cam.frame(heroes)`. Split: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [h1, h2], layout: 'auto' })` (`'columns'`, `'rows'`, `'grid'`; up to 4) draws the scene once per viewport (`r.viewport` is `{ i, x, y, w, h }`), then `scene.hud(r)` once over the whole screen; `game.viewports[i].follow(t)` or `.focus(x, y)`.\n146\t- `clear()` releases held inputs, pending presses, virtual sticks and touch buttons; focus loss does this automatically. `suppress(a)` blocks an action for the current tick; `suppressCode('Mouse0')` lets a HUD widget consume that mouse button while other devices bound to the action still work.\n147\t- Controller settings: `deadzone` (default `.18`, radial and rescaled), `invertAimY`, `lastDevice` (the index has the rest). The left stick and the touch stick, pushed past halfway, press `up` / `down` / `left` / `right` as the d-pad does (menus, dialog choices, ladders); bind `PadAxisUp/Down/Left/Right` to your own menu actions for the same; the right stick is `PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right`, the wheel `WheelUp`/`WheelDown`; `value(a)`: 0 to 1 (a trigger's travel, a stick's push). Options while Create is held is no press (the AI Hub's chord).\n148\t- Touch settings: `touchEnabled`, `stickSide` (`'left'` or `'right'`), `stickRadius` (CSS pixels), `touchFilter(event)` (return false to reserve a canvas tap for UI). `touchArea()`: where the touch buttons cover the screen (`{ x0, y0, x1, y1 }`, or null): keep a prompt clear of it. `clicked()`: where a click or tap on the canvas landed (`[x, y]` in screen pixels) the step after it, else null; `pointer()`: the mouse in screen pixels; `drag()`: how far the stick's finger moved this step (`[dx, dy]`, or null), to steer one to one; `steer = 'drag'`: a finger anywhere steers, no stick drawn (`stickOn`: a finger is on it). `zones = [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }]` (fractions of the canvas): a finger in one holds its act until it lifts (a pinball's halves). Gestures (a mouse or one finger, screen pixels): `box()` → `{ x0, y0, x1, y1, done }` (a left drag; on touch a long press, then a drag), `longPress()`, `doubleTap()`, `pan()` → `[dx, dy]` (a finger's drag on open ground, a middle or right drag), `zoomPoint()`; `feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch, button, id })` plays a pointer in a test. `bindButtons(root)` wires `[data-act]` elements.\n149\t\n150\t## Drawing: `r` in `draw(r)`\n151\t- Background: `r.sky(['#top', '#mid', '#bottom'])`, `r.starfield({ count, speed, dir: 'down' | 'left' })`.\n152\t- Depth sorted: `r.actor(x, y, z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view), { flash: '#ffffff', flashMix: .75, alpha, xray, outline, ghost, emissive, every, key })` (the flash tints over the sprite so shading shows: `flashMix` .5 by default, 1 solid; `every: 2, key: npc` redraws a background character every other frame; actors up to about 250 px tall), `r.sprite(x, y, z, sprite, { flip, anchor: 'bottom' | 'center', glow, flash })`, `r.queue(x, y, z, g => {...}, { bias })` (sorted by that one point: a long wall by `r.queueSpan(a, b, z, h, fn)`), `r.actorNow(...)` (as `r.actor`, drawn at once over what is drawn so far), `r.box(g, x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, top, side)` (inside a queue function). `{ pick: unit }` on any of them (and `r.shape`), then `r.pick(sx, sy)` → what the last frame drew under a screen point, front to back.\n153\t- Floor marks: `r.shadow(x, y, radius, alpha, color, surfaceZ)`, `r.decal(g => r.groundArc(...))`, `r.groundDisc`, `r.groundRing`, `r.groundPts(x, y, rad, n, z)`.\n154\t- On top, unlit, screen pixels: `r.text(str, x, y, color, { align, scale, outline })`, `r.textAt(x, y, z, str, color)`, `r.overlay(g => {...})`.\n155\t- `r.w(x, y, z)` = buffer pixel of a world point. `r.W`, `r.H`, `r.ix`, `r.iy` (pass to dither functions), `r.view`, `r.visible(x, y, z)`.\n156\t- X-ray: `r.actor(..., { xray: true })` shows the actor's hidden part, a translucent tint with a 1-px outline (a checker in `E.style.trans = 'dither'`), wherever a wall, a prop or shape at least chest high (22; `hides: true | false` says otherwise) or anything queued with `{ hides: true }` is drawn in front of it; not by one rising less than 8 above its feet (the roof it stands on, a cut-down wall). `xray: { color, outline (false: none), actors: true }`: with `actors` other bodies in front hide it too (a hero stays findable in a crowd). Afterimages: `ghost: { color, life, alpha, owner }`; `owner` (the actor's `{x, y, z}`) keeps a trail left nearer the camera sorted behind the live body.\n157\t- Glowing things you draw yourself: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { emissive: true })` is drawn again after lighting so darkness never dims it (flames, lasers, magic, neon). It still hides behind whatever stands in front of it: walls and `r.actor` bodies do this on their own, and any other queued draw can join them with `{ solid: true }`. (Canvas lighting builds a depth-ordered mask for this; `game.r.glowMask = false` skips it on slow machines.)\n158\t\n159\t## Pixels, sprites, text, UI\n160\t- `px.rect(g, x, y, w, h, c)`, `dot`, `line(g, x0, y0, x1, y1, c, width)`, `disc(g, x, y, r, c)`, `ell`, `poly(g, pts, c)`, `polyDither(g, pts, c, 0..1, r.ix, r.iy)`, `ddisc`, `sprite(g, spr, x, y, flip)` (or `{ flip, rot, scale }`), `glow(g, 0..1)` (GPU glow for what follows).\n161\t- Sprites from strings (for coins, bullets, ships, icons, NES-style enemies): `const COIN = E.sprite(['.yy.', 'yYYy', '.yy.'], { y: '#f0b020', Y: '#fff0a0' }, scale)`. `.` and space are transparent. Frames: `const walk = E.anim([f1, f2, f3], 8, { pingpong })` (or one sheet: `E.anim(sheet, 8, { frames: 4 })`), drawn with `r.sprite(x, y, z, walk, { t })`. `r.sprite(..., { rot: angle, scale })` turns and scales in whole pixels (cached in 16 steps a turn; `steps: 32`); `E.recolor(spr, { '#3a8a4a': '#8a3a4a' })` swaps a palette.\n162\t- Font (5x7 proportional with lower case; `font: 'tiny'` for 3x5): `E.font.text(g, str, x, y, color, { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font })`, `E.font.title(g, str, x, y, { scale, colors, outline, depth, depthColor, align })`, `E.font.width(str, opts)`, `E.font.lineHeight(opts)`, `E.font.wrap(str, maxW, opts)`. Glyphs: ASCII plus `♥ ★ ← → ↑ ↓ • × ♪ ▸ ©`.\n163\t- UI (inside `r.overlay(g => ...)`): `E.ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, { bg, border, gradient, alpha })` (a gradient window like Final Fantasy; `bg` can be `[top, bottom]`, `gradient: false` for flat, `alpha: .6` for a see-through HUD panel), `E.ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, 0..1, color)`, `E.ui.hearts(g, x, y, hp, max)` (half hearts allowed), `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, size, { zoom: 3, turn: .6, bg, frame, cape: false, weapon: false, tint: '#808080' })` (character close-up; it stays upright while the rig kneels or lies down; `tint` greys out a KO'd hero).\n164\t- Dialog: `const talk = new E.Dialog(game)`; `talk.say(['Line one.', 'Page two.'], { name: 'ELDER', portrait: elderRig, onDone, choices: ['YES', 'NO'], onChoice(i) })` (choices show on the last page: the keys, or a tap or click on one, pick); in update `if (talk.update(dt)) return;` (true while a modal box is open: play waits); in draw `talk.draw(r)`. `portrait` takes a Humanoid (live close-up; `portraitSize: 40`, `portraitOpts: { zoom, turn }`) or a function `(g, x, y, size) => {...}`. Radio chatter during play: `talk.say('Enemy fighters ahead!', { name, portrait, auto: 2.5, modal: false })` turns its pages and closes by itself while the game keeps running. Per line: `place: 'top' | 'middle' | 'bottom'`, `y` (pixels) and `alpha` (see-through box). `new E.Dialog(game, { speed, lines, color, nameColor, voice, width, bg, border, vars })` (`voice`: a sound, or `(ch, i, talk) => {}` per letter); `talk.close()`, `talk.open`. Markup in the text: `{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}` a color, `{w=.5}` a pause, `{s=12}slowly{/s}` letters a second, `{shake}..{/shake}`, `{wave}..{/wave}`, `{$name}` a value from `say(text, { vars })` or `talk.vars`.\n165\t- Menu: `const menu = new E.Menu(game, ['START', { label: 'LOAD', disabled: true }], { x: 'center', y: 100, title, onPick(i, item), onCancel })`; `menu.update(dt)`; `menu.draw(r)`. Keys, d-pad and sticks move it; a click or tap on an entry picks it (so a phone can leave a title), and the mouse over one moves the cursor. A tap on a dialog box turns its page as confirm does.\n166\t- HTML screens and HUD: `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme, settings, screens: { pause, help } })`; `ui.show('title' | 'results' | 'map' | ..., spec)`; each step `ui.hud({ objective, status, bars, prompt, minimap })` (`minimap: { map, fog, camera: true, markers, onClick(x, y) }`); `ui.toast(title, text)`.\n167\t\n168\t## Sound: `game.audio` (chip synth, no files, starts after the first key press)\n169\t- `sfx(name, { vol, pitch })`. Presets: `jump jump2 land step coin pickup key powerup oneup heal hit hurt stomp bump swing whoosh punch kick shoot laser charge explode boom door secret warp die select confirm cancel pause text blip`.\n170\t- Custom: `sfx({ wave: 'square' | 'pulse' | 'pulse12' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise', freq: 440 or 'A4', to: 880, dur: .2, vol: .3, arp: [0, 4, 7], step: .05, vib: [8, .03] })`, or `audio.define('zap', voice)`.\n171\t- Music: `music('title' | 'adventure' | 'dungeon' | 'boss' | 'victory')` (built in; any other name warns and plays nothing), or your own `{ bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [{ wave: 'square', vol: .15, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 . . . | ...' }, { wave: 'triangle', notes: '...' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h .' }] }`. Tokens: a note (`C4`, `F#3`, `Bb2`), `-` holds, `.` rests, `|` is ignored; drums `k s h o c t`. `music(null)` stops. `mute()`, `setVolume(0..1)` (or set `audio.musicVolume`, `sfxVolume`, then `audio.applyVolume()`), `duck(on)` (quieter music under your own pause or map screen). Your own Web Audio connects to `audio.bus('sfx' | 'music' | 'voice')`, under the volume and mute.\n172\t- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time, headless too, and the sound follows it. `music(song, { sync: true })` pauses with the game (else it plays on); `audio.pos` → `{ t, step, beat, bar, at, loop, bpm, layers, playing }`; `at(step, fn)`, `onBeat(fn)` (on the update step); `tempo(bpm)`; `layer(name or index, vol, fade)` (a track's `name`, `on: false` starts it silent: the chase's drums in by layers); `music(song, { fade: 1 })` crossfades; `sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` over the music; `sfx(voice, { step })` on a step of the music. `settings.audioOffset` (ms, Settings, Sound: Calibrate) sets `audio.offset`; `audio.calibrate()` → `{ tap(), result, done, stop() }`.\n173\t\n174\t## Levels\n175\t**TileMap** (top-down, isometric, brawler): walls stand on the ground plane.\n176\t- `new E.TileMap({ rows, legend, types, floorTex, block: ['water', 'lava'], ao: 5, outside })` (`ao` = width of the contact shadow along wall bases, 0 = none; `outside`: the ground past the map, a color, or `false` for none). `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut: true, cutH, face, roof, beam }` (`cut` lowers walls that block the camera, those `map.isFront(cx, cy, view)` says; a map may replace it; `face`: `'brick'` (default) `'stone' 'rock' 'plank' 'timber' 'plaster' 'hedge' 'none'`; `roof`: `'speckle'` (default) `'plain' 'tiles' 'slab' 'leaves' 'grass'`; `beam` = timber color). `floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view)` returns `[r, g, b]` (or `[r, g, b, glow]`); ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`. A map of more than 128 x 128 cells bakes its floor in squares as the camera comes to them and lets the far ones go (`chunks: true | false` to choose; `map.floorBytes()` what it holds), so a 256 x 256 world map costs what the screen shows.\n177\t- Legend values: a number = wall type, a string = spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn, block }` for both. `block: true` (or the map's `block` list of floor tags) makes deep water, lava or pits stop walkers (jumping bodies pass over); `walkable(cx, cy)`, `block(cx, cy, on)` (a bridge appears). FlowField paths go around blocked cells and follow `block` and `set` at their next `update`.\n178\t- `find(tag)` / `findAll(tag)` give `{ x, y, z, cx, cy }`. Also `cell(cx, cy)`, `set(cx, cy, id)` (open doors), `toCell(x, y)`, `center(cx, cy)`, `heightAt(x, y)`, `floorAt(x, y)`, `groundAt(x, y, r, z)`, `collide(body)`, `los(x0, y0, x1, y1)` (ground only), `bounds(view)` (`{ x0, y0, x1, y1 }`, buffer pixels), `drawFloor(r)`, `queueWalls(r)`.\n179\t\n180\t**Heights** (collision, standing, sight, reach and navigation share one rule): a body's `z` is its feet; a cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)` (0 = floor); a body stands on the highest top within `z + 2`. Eyes at `z + E.HEIGHTS.eye` (30) see a target at `z + E.HEIGHTS.chest` (22); a body's own `eye` / `chest` override them (crouching). Same level: `|dz| <= 8`. On a TileMap (`dir` = an angle or `[dx, dy]`): `ray(a, b, { eye, target })` → `{ clear, t, x, y, z }`, `sight(a, b)`, `standable(x, y, z, r)`, `ledge(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, rise, facing, edge }` (climb), `landing(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, d }` (a jump across a gap), `drop(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, fall }`, `arcClear(a, b, apex, r)`, `pick(sx, sy)` → `{ cx, cy, z, face }` (the cell under a screen point: `input.clicked()`, any view and turn). Helpers: `E.sameLevel(a, b)`, `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, dz, sight: map })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]` nearest first, `E.inArc(..., dz)`. `new E.FlowField(map, { climb, drop, jump })` paths over roofs, between levels and across gaps.\n181\t\n182\t**PlatformMap** (side-scrollers; 2.5D in tilted views): tiles on the x/z plane, top row first.\n183\t- `new E.PlatformMap({ rows, legend, types })`. `types[id] = { kind, style, side, top, line, mat }` (`mat`: any material for a solid tile's face). Example: `rows: ['      ?    ', '   ===     ', '@  g   ^^  ', '###########']`, `legend: { '#': 1, '=': 2, '?': 3, '^': 4, '@': 'hero', 'g': 'walker' }`, `types: { 1: { style: 'ground', side: '#8a5a32' }, 2: { kind: 'oneway' }, 3: { style: 'bonus', side: '#e8a830' }, 4: { kind: 'hazard' } }`. Kinds: `'solid'` (default), `'oneway'` (jump up through), `'ladder'`, `'slope'` (`dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left; `from` / `to` heights 0..1 for gentle slopes), `'hazard'` (spikes, lava), `'deco'`, `'back'` (background wall). Styles: `'stone'` (`moss`) `'brick' 'wood' 'metal' 'ground'` (`flower`) `'block' 'pipe' 'bonus' 'plank' 'spikes' 'ladder' 'liquid' 'plain'`.\n184\t- Spawns: `find(tag)` gives `{ x, z }` with z = the bottom of that cell (feet). Standing on the ground means z = the top of the floor tiles (`level.groundBelow(x, z)`, which returns `null` over a pit), not z = 0. `cell(cx, cz)` / `set(cx, cz, id)` count cz from the bottom row. `slopeZ(cx, cz, x)`, `touching(body, 'hazard')`, `cellsTouching(body, kind)`, `groundBelow(x, z)`, `solidAt(x, z)`, `move(body, dt, solids)`, `bounds(view)`, `draw(r)` (call first, after `r.sky`), `width`, `height`.\n185\t\n186\t## Movement and physics\n187\t**Platformer** (side-scroller character): `const hero = new E.Platformer({ x, z, w: 8, h: 22, run: 95, jump: 285, gravity: 800, airJumps: 0, wallJump: false, dash: 0 })`.\n188\t- Each update: `hero.update(dt, level, game.input, movingPlatforms)`. For enemies pass `{ x: -1..1, jump, jumpPressed, up, down, dash }` instead of the input.\n189\t- Read: `onGround`, `jumped`, `landed`, `facing` (1 or -1), `air`, `climbing`, `hitWall`, `hitCeiling`, `bumped` (`{ cx, cz, id }` of a block hit from below). Methods: `knock(dir)`, `rigState(extra)` (feed it to `rig.update`).\n190\t- Built in: coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height (let go early for a short hop), slopes and small steps (sticks to the ground going down), ladders (up / down), drop through one-way tiles (down + jump), optional double jump, wall jump and dash.\n191\t- Further moves, each on request: `crouch` (down: half as tall, it crawls), `ledge` (catches a ledge falling past it; up climbs), `wallCling`, `momentum` (slopes, a roll, a charged dash); tile kinds `'water'` (it swims), `'rope'` (up grabs it), `'bars'` (it hangs and travels), grip `friction` (ice .15), `carry` (a conveyor), `bounce` (a spring), `crumble` and `respawn` (`level.update(dt)` each step); `hero.swingFrom(x, z)` (a grapple: a pendulum, jump lets go); draw `level.drawWater(r)` after the actors. State: `crouching`, `hanging`, `clinging`, `swimming`, `onRope`, `onBars`, `swinging`, `rolling`; `rigState()` poses them.\n192\t- Moving platforms: `{ x, z, w, h, vx, vz }` boxes you move yourself and pass as `solids`; riders are carried.\n193\t\n194\t**Body** (top-down / isometric / brawler): `new E.Body({ x, y, r: 5, friction: 0, bounce: 0, gravity: 700 })`. Set `vx`, `vy`, then `body.update(dt, map)`. Also `push(ix, iy, iz)` (knockback), `jump(v)`, and `onGround`, `z`, `groundZ`, `hitWall`, `landed`, `bounced` (hit the ground and bounced: dust, a thud). Walls lower than your feet are walkable, so bodies can jump onto blocks.\n195\t\n196\t**Mover** (third person): `const mover = new E.Mover({ x, y })`; `mover.update(dt, map, game.input)` (input `'THIRDPERSON'`) or an AI's `{ move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }`: it sprints, sneaks, climbs, vaults, leaps gaps, hops down and rolls; `climbAny: true` climbs any wall (jump at it; up the screen is up the wall; jump leaps up, crouch lets go; `mover.wall`, `map.wall(from, dir)`), `glide: true` glides (jump again in the air; `lift`: an updraft), `staminaMax: 100` (climbing, gliding and sprinting spend `mover.stamina`); `rig.update(dt, mover.rigState())`; `mover.play('takedown', { other })`, your own `E.ACTIONS`. Stealth: `E.sees(guard, hero, { map })` → `{ seen, exposure }`, `E.hears`, `new E.Suspicion()` (`update(dt, seen, hero)` → `'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'`), `r.sightCone(guard, { map })`, `r.marker(x, y, z, { label })`.\n197\t\n198\t**Attack** (melee timing for swords, punches, kicks): `const slash = new E.Attack('slash')` (a move from `E.MOVES`, optionally with overrides: `new E.Attack('thrust', { reach: 10 })`) or a spec `{ a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, wind, active, recover }`. `if (input.buffered('attack') && slash.start()) input.consume('attack')`; `slash.update(dt)` (returns the phase that just began); `slash.hits(enemies, e => E.inArc(hero, facing, e, 22, 1.4), e => {...})` hits each target once per swing during `'active'` (moves wait until `hitAt: .35` of the swing, so hit-stop freezes the strike, not the wind-up); `rig.update(dt, { ..., attack: slash.state })`. Also `busy`, `active`, `cancel()`. **Combo**: `const combo = new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'], { window: .3 })` (names, specs or Attacks); `combo.press()` starts or chains the next hit; `update`, `hits`, `state`, `step` work the same. **Knockback**: `E.knockback(attacker, target, 160, up)` pushes a Body (or anything with vx / vy) away.\n199\t\n200\t**Bullets**: `const shots = new E.Bullets(game, { plane: 'ground' | 'side' })`; `shots.fire({ x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, r: 2, team: 'player', color, life, dmg, pierce, sprite })`; `shots.burst(base, E.pattern.spread(angle, n, arc, speed))`; `shots.update(dt, map)`; `shots.hit(targets, (b, t) => {...}, 'player')`; `shots.draw(r)`; `shots.clear(team)`. Patterns: `E.pattern.dir(angle, speed)`, `aim(a, b, speed)`, `aimSide(a, b, speed)`, `spread(angle, n, arc, speed)`, `ring(n, speed, offset)`. In side view the second number of a pattern velocity is vz.\n201\t\n202\t**Helpers**: `E.dist(a, b)`, `E.angleTo(a, b)`, `E.arc(a, b, gravity, { apex | time | speed })` → `{ vx, vy, vz, t }` (a throw that lands at b: a lob, a grenade), `E.overlap(a, b)` (circles x, y, r), `E.overlapBox(a, b)` (side boxes x center, z feet, w, h), `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)` (melee cone; `dz` limits the height difference), `E.rand(a, b)`, `E.randInt(a, b)`, `E.pick(list)`, `E.chance(p)`, `E.prune(list, fn)`, `E.store.get(key, fallback)` / `set(key, value)` (save data; slots: `game.saves`), `new E.SpatialHash(32)` (`build(list)`, `near(x, y, r, fn)`), `new E.FlowField(map)` (`update(tx, ty)` or `update([[x, y], ...])`: the nearest; `dir(x, y, tx, ty)` walks around walls and `map.solid` shapes), math `clamp lerp approach ease.* angDiff approachAng smoothDamp rng(seed) noise2 hex shade mix`; `E.version` (the release) and `E.versionLabel()` ('v0.7.0 · a1b2c3d 2026-10-07': show it on a title or a debug screen).\n203\t\n204\t## Characters\n205\t**Humanoid** (knights, heroes, soldiers, zombies): `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'chibi' (default) | 'heroic' (tall, SotN) | 'bulky' (brawlers, bosses) | 'skeleton' (rib cage, skull, glowing eye sockets; tint with colors.bone), weapon: 'sword' (the default) | 'gun' | 'staff' | null (fists), outfit: 'shirt' | 'tunic' | 'robe' | 'coat', hair: 'short' | 'spiky' | 'long' | 'ponytail' | 'bald', hat: 'cap' | 'pointed' | 'helmet' | 'band' | 'crown' (or { style, color }), sleeves: 'short' | 'long' | 'none', armor, hood (in colors.hair), cape: { len: 6, width: 5, seg: 2.5 }, eyeGlow, hunch, lean, style: 'hd' (default) | 'classic' (stick figures for huge crowds), colors: { skin, hair, cloth, coat, pants, boot, belt, trim, glove, cape, capeIn, metal, plate, hilt, eye, iris } })` (`metal` colors the weapon, `plate` the armor, defaulting to `metal`)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"84c94b97-47a4-4e3d-8032-7c7aa879abef","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:30.116Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/API.md","content":"# my-3D2dge API card (v0.88.0)\n\nmy-3D2dge (\"My 3D 2D Game Engine\") is a general-purpose retro-modern 2D game engine built for AI models. Use it to port, remaster, reimagine and remix games from the 8-bit to 64-bit eras (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1), or to make spiritual successors to them. Everything is drawn by code: no image or sound files. Characters are 3D skeletons drawn as pixel art, and the camera can be any classic 2D view.\n\n**Start from a kit:** each genre has one, a complete small game written to be copied (the Genre playbook below names it). In a single file it sits between `GAME START` and `GAME END`: the starter's shell (the game, its title menu, the proof), the slice and the start; a genre kit holds one slice, `dist/my-3d2dge.html` every one and the ANIMATION LAB (how each animation is driven). In the handoff package each kit's game is `kits/<id>.js`, readable. **To make a game, change the slice into yours, with its own title, play and game-over scenes, and delete the starter's menu and the slices you do not use. The engine is yours to change too:** fix, extend or rewrite any of it as you see fit.\n\n**Looking a name up:** grep `API-INDEX.md` (every public name, one line; members are filed under instance names: `rig.`, `talk.`, `mover.`, `flow.`, `map.`), not the engine's source. It, the guide (`AI_GUIDE.md`) and the tools are in the handoff package (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/handoff/) and the repository.\n\n## Quick start (a complete game)\n```js\n(() => {\nconst E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp } = E;            // always start like this\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'snes', view: 'threequarter' });\nconst map = new E.TileMap({\n  rows: ['##########', '#@...s...#', '#..##....#', '#........#', '##########'],\n  legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero', 's': 'slime' },  // number = wall type, string = spawn tag\n  types: { 1: { h: 30, top: '#57506a', side: '#3d3750' } },\n  floorTex: (x, y) => E.tex.flagstone(x, y, { stones: ['#4b4559', '#554f64'].map(E.hex), mortar: E.hex('#221e2b'), hi: E.hex('#6f6782'), lo: E.hex('#322d3e'), speck: E.hex('#2a2634') })\n});\ngame.cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v);\nconst s = map.find('hero'), hero = new E.Body({ x: s.x, y: s.y, r: 5 });\nconst rig = new E.Humanoid({ hat: 'pointed' });\ngame.start({\n  update(dt) {\n    const m = game.input.move(), d = game.view.screenDirToGround(m[0], m[1]);\n    hero.vx = d[0] * 80; hero.vy = d[1] * 80; hero.update(dt, map);\n    if (Math.hypot(d[0], d[1]) > .1) hero.facing = Math.atan2(d[1], d[0]);\n    rig.update(dt, { x: hero.x, y: hero.y, z: hero.z, vx: hero.vx, vy: hero.vy, facing: hero.facing || 0 });\n    game.focus(hero.x, hero.y, 10);                // the camera looks here: call every update\n  },\n  draw(r) {\n    map.drawFloor(r); r.shadow(hero.x, hero.y, 5); map.queueWalls(r);\n    r.actor(hero.x, hero.y, hero.z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view));\n    r.text('HELLO', 4, 4, '#ffffff');              // HUD text in screen pixels\n  }\n});\n})();\n```\n\n## Look target (read this before drawing anything)\nAim above SNES, level with the best PS1 / N64 2D games (Symphony of the Night, Metal Slug, Legend of Mana, Klonoa). Games that skip these steps look like Atari games. **Every game should use all of them:**\n1. **Resolution** `res: 'ps1'` (320x240) or `'snes'`, with `expand: true` so it fills any screen (anchor the HUD to `r.W` / `r.H`), and characters 40-60 px tall: `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3 })` (HD style is the default: shaded volumetric limbs, outfits, hair).\n2. **Dress the character**: an `outfit`, `hair`, a `hat`, `armor`, a `cape`, `sleeves` (Characters), and a real palette in `colors` (never leave the defaults on everyone).\n3. **Backdrop behind every side-view level**: `const bg = new E.Backdrop('castle-night')` then `bg.draw(r)` first in `draw` (a ground view's floor runs on to the screen's edges: `map.outside`). Presets: `day dusk night castle-night city-night desert forest ocean cave space`, or your own `{ sky, sun | moon, stars, starsY: .7, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains' | 'hills' | 'forest' | 'city' | 'castle' | 'clouds' | 'sea' | 'fog' | 'stalactites', color, y, height, parallax, fill }] }` (`starsY` = how far down the stars reach; a layer but clouds, fog and stalactites fills the screen below its skyline unless `fill: false`).\n4. **Textured tiles**: PlatformMap styles `stone` (with `moss`), `brick`, `wood`, `metal`, `ground` (with `flower`), `block`, `pipe`, `bonus`; TileMap floors from `E.tex.*`, TileMap walls with `face: 'brick' | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge'` and tops with `roof: 'tiles' | 'slab' | 'leaves' | 'grass'` (a village = timber and plaster houses with tile roofs, hedges, rock cliffs).\n5. **Props in every room** (3-8 per screen): `r.prop(name, x, y, z, { size, color, light: true })` with `torch candle candelabra chandelier brazier window banner pillar statue crate barrel chest pot sign fence lamp door tree pine bush flowers grass rock crystal bones skull vines cobweb lantern streetlamp awning hydrant trashcan firedrum sandbags cactus palm mushroom`. Flames animate, glow and can light the scene. `chest` / `door` take `{ open: true }`, `pillar` takes `{ broken: true }`, `window` is a gothic rose window with lead cames (`colors: [...]` for the glass, `size: 2-3` for a cathedral); hang `vines`, `cobweb`, `lantern` with `{ anchor: 'top' }`. With heroes at size 1.2+, give the scene `propSize: 1.4` (or set `E.style.propSize`) so props match them.\n6. **Effects on every hit**: `particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)`, `smoke`, `fire`, `glints`, `sparks`, `game.flash(color, s)`, `game.freeze`, `game.shake`.\n7. **Color**: `E.tones(hex)` gives `{ deep, sh, base, lt, hi }` hue-shifted shades and `E.ramp(hex, n)` a palette ramp. Shade everything you draw yourself with them (shadow side `sh`, lit edge `lt`, a `hi` sparkle).\n8. **Typography**: the default font is a 5x7 proportional font with lower case. Titles: `E.font.title(g, 'NAME', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: [...], align: 'center' })`. HUD text: `{ shadow: '#000', outline: false }` or `gradient: [top, bottom]`. `font: 'tiny'` is the old 3x5 font.\n9. **Light every scene**: `game.lights.enabled = true; game.lights.ambient = .5` in a scene's `enter`, then each draw a few lights where light comes from (`r.prop('lamp', x, y, 0, { light: true })`, `game.lights.add(x, y, z, 60, .9, { color })`): dusk, torchlight and glows give depth. It looks the same in every browser and check; `game.enableGPU({ map })` adds shadows in ground views, and falls back to it.\n10. **Faces in dialog**: `talk.say(lines, { name: 'MIRA', portrait: miraRig })` puts a live close-up of that Humanoid (blinking, hair, hat) beside every line. `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40)` draws one anywhere (HUD, RPG party windows).\n\n## Coordinates: what x, y, z mean in each view\nWorld units: one tile = 16. **z is always up.** Game logic never stores screen pixels.\n\n| View | Looks like | Ground plane | Use for levels | Move with |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `'iso'` (yaw 45, pitch 30) | Diablo, Landstalker | x/y ground, z up | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n| `'threequarter'` (pitch 55) | Zelda LttP, Stardew | x right, y down the screen | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n| `'topdown'` (pitch 80) | GTA 1, Hotline Miami | x right, y down | `E.TileMap` | `E.Body` |\n| `'brawler'` (pitch 25) | Final Fight, 2.5D | x right, y into the screen | `E.TileMap` or `E.PlatformMap` | `E.Body` or `E.Platformer` |\n| `'side'` (pitch 0) | Mario, Mega Man | **x right, z up, y = 0 (depth)** | `E.PlatformMap` | `E.Platformer` |\n| `'overhead'` (pitch 90, scale 1) | 1942, Galaga, Pac-Man | x = screen x px, y = screen y px | none, or a TileMap | your own x/y, or `E.Body` |\n\n- A unit is `r.ppu` pixels: at zoom 1 iso 1.41, overhead 1, the others 1.5 (a tile about 24). A sprite's pixel is a screen pixel (`{ world: true }`: it grows with the view, as rigs and maps do).\n- In `'side'` the screen shows x (right) and z (up). **y is depth and is normally 0.** Focus the camera with `game.focus(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 24)`.\n- In ground views, the screen direction the player pushes becomes a world direction through `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`.\n- `r.actor(x, y, z, (g, ox, oy) => ...)` calls your function with `(ox, oy)` = the buffer pixel where world point (x, y, z) lands. Draw the character around that point.\n\n## Rules\n1. Keep game logic in world units, and use `dt` everywhere. `update(dt)` runs in exact fixed steps: every 1/120 s of real time is one step of `dt = 1/120 * game.timeScale`, the same at any refresh rate (`game.alpha` = the part of a step left over).\n2. Draw only with `E.px.*`, `E.font`, `E.ui`, sprites, props and the `r.*` helpers. Never use `ctx.arc`, `ctx.stroke`, `ctx.fillText` or canvas gradients on the game buffer (they blur pixels). For translucency and glow use `px.blend(g, alpha, 'add' | 'multiply' | 'screen' | 'normal', () => {...})`, `px.polyDither`, `px.ddisc`, `r.glowDisc` and `r.shadow`: they use stepped alpha and blend modes like PS1 / SNES hardware (or dithering with `E.style.trans = 'dither'` for an NES / Genesis look).\n3. Anything that can overlap anything else goes through `r.actor`, `r.sprite` or `r.queue` (depth sorted). Backgrounds (`r.sky`, `map.drawFloor`, `level.draw`) come first in `draw`.\n4. Read input only in `update`. Use `pressed(a)` for one-shot actions (menus, jumps, shots), `down(a)` for held actions, and `buffered(a)` + `consume(a)` for forgiving combat input.\n5. Animate characters from continuous values (velocity, timers, easing): the rigs, not frame lists.\n6. Colors are `'#rrggbb'` (or `'#rgb'`) strings.\n7. Put the whole game in one IIFE `(() => { ... })();` between the markers, and start it with `game.start(...)`.\n\n## Common mistakes (check these first)\n- Nothing on screen: the camera looks at the wrong place. Use `game.follow(hero)` once (in the scene's `enter`), or call `game.focus(...)` every update. In side view the hero has y = 0.\n- Walls or level missing: you forgot `map.drawFloor(r)` + `map.queueWalls(r)` (TileMap) or `level.draw(r)` (PlatformMap).\n- Using a TileMap for a side-scroller. Side-scrollers use `E.PlatformMap` + `E.Platformer`.\n- HUD drawn in world coordinates. Use `r.text(...)` or `r.overlay(g => ...)` with screen pixels (`r.W`, `r.H`).\n- An old key layout: platformers want `input: 'PLATFORMER'` (Space jumps), shooters `input: 'SHMUP'` (Space fires).\n- Errors show in a red box on screen and in the console. Fix the first one first.\n- Colours are `'#rrggbb'`, `rgb()`, `hsl()` or a basic name ('orange'); any other draws magenta.\n- `mouseGround()` is `[x, y]`; `settings.reset(group)` resets a group ('Sound'; none: all), not a key; `particles.explosion()` adds a hit-stop and a shake (`{ freeze: false, shake: 0 }`); `E.font.text` outlines unless `outline: false`.\n- A frame past 50 ms gives up its time (no catch-up): a test in a slow browser waits on the game's state, not the clock.\n\n## Game\n`new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', view, views, res, expand, input, bg })`\n- `res`: `'nes'` 256x240, `'snes'` 256x224, `'genesis'` 320x224, `'gb'` 160x144, `'gba'` 240x160, `'ps1'` / `'n64'` 320x240, `'wide'` 400x225, or `[w, h]`. Fixed pixels: whole-number scaling on a desktop screen, the largest fit on a phone (Settings, Pixel scaling). Leave it out for an adaptive size.\n- `expand: true`: full screen at any shape, no bars. `res` becomes the least the screen shows and `r.W` x `r.H` grows to fill the window (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427x240 of `'ps1'`, a phone held upright about 320x690). Anchor the HUD to `r.W` and `r.H`, and give the level something to show in the extra space (more of it, a backdrop, a HUD deck under it on a tall screen). `expand: { maxW, maxH }` caps the growth. A scene may set it too.\n- Adaptive size: `minW` / `minH` set the scale (the screen shows at least that many pixels), `maxW` / `maxH` cap the view (the rest is letterboxed). `portrait: { maxH: 1000 }` overrides them while the screen is taller than wide, so a phone held upright (9:16) is filled instead of showing a band across its middle. `game.screen.portrait` says which way it is.\n- `input`: `'DEFAULT'` (top-down action), `'PLATFORMER'`, `'SHMUP'`, `'THIRDPERSON'`, or your own `{ action: ['KeyQ', 'Pad0'] }`.\n- `views`: the views your game supports; `game.nextView()` cycles them (bind it to a key).\n- Start: `game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) })`, or with scenes (below).\n- Methods: `follow(obj, { z: 16, lead: 20 })` (camera tracks it automatically), `focus(x, y, z)` (or aim it yourself every update), `setView(id)`, `nextView()`, `freeze(s)` (hit-stop: `update` waits; `game.hitstop` is the time left), `shake(n)`, `after(s, fn)`, `every(s, fn)` (timers in game time, return `{ cancel() }`), `hook('input' | 'step' | 'enter', fn)` (your own each step, paused too for input; returns its removal), `mouseGround()`, `go(scene, data)`, `enableGPU({ map })`.\n- Fields: `W`, `H` (screen pixels), `time`, `real`, `timeScale` (0.25 = slow motion), `paused`, `input`, `audio`, `particles`, `lights`, `view`, `errors`, `cam`. `game.fail('my power', err)` reports an error in your code (logged, in `errors`, on the error panel) and plays on.\n- Camera zoom and turn: `game.setZoom(1.5)` (.5 .. 3; everything drawn by the engine re-rasterizes crisply, the HUD stays), `game.rotateView(45)` (ground views only), `game.resetCamera()` (to the scene's zoom), `game.zoom`, `game.yaw`, `game.note('ZOOM 2x')` (a short notice at the top of the screen; `game.shownNote` is the one showing). Custom painters can read `r.view.zoom` (undefined at 1x) to scale their own art.\n- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` lets the player zoom within `zoom` from `start` (wheel, `-` `=`, pinch, Settings; `false`: none; `AI_GUIDE.md`, Camera).\n- Tests and tools: `new E.Game({ headless: true })` (the default in Node: no screen, sound or DOM; `draw` is skipped, or with `draw: true` run on a stand-in, its errors in `game.errors`) or `{ loop: false }` runs only by `game.advance(seconds, { draw })` (exact fixed steps at once); `input.tap(action)` presses for one step, `input.hold(action, on)`; `game.debug = { snapshot() {...} }` is your game's handle (`tools/check.mjs` prints it); `game.stop()`, `start()`, `dispose()`.\n- Sessions: `?record=1`, then the pause menu's Save session; `node tools/replay.mjs session.json` replays it exactly, headless (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Sessions).\n- Camera: `cam.offset = [dx, dy]` sits the focus that many screen pixels from the centre (above a phone's touch buttons: `[0, -60]`); `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` keeps it inside the level (in side views a level shorter than the screen sits on the bottom edge; `cam.align = 'center'` to change); `cam.room = [160, 128]` moves screen by screen (Zelda, Metroid) and `cam.moving` is true while it slides; `cam.smooth` = lag in seconds (0: locked to the target); `cam.window = [48, 24]` a dead zone the focus moves in before the camera follows; `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (side-scrollers); `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps them all in view, zooming out as they part (`frame(null)` stops); `cam.free = { keys, edge: 10, drag, speed: 300, bounds: [x0, y0, x1, y1] }` a free camera (strategy, sims): keys, a stick, the screen's edge or a drag pan it, a zoom goes toward the pointer or the pinch.\n\n## Scenes (title, play, game over)\n```js\ngame.start({ scene: 'title', scenes: {\n  title: { update(dt) { if (game.input.pressed('start')) game.go('play', { level: 1 }); },\n           draw(r) { r.sky(['#101848', '#402a70']); r.text('PRESS START', r.W / 2, 100, '#fff', { align: 'center', scale: 2 }); } },\n  play: { pausable: true, view: 'side', input: 'PLATFORMER', res: 'snes', views: ['side', 'brawler'], touch: ['jump', 'attack'],\n          enter(data) { /* build the level */ }, exit() {}, update(dt) {}, draw(r) {} }\n} });\n```\n`game.go(name, data)` switches after a short dithered fade (`game.fadeTime`: a test advances past it; `{ fade: 0 }` cuts; `{ fade: 'wipe' | 'iris' | 'mosaic' | 'swirl' | 'dissolve' | 'white', seconds: .4, at: hero }` a transition, a plain fade under reduce motion); timers (but `after(s, fn, { keep: true })`) and particles are cleared, a hit-stop ends (`timeScale` stays). `pausable: true`: Escape / P / Start pauses it, with the pause menu (below). `view`, `views`, `input`, `res`, `expand`, `touch`, `camera`, `propSize` are options applied on entry (a method so named warns). A scene is one object every visit: reset its state in `enter`. `back()` takes a phone's back gesture first (true if it went back: a sub-menu closed).\n\n## Motion and timing: tweens, scripts, paths, weather\n- Tweens (Phaser's): `game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases numbers on game time: paused with the game, slowed by `timeScale`, ended by a scene change unless `keep`. Returns `{ done, cancel() }`. `E.ease` has Penner's set: `linear`, `inSine` `outSine` `inOutSine`, `inQuad` `outQuad` `inOutQuad`, the same for `Cubic`, `Back`, `Elastic`, `Bounce`.\n- Scripts (Unity's coroutines) for cutscenes, boss patterns, wave timelines, tutorials:\n```js full-script\ngame.script(function* () {\n  yield 1;                                   // seconds of game time\n  yield E.until(() => door.open);            // a condition\n  yield game.tween(boss, { y: 80 }, 1.5);    // a tween (or another script, or a generator) finishing\n  for (let w = 0; w < 3; w++) { spawnWave(w); yield E.any([8, () => enemies.length === 0]); }\n});\n```\n  `E.all([...])` waits for all; a bare `yield` waits one step. It runs at once to its first `yield`, the same at any refresh rate; `{ keep: true }` outlives the scene; `.cancel()`, `.done`, `.value`.\n- Paths: `const p = new E.Path([[x, y, z], ...], { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })`: `p.length`, `p.at(d)` (`{ x, y, z, angle }` by distance along: moving platforms, dives, rails), `p.nearest(x, y)` (a racer's progress), `p.draw(r)` to see it.\n- Weather: `game.weather = { kind: 'rain' | 'snow' | 'ash' | 'leaves' | 'sand', amount: .8, wind: .3, lightning: .2 }` (null: clear) falls through the world in every view, settles or splashes, flashes and thunders; cosmetic only (its own random stream), and it ends with the scene: set it in `enter`.\n- Wrap-around (Asteroids): `x = E.wrap(x, 0, W)`, `E.wrapDiff(a, b, W)` (the short way across the seam), and `wrap: [W, H]` on `r.sprite` or `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge.\n\n## Standard features (every game has them: don't build your own)\nWith no code: a **pause menu** on pausable scenes (Resume, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Quit to title; keys, mouse, gamepad and touch), **settings** saved per game (`game.settings`: volume, music, effects, mute, reduce motion, shake and flashes, pixel scaling, a screen filter, the frame rate and its readout), **controls** the player rebinds (conflicts said first; dead zone, invert aim and move, button symbols, vibration; touch button side, size, opacity), **full screen** (a phone's first tap, a top-right button, the menu; turning it off is remembered), a **menu button** at the top right (on a touch screen it stays in play: it is the touch START), a **way out** of every menu screen, the **back gesture** stepping back in the game (`game.back()`) before it leaves the page, **pausing** when the page is hidden or the controller unplugs, and a page set up for phones (safe areas, no scrolling, zooming or pull to refresh on the canvas).\n- Adjust: `new E.Game({ menu: { items: [['Restart', fn]], settings: [{ key: 'hints', label: 'Hints', value: true }, { key: 'difficulty', options: ['Easy', 'Normal', 'Hard'], value: 'Normal' }], controls: { attack: 'Sword', skill: false }, quit: 'title' } })`, then read `game.settings.hints`. Leave parts out: `menu: false` (your own pause), `fullscreen: 'button' | false`, `corner: false`, `backGesture: false`, `autoPause: false`, `touch: 'never'` (a phone is told a controller or keyboard is needed). `game.cornerArea()`: where the corner's buttons cover the screen (or null): keep a HUD clear of it.\n- Save slots (`game.saves`): three slots and an autosave, each with its place, level and time played. `game.saves.write = () => ({ data, place, level })` and `read = save => {...}`; then `save(slot)` (0, 1, 2 or `'auto'`), `load(slot)`, `copy(a, b)`, `erase(slot)`, `autosave()`, `list()`, `latest()` (a title's Continue). `menu: { saves: true }` (or `{ slots, version, migrate(data, from) }`) puts Save (while `saves.canSave`: a save point) and Load in the pause menu, and `saves.show('save' | 'load')` opens the screen from your own menu. Blocked storage keeps them for the visit.\n- Prompts: `game.input.label('jump')` is that action's key or button on the player's device, after rebinding ('Space', 'A', '✕'). PlayStation symbols by default: `settings: [{ key: 'glyphs', value: 'playstation' }]` (a standard key without a label sets its default). A canvas title's OPTIONS: `game.menu('settings')`. From code: `game.settings.set(key, v)`, `game.fullscreen.toggle()` (from a key, click or tap), `game.pause(reason)`, `game.resume()`. `live: true` (or `game.menu(name, { pause: false })`): menus over a world that keeps stepping (online play).\n- **Boot**: heavy setup in steps behind a boot screen, and a report for any error before play: `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { title, steps: [['Building the city', () => { map = build(); }]] } })` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Boot).\n\n## Input: `game.input`\n- Actions in every preset: `up down left right` (WASD, arrows, d-pad), `start` (Enter, Start), `pause` (Escape, P, Start), `confirm` (Enter, Space, J, Z, pad A), `cancel` (Escape, Backspace, X, K, pad B).\n- DEFAULT adds `attack` (J, X, click), `dash` (Space, Shift, K), `skill` (L, E, right click), `jump` (Z). PLATFORMER: `jump` (Space, Z, K, pad A), `attack` (X, J, click), `dash` (Shift, C, L), `skill`. SHMUP: `fire` (Space, Z, J, click), `bomb` (X, K). THIRDPERSON: `sprint` (Shift), `jump` (Space), `crouch` (C, Ctrl), `interact` (E, F), `attack` (J), `block` (K), `dodge` (L), `use` (Q), `map` (M, Tab).\n- `down(a)`, `pressed(a)`, `released(a)`, `repeat(a, delay, rate)` (menus), `buffered(a, win)` + `consume(a)`, `consumeAll()`, `anyPressed()`, `move()` (screen direction `[x, y]`, length up to 1), `use(preset)`, `touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'])`, `touchSet('confirm', 'TALK', near)` (a context button: relabel it, or hide it while it has nothing to do).\n- Moves: `input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing })` is true on the step a quarter circle forward and punch ends (numpad notation, mirrored when `facing` is -1: `'623'`, `'41236'`); `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down+right', 'attack'], { window: .5 })` any run of inputs (a cheat code); `input.history` the last second of them (`{ t, dir, pressed }`, for an input display).\n- Players (two to four on one machine): `game.players` is `[game.input, ...]`, each an input with this API, its `player` number, `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`) and device. `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => {} })` lets them in: Start on a free gamepad, or 2 on the keyboard (player 2 takes its right half: arrows, `.` `/` `,` and the numpad; player 1 keeps WASD with F G H; a preset's `halves: { left: { pass: ['KeyR'] }, right: {...} }` its own); `game.addPlayer({ pad: 0 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })`, `game.removePlayer(p)`. Prompts, the pause (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller and the Controls screen are per player. One screen: `cam.frame(heroes)`. Split: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [h1, h2], layout: 'auto' })` (`'columns'`, `'rows'`, `'grid'`; up to 4) draws the scene once per viewport (`r.viewport` is `{ i, x, y, w, h }`), then `scene.hud(r)` once over the whole screen; `game.viewports[i].follow(t)` or `.focus(x, y)`.\n- `clear()` releases held inputs, pending presses, virtual sticks and touch buttons; focus loss does this automatically. `suppress(a)` blocks an action for the current tick; `suppressCode('Mouse0')` lets a HUD widget consume that mouse button while other devices bound to the action still work.\n- Controller settings: `deadzone` (default `.18`, radial and rescaled), `invertAimY`, `lastDevice` (the index has the rest). The left stick and the touch stick, pushed past halfway, press `up` / `down` / `left` / `right` as the d-pad does (menus, dialog choices, ladders); bind `PadAxisUp/Down/Left/Right` to your own menu actions for the same; the right stick is `PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right`, the wheel `WheelUp`/`WheelDown`; `value(a)`: 0 to 1 (a trigger's travel, a stick's push). Options while Create is held is no press (the AI Hub's chord).\n- Touch settings: `touchEnabled`, `stickSide` (`'left'` or `'right'`), `stickRadius` (CSS pixels), `touchFilter(event)` (return false to reserve a canvas tap for UI). `touchArea()`: where the touch buttons cover the screen (`{ x0, y0, x1, y1 }`, or null): keep a prompt clear of it. `clicked()`: where a click or tap on the canvas landed (`[x, y]` in screen pixels) the step after it, else null; `pointer()`: the mouse in screen pixels; `drag()`: how far the stick's finger moved this step (`[dx, dy]`, or null), to steer one to one; `steer = 'drag'`: a finger anywhere steers, no stick drawn (`stickOn`: a finger is on it). `zones = [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }]` (fractions of the canvas): a finger in one holds its act until it lifts (a pinball's halves). Gestures (a mouse or one finger, screen pixels): `box()` → `{ x0, y0, x1, y1, done }` (a left drag; on touch a long press, then a drag), `longPress()`, `doubleTap()`, `pan()` → `[dx, dy]` (a finger's drag on open ground, a middle or right drag), `zoomPoint()`; `feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch, button, id })` plays a pointer in a test. `bindButtons(root)` wires `[data-act]` elements.\n\n## Drawing: `r` in `draw(r)`\n- Background: `r.sky(['#top', '#mid', '#bottom'])`, `r.starfield({ count, speed, dir: 'down' | 'left' })`.\n- Depth sorted: `r.actor(x, y, z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view), { flash: '#ffffff', flashMix: .75, alpha, xray, outline, ghost, emissive, every, key })` (the flash tints over the sprite so shading shows: `flashMix` .5 by default, 1 solid; `every: 2, key: npc` redraws a background character every other frame; actors up to about 250 px tall), `r.sprite(x, y, z, sprite, { flip, anchor: 'bottom' | 'center', glow, flash })`, `r.queue(x, y, z, g => {...}, { bias })` (sorted by that one point: a long wall by `r.queueSpan(a, b, z, h, fn)`), `r.actorNow(...)` (as `r.actor`, drawn at once over what is drawn so far), `r.box(g, x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, top, side)` (inside a queue function). `{ pick: unit }` on any of them (and `r.shape`), then `r.pick(sx, sy)` → what the last frame drew under a screen point, front to back.\n- Floor marks: `r.shadow(x, y, radius, alpha, color, surfaceZ)`, `r.decal(g => r.groundArc(...))`, `r.groundDisc`, `r.groundRing`, `r.groundPts(x, y, rad, n, z)`.\n- On top, unlit, screen pixels: `r.text(str, x, y, color, { align, scale, outline })`, `r.textAt(x, y, z, str, color)`, `r.overlay(g => {...})`.\n- `r.w(x, y, z)` = buffer pixel of a world point. `r.W`, `r.H`, `r.ix`, `r.iy` (pass to dither functions), `r.view`, `r.visible(x, y, z)`.\n- X-ray: `r.actor(..., { xray: true })` shows the actor's hidden part, a translucent tint with a 1-px outline (a checker in `E.style.trans = 'dither'`), wherever a wall, a prop or shape at least chest high (22; `hides: true | false` says otherwise) or anything queued with `{ hides: true }` is drawn in front of it; not by one rising less than 8 above its feet (the roof it stands on, a cut-down wall). `xray: { color, outline (false: none), actors: true }`: with `actors` other bodies in front hide it too (a hero stays findable in a crowd). Afterimages: `ghost: { color, life, alpha, owner }`; `owner` (the actor's `{x, y, z}`) keeps a trail left nearer the camera sorted behind the live body.\n- Glowing things you draw yourself: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { emissive: true })` is drawn again after lighting so darkness never dims it (flames, lasers, magic, neon). It still hides behind whatever stands in front of it: walls and `r.actor` bodies do this on their own, and any other queued draw can join them with `{ solid: true }`. (Canvas lighting builds a depth-ordered mask for this; `game.r.glowMask = false` skips it on slow machines.)\n\n## Pixels, sprites, text, UI\n- `px.rect(g, x, y, w, h, c)`, `dot`, `line(g, x0, y0, x1, y1, c, width)`, `disc(g, x, y, r, c)`, `ell`, `poly(g, pts, c)`, `polyDither(g, pts, c, 0..1, r.ix, r.iy)`, `ddisc`, `sprite(g, spr, x, y, flip)` (or `{ flip, rot, scale }`), `glow(g, 0..1)` (GPU glow for what follows).\n- Sprites from strings (for coins, bullets, ships, icons, NES-style enemies): `const COIN = E.sprite(['.yy.', 'yYYy', '.yy.'], { y: '#f0b020', Y: '#fff0a0' }, scale)`. `.` and space are transparent. Frames: `const walk = E.anim([f1, f2, f3], 8, { pingpong })` (or one sheet: `E.anim(sheet, 8, { frames: 4 })`), drawn with `r.sprite(x, y, z, walk, { t })`. `r.sprite(..., { rot: angle, scale })` turns and scales in whole pixels (cached in 16 steps a turn; `steps: 32`); `E.recolor(spr, { '#3a8a4a': '#8a3a4a' })` swaps a palette.\n- Font (5x7 proportional with lower case; `font: 'tiny'` for 3x5): `E.font.text(g, str, x, y, color, { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font })`, `E.font.title(g, str, x, y, { scale, colors, outline, depth, depthColor, align })`, `E.font.width(str, opts)`, `E.font.lineHeight(opts)`, `E.font.wrap(str, maxW, opts)`. Glyphs: ASCII plus `♥ ★ ← → ↑ ↓ • × ♪ ▸ ©`.\n- UI (inside `r.overlay(g => ...)`): `E.ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, { bg, border, gradient, alpha })` (a gradient window like Final Fantasy; `bg` can be `[top, bottom]`, `gradient: false` for flat, `alpha: .6` for a see-through HUD panel), `E.ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, 0..1, color)`, `E.ui.hearts(g, x, y, hp, max)` (half hearts allowed), `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, size, { zoom: 3, turn: .6, bg, frame, cape: false, weapon: false, tint: '#808080' })` (character close-up; it stays upright while the rig kneels or lies down; `tint` greys out a KO'd hero).\n- Dialog: `const talk = new E.Dialog(game)`; `talk.say(['Line one.', 'Page two.'], { name: 'ELDER', portrait: elderRig, onDone, choices: ['YES', 'NO'], onChoice(i) })` (choices show on the last page: the keys, or a tap or click on one, pick); in update `if (talk.update(dt)) return;` (true while a modal box is open: play waits); in draw `talk.draw(r)`. `portrait` takes a Humanoid (live close-up; `portraitSize: 40`, `portraitOpts: { zoom, turn }`) or a function `(g, x, y, size) => {...}`. Radio chatter during play: `talk.say('Enemy fighters ahead!', { name, portrait, auto: 2.5, modal: false })` turns its pages and closes by itself while the game keeps running. Per line: `place: 'top' | 'middle' | 'bottom'`, `y` (pixels) and `alpha` (see-through box). `new E.Dialog(game, { speed, lines, color, nameColor, voice, width, bg, border, vars })` (`voice`: a sound, or `(ch, i, talk) => {}` per letter); `talk.close()`, `talk.open`. Markup in the text: `{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}` a color, `{w=.5}` a pause, `{s=12}slowly{/s}` letters a second, `{shake}..{/shake}`, `{wave}..{/wave}`, `{$name}` a value from `say(text, { vars })` or `talk.vars`.\n- Menu: `const menu = new E.Menu(game, ['START', { label: 'LOAD', disabled: true }], { x: 'center', y: 100, title, onPick(i, item), onCancel })`; `menu.update(dt)`; `menu.draw(r)`. Keys, d-pad and sticks move it; a click or tap on an entry picks it (so a phone can leave a title), and the mouse over one moves the cursor. A tap on a dialog box turns its page as confirm does.\n- HTML screens and HUD: `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme, settings, screens: { pause, help } })`; `ui.show('title' | 'results' | 'map' | ..., spec)`; each step `ui.hud({ objective, status, bars, prompt, minimap })` (`minimap: { map, fog, camera: true, markers, onClick(x, y) }`); `ui.toast(title, text)`.\n\n## Sound: `game.audio` (chip synth, no files, starts after the first key press)\n- `sfx(name, { vol, pitch })`. Presets: `jump jump2 land step coin pickup key powerup oneup heal hit hurt stomp bump swing whoosh punch kick shoot laser charge explode boom door secret warp die select confirm cancel pause text blip`.\n- Custom: `sfx({ wave: 'square' | 'pulse' | 'pulse12' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise', freq: 440 or 'A4', to: 880, dur: .2, vol: .3, arp: [0, 4, 7], step: .05, vib: [8, .03] })`, or `audio.define('zap', voice)`.\n- Music: `music('title' | 'adventure' | 'dungeon' | 'boss' | 'victory')` (built in; any other name warns and plays nothing), or your own `{ bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [{ wave: 'square', vol: .15, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 . . . | ...' }, { wave: 'triangle', notes: '...' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h .' }] }`. Tokens: a note (`C4`, `F#3`, `Bb2`), `-` holds, `.` rests, `|` is ignored; drums `k s h o c t`. `music(null)` stops. `mute()`, `setVolume(0..1)` (or set `audio.musicVolume`, `sfxVolume`, then `audio.applyVolume()`), `duck(on)` (quieter music under your own pause or map screen). Your own Web Audio connects to `audio.bus('sfx' | 'music' | 'voice')`, under the volume and mute.\n- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time, headless too, and the sound follows it. `music(song, { sync: true })` pauses with the game (else it plays on); `audio.pos` → `{ t, step, beat, bar, at, loop, bpm, layers, playing }`; `at(step, fn)`, `onBeat(fn)` (on the update step); `tempo(bpm)`; `layer(name or index, vol, fade)` (a track's `name`, `on: false` starts it silent: the chase's drums in by layers); `music(song, { fade: 1 })` crossfades; `sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` over the music; `sfx(voice, { step })` on a step of the music. `settings.audioOffset` (ms, Settings, Sound: Calibrate) sets `audio.offset`; `audio.calibrate()` → `{ tap(), result, done, stop() }`.\n\n## Levels\n**TileMap** (top-down, isometric, brawler): walls stand on the ground plane.\n- `new E.TileMap({ rows, legend, types, floorTex, block: ['water', 'lava'], ao: 5, outside })` (`ao` = width of the contact shadow along wall bases, 0 = none; `outside`: the ground past the map, a color, or `false` for none). `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut: true, cutH, face, roof, beam }` (`cut` lowers walls that block the camera, those `map.isFront(cx, cy, view)` says; a map may replace it; `face`: `'brick'` (default) `'stone' 'rock' 'plank' 'timber' 'plaster' 'hedge' 'none'`; `roof`: `'speckle'` (default) `'plain' 'tiles' 'slab' 'leaves' 'grass'`; `beam` = timber color). `floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view)` returns `[r, g, b]` (or `[r, g, b, glow]`); ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`. A map of more than 128 x 128 cells bakes its floor in squares as the camera comes to them and lets the far ones go (`chunks: true | false` to choose; `map.floorBytes()` what it holds), so a 256 x 256 world map costs what the screen shows.\n- Legend values: a number = wall type, a string = spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn, block }` for both. `block: true` (or the map's `block` list of floor tags) makes deep water, lava or pits stop walkers (jumping bodies pass over); `walkable(cx, cy)`, `block(cx, cy, on)` (a bridge appears). FlowField paths go around blocked cells and follow `block` and `set` at their next `update`.\n- `find(tag)` / `findAll(tag)` give `{ x, y, z, cx, cy }`. Also `cell(cx, cy)`, `set(cx, cy, id)` (open doors), `toCell(x, y)`, `center(cx, cy)`, `heightAt(x, y)`, `floorAt(x, y)`, `groundAt(x, y, r, z)`, `collide(body)`, `los(x0, y0, x1, y1)` (ground only), `bounds(view)` (`{ x0, y0, x1, y1 }`, buffer pixels), `drawFloor(r)`, `queueWalls(r)`.\n\n**Heights** (collision, standing, sight, reach and navigation share one rule): a body's `z` is its feet; a cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)` (0 = floor); a body stands on the highest top within `z + 2`. Eyes at `z + E.HEIGHTS.eye` (30) see a target at `z + E.HEIGHTS.chest` (22); a body's own `eye` / `chest` override them (crouching). Same level: `|dz| <= 8`. On a TileMap (`dir` = an angle or `[dx, dy]`): `ray(a, b, { eye, target })` → `{ clear, t, x, y, z }`, `sight(a, b)`, `standable(x, y, z, r)`, `ledge(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, rise, facing, edge }` (climb), `landing(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, d }` (a jump across a gap), `drop(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, fall }`, `arcClear(a, b, apex, r)`, `pick(sx, sy)` → `{ cx, cy, z, face }` (the cell under a screen point: `input.clicked()`, any view and turn). Helpers: `E.sameLevel(a, b)`, `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, dz, sight: map })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]` nearest first, `E.inArc(..., dz)`. `new E.FlowField(map, { climb, drop, jump })` paths over roofs, between levels and across gaps.\n\n**PlatformMap** (side-scrollers; 2.5D in tilted views): tiles on the x/z plane, top row first.\n- `new E.PlatformMap({ rows, legend, types })`. `types[id] = { kind, style, side, top, line, mat }` (`mat`: any material for a solid tile's face). Example: `rows: ['      ?    ', '   ===     ', '@  g   ^^  ', '###########']`, `legend: { '#': 1, '=': 2, '?': 3, '^': 4, '@': 'hero', 'g': 'walker' }`, `types: { 1: { style: 'ground', side: '#8a5a32' }, 2: { kind: 'oneway' }, 3: { style: 'bonus', side: '#e8a830' }, 4: { kind: 'hazard' } }`. Kinds: `'solid'` (default), `'oneway'` (jump up through), `'ladder'`, `'slope'` (`dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left; `from` / `to` heights 0..1 for gentle slopes), `'hazard'` (spikes, lava), `'deco'`, `'back'` (background wall). Styles: `'stone'` (`moss`) `'brick' 'wood' 'metal' 'ground'` (`flower`) `'block' 'pipe' 'bonus' 'plank' 'spikes' 'ladder' 'liquid' 'plain'`.\n- Spawns: `find(tag)` gives `{ x, z }` with z = the bottom of that cell (feet). Standing on the ground means z = the top of the floor tiles (`level.groundBelow(x, z)`, which returns `null` over a pit), not z = 0. `cell(cx, cz)` / `set(cx, cz, id)` count cz from the bottom row. `slopeZ(cx, cz, x)`, `touching(body, 'hazard')`, `cellsTouching(body, kind)`, `groundBelow(x, z)`, `solidAt(x, z)`, `move(body, dt, solids)`, `bounds(view)`, `draw(r)` (call first, after `r.sky`), `width`, `height`.\n\n## Movement and physics\n**Platformer** (side-scroller character): `const hero = new E.Platformer({ x, z, w: 8, h: 22, run: 95, jump: 285, gravity: 800, airJumps: 0, wallJump: false, dash: 0 })`.\n- Each update: `hero.update(dt, level, game.input, movingPlatforms)`. For enemies pass `{ x: -1..1, jump, jumpPressed, up, down, dash }` instead of the input.\n- Read: `onGround`, `jumped`, `landed`, `facing` (1 or -1), `air`, `climbing`, `hitWall`, `hitCeiling`, `bumped` (`{ cx, cz, id }` of a block hit from below). Methods: `knock(dir)`, `rigState(extra)` (feed it to `rig.update`).\n- Built in: coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height (let go early for a short hop), slopes and small steps (sticks to the ground going down), ladders (up / down), drop through one-way tiles (down + jump), optional double jump, wall jump and dash.\n- Further moves, each on request: `crouch` (down: half as tall, it crawls), `ledge` (catches a ledge falling past it; up climbs), `wallCling`, `momentum` (slopes, a roll, a charged dash); tile kinds `'water'` (it swims), `'rope'` (up grabs it), `'bars'` (it hangs and travels), grip `friction` (ice .15), `carry` (a conveyor), `bounce` (a spring), `crumble` and `respawn` (`level.update(dt)` each step); `hero.swingFrom(x, z)` (a grapple: a pendulum, jump lets go); draw `level.drawWater(r)` after the actors. State: `crouching`, `hanging`, `clinging`, `swimming`, `onRope`, `onBars`, `swinging`, `rolling`; `rigState()` poses them.\n- Moving platforms: `{ x, z, w, h, vx, vz }` boxes you move yourself and pass as `solids`; riders are carried.\n\n**Body** (top-down / isometric / brawler): `new E.Body({ x, y, r: 5, friction: 0, bounce: 0, gravity: 700 })`. Set `vx`, `vy`, then `body.update(dt, map)`. Also `push(ix, iy, iz)` (knockback), `jump(v)`, and `onGround`, `z`, `groundZ`, `hitWall`, `landed`, `bounced` (hit the ground and bounced: dust, a thud). Walls lower than your feet are walkable, so bodies can jump onto blocks.\n\n**Mover** (third person): `const mover = new E.Mover({ x, y })`; `mover.update(dt, map, game.input)` (input `'THIRDPERSON'`) or an AI's `{ move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }`: it sprints, sneaks, climbs, vaults, leaps gaps, hops down and rolls; `climbAny: true` climbs any wall (jump at it; up the screen is up the wall; jump leaps up, crouch lets go; `mover.wall`, `map.wall(from, dir)`), `glide: true` glides (jump again in the air; `lift`: an updraft), `staminaMax: 100` (climbing, gliding and sprinting spend `mover.stamina`); `rig.update(dt, mover.rigState())`; `mover.play('takedown', { other })`, your own `E.ACTIONS`. Stealth: `E.sees(guard, hero, { map })` → `{ seen, exposure }`, `E.hears`, `new E.Suspicion()` (`update(dt, seen, hero)` → `'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'`), `r.sightCone(guard, { map })`, `r.marker(x, y, z, { label })`.\n\n**Attack** (melee timing for swords, punches, kicks): `const slash = new E.Attack('slash')` (a move from `E.MOVES`, optionally with overrides: `new E.Attack('thrust', { reach: 10 })`) or a spec `{ a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, wind, active, recover }`. `if (input.buffered('attack') && slash.start()) input.consume('attack')`; `slash.update(dt)` (returns the phase that just began); `slash.hits(enemies, e => E.inArc(hero, facing, e, 22, 1.4), e => {...})` hits each target once per swing during `'active'` (moves wait until `hitAt: .35` of the swing, so hit-stop freezes the strike, not the wind-up); `rig.update(dt, { ..., attack: slash.state })`. Also `busy`, `active`, `cancel()`. **Combo**: `const combo = new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut'], { window: .3 })` (names, specs or Attacks); `combo.press()` starts or chains the next hit; `update`, `hits`, `state`, `step` work the same. **Knockback**: `E.knockback(attacker, target, 160, up)` pushes a Body (or anything with vx / vy) away.\n\n**Bullets**: `const shots = new E.Bullets(game, { plane: 'ground' | 'side' })`; `shots.fire({ x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, r: 2, team: 'player', color, life, dmg, pierce, sprite })`; `shots.burst(base, E.pattern.spread(angle, n, arc, speed))`; `shots.update(dt, map)`; `shots.hit(targets, (b, t) => {...}, 'player')`; `shots.draw(r)`; `shots.clear(team)`. Patterns: `E.pattern.dir(angle, speed)`, `aim(a, b, speed)`, `aimSide(a, b, speed)`, `spread(angle, n, arc, speed)`, `ring(n, speed, offset)`. In side view the second number of a pattern velocity is vz.\n\n**Helpers**: `E.dist(a, b)`, `E.angleTo(a, b)`, `E.arc(a, b, gravity, { apex | time | speed })` → `{ vx, vy, vz, t }` (a throw that lands at b: a lob, a grenade), `E.overlap(a, b)` (circles x, y, r), `E.overlapBox(a, b)` (side boxes x center, z feet, w, h), `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)` (melee cone; `dz` limits the height difference), `E.rand(a, b)`, `E.randInt(a, b)`, `E.pick(list)`, `E.chance(p)`, `E.prune(list, fn)`, `E.store.get(key, fallback)` / `set(key, value)` (save data; slots: `game.saves`), `new E.SpatialHash(32)` (`build(list)`, `near(x, y, r, fn)`), `new E.FlowField(map)` (`update(tx, ty)` or `update([[x, y], ...])`: the nearest; `dir(x, y, tx, ty)` walks around walls and `map.solid` shapes), math `clamp lerp approach ease.* angDiff approachAng smoothDamp rng(seed) noise2 hex shade mix`; `E.version` (the release) and `E.versionLabel()` ('v0.7.0 · a1b2c3d 2026-10-07': show it on a title or a debug screen).\n\n## Characters\n**Humanoid** (knights, heroes, soldiers, zombies): `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'chibi' (default) | 'heroic' (tall, SotN) | 'bulky' (brawlers, bosses) | 'skeleton' (rib cage, skull, glowing eye sockets; tint with colors.bone), weapon: 'sword' (the default) | 'gun' | 'staff' | null (fists), outfit: 'shirt' | 'tunic' | 'robe' | 'coat', hair: 'short' | 'spiky' | 'long' | 'ponytail' | 'bald', hat: 'cap' | 'pointed' | 'helmet' | 'band' | 'crown' (or { style, color }), sleeves: 'short' | 'long' | 'none', armor, hood (in colors.hair), cape: { len: 6, width: 5, seg: 2.5 }, eyeGlow, hunch, lean, style: 'hd' (default) | 'classic' (stick figures for huge crowds), colors: { skin, hair, cloth, coat, pants, boot, belt, trim, glove, cape, capeIn, metal, plate, hilt, eye, iris } })` (`metal` colors the weapon, `plate` the armor, defaulting to `metal`).","numLines":205,"startLine":1,"totalLines":298,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KtfGkDVB8tghaLdMNAAfxW","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj8ykajyBHYXD7SzzWZ3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Uw711R8AUQF9JpQuSHzHPq","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/kits/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":33866,"cache_read_input_tokens":29406,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":33866},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"214ff6bb-f2cc-4035-8179-bd033dcbe4c8","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:30.808Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj8ykK83NB45vD6u5dMZ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Uw711R8AUQF9JpQuSHzHPq":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/kits/README.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Uw711R8AUQF9JpQuSHzHPq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# The genre kits (v0.88.0)\n2\t\n3\tA kit is a complete small game in one genre, written to be copied: its title, play and end, the modules it rests on,\n4\tits sound, touch controls and a phone's screen. The API card's Genre playbook (`API.md`) names a kit for each genre (its\n5\t\"Copy slice\" column); the table says which file is which. Each kit is the same game as the single-file kit on the site\n6\t(my-3d2dge.vercel.app/kits/<id>), readable, on this package's engine.\n7\t\n8\t- **`<id>.js`**, its game: the starter's shell (the title menu, the keys every kit shares, the proof), the slice, and the\n9\t  start, as the single-file kit holds them between `GAME START` and `GAME END`. Its header says what it plays, its keys,\n10\t  its deep links and how it works.\n11\t- **`<id>.html`** plays it (open it, or `node tools/check.mjs kits/<id>.html#<id>`); `#<id>` starts the slice, and\n12\t  `#<id>/<part>` jumps into a part.\n13\t- **`node kits/test.mjs <id>`** runs it headless: the slice plays, and its proof gives the same hash twice.\n14\t\n15\t**Start a game from one:** copy `<id>.js` to `game/game.js` (and, if its page loads an asset set, that `<script>` line into\n16\t`game/index.html` before `game.js`). Then replace the starter's title menu with your own title scene and keep only your\n17\tgame, as its header says. Its test: copy `kits/test.mjs`'s three stand-ins (`window`, `addEventListener`, `location`) into\n18\t`game/test.mjs`. A game of several genres reads the kits it mixes and builds on the full engine, which has every module.\n19\t\n20\t| Kit | Files | What it plays | Modules | Deep links |\n21\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n22\t| ADVENTURE | [adventure.js](adventure.js), [adventure.html](adventure.html) | a top-down action adventure: a village, ruins and a shrine, a sword, a key and a great door, talk with portraits; Zelda and Secret of Mana | none | #adventure |\n23\t| POINT-AND-CLICK | [pointclick.js](pointclick.js), [pointclick.html](pointclick.html) | an adventure of three rooms: a dock, a tavern, a lighthouse; four puzzles, the nine verbs or a verb coin, an inventory, a riddle contest | click, story | #pointclick |\n24\t| PLATFORMER | [platformer.js](platformer.js), [platformer.html](platformer.html) | a side-scroller: a night approach to a castle in five zones, a sword, a dash, candles, a 2.5D view; Castlevania, Mega Man X, Klonoa | none | #platformer |\n25\t| METROIDVANIA | [metroidvania.js](metroidvania.js), [metroidvania.html](metroidvania.html) | a world of rooms: its map, two abilities that open its gates, a save room, two bosses | rooms | #metroidvania, #metroidvania/miniboss, #metroidvania/boss, #metroidvania/map |\n26\t| SHOOTER | [shooter.js](shooter.js), [shooter.html](shooter.html) | a vertical shoot-'em-up stage, then a horizontal one: waves, power-ups, bosses; 1943 and Raiden, then Gradius and R-Type | arcade | #shooter, #shooter/horizontal |\n27\t| ARCADE | [arcade.js](arcade.js), [arcade.html](arcade.html) | a cabinet of three: maze, formation, rocks | arcade, grid, gen | #arcade, #arcade/maze, #arcade/formation, #arcade/rocks |\n28\t| PINBALL | [pinball.js](pinball.js), [pinball.html](pinball.html) | one table: flippers, bumpers, a ramp, drop targets, a spinner, a kickback, a mission, multiball, tilt, a dot-matrix display | physics, arcade | #pinball |\n29\t| ARTILLERY | [artillery.js](artillery.js), [artillery.html](artillery.html) | two teams of three on an island every blast carves: turns, aim and power, wind, a rocket, a bouncing grenade, a cluster bomb, falls and the sea, a CPU that tries its shots in simulation | physics, terrain, grid | #artillery, #artillery/cpu |\n30\t| PUZZLE | [puzzle.js](puzzle.js), [puzzle.html](puzzle.html) | falling blocks and falling pairs that chain, versus with a second player or the CPU; Tetris, Puyo Puyo | grid | #puzzle |\n31\t| RHYTHM | [rhythm.js](rhythm.js), [rhythm.html](rhythm.html) | four lanes on the music clock: two songs, notes, jumps, holds, a rapping teacher to answer, a dancer and a crowd on the beat, grades, the audio offset | songs, rhythm | #rhythm, #rhythm/stepup, #rhythm/rapschool, #rhythm/calibrate |\n32\t| RPG | [rpg.js](rpg.js), [rpg.html](rpg.html) | a JRPG's journey: a town, a world map, a cave and its boss, the ATB battle, menus and save slots | story, rpg | #rpg, #rpg/battle |\n33\t| ROGUELIKE | [roguelike.js](roguelike.js), [roguelike.html](roguelike.html) | a turn-based crypt of ten generated floors: fields of view and fog, turns by speed, potions and scrolls; Rogue, Mystery Dungeon | grid, gen | #roguelike |\n34\t| TACTICS | [tactics.js](tactics.js), [tactics.html](tactics.html) | an isometric battle on a map of heights: charge time or phases, facing, arcs, areas, a scoring AI | grid, story | #tactics, #tactics/battle, #tactics/phases |\n35\t| RTS | [rts.js](rts.js), [rts.html](rts.html) | real-time strategy: workers, gold, a barracks and a stable, three kinds to fight, fog, an enemy camp that builds and attacks in waves | grid, strategy | #rts |\n36\t| SIM | [sim.js](sim.js), [sim.html](sim.html) | a theme park: paths, four rides and two shops, guests with needs and money, prices, a rating, day and night, time controls | grid, strategy | #sim |\n37\t| BRAWLER | [brawler.js](brawler.js), [brawler.html](brawler.html) | a beat-'em-up: a city block at dusk, combos, grabs and kicks, waves that lock the screen, a second player who drops in; Final Fight, Streets of Rage | none | #brawler |\n38\t| FIGHTING | [fighting.js](fighting.js), [fighting.html](fighting.html) | one on one: three fighters, versus, arcade, training | fight | #fighting, #fighting/versus, #fighting/arcade, #fighting/training |\n39\t| ANIMATION LAB | [animlab.js](animlab.js), [animlab.html](animlab.html) | every move, pose, skin and view of the character rigs | none | #animlab |\n40\t| KART | [kart.js](kart.js), [kart.html](kart.html) | Mode 7 karts: six on a circuit, three laps, items, two players in a split screen | mode7, drive | #kart, #kart/race, #kart/vs |\n41\t| ROAD | [road.js](road.js), [road.html](road.html) | a pseudo-3D coast road against the clock: curves, hills, a tunnel, traffic, checkpoints, a radio | road | #road, #road/drive |\n42\t| SPORTS | [sports.js](sports.js), [sports.html](sports.html) | two against two in a walled cage, up to four players: a ball that bounces and curls, passes, lobs, shots on a meter, formations, a match clock; Sensible Soccer and NBA Jam | none | #sports, #sports/match, #sports/vs |\n43\t| STEALTH | [stealth.js](stealth.js), [stealth.html](stealth.html) | third-person stealth and parkour: a rooftop district at dusk, guards with sight and suspicion, climbing, takedowns, an HTML HUD; Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear | none | #stealth |\n44\t\n45\t**3D:** the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows, are not in this package. They are on the\n46\tsite (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d, /kits/3d-<genre>) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge:\n47\t`engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, its header the 3D manual; `src/starter3d/`). Here, a 3D game is built on the 2D engine's views,\n48\ta world in x, y and z drawn from any angle (`API.md`, Coordinates).\n49\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"f148bbe0-bed1-4922-a93a-bf4516693306","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:30.814Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/kits/README.md","content":"# The genre kits (v0.88.0)\n\nA kit is a complete small game in one genre, written to be copied: its title, play and end, the modules it rests on,\nits sound, touch controls and a phone's screen. The API card's Genre playbook (`API.md`) names a kit for each genre (its\n\"Copy slice\" column); the table says which file is which. Each kit is the same game as the single-file kit on the site\n(my-3d2dge.vercel.app/kits/<id>), readable, on this package's engine.\n\n- **`<id>.js`**, its game: the starter's shell (the title menu, the keys every kit shares, the proof), the slice, and the\n  start, as the single-file kit holds them between `GAME START` and `GAME END`. Its header says what it plays, its keys,\n  its deep links and how it works.\n- **`<id>.html`** plays it (open it, or `node tools/check.mjs kits/<id>.html#<id>`); `#<id>` starts the slice, and\n  `#<id>/<part>` jumps into a part.\n- **`node kits/test.mjs <id>`** runs it headless: the slice plays, and its proof gives the same hash twice.\n\n**Start a game from one:** copy `<id>.js` to `game/game.js` (and, if its page loads an asset set, that `<script>` line into\n`game/index.html` before `game.js`). Then replace the starter's title menu with your own title scene and keep only your\ngame, as its header says. Its test: copy `kits/test.mjs`'s three stand-ins (`window`, `addEventListener`, `location`) into\n`game/test.mjs`. A game of several genres reads the kits it mixes and builds on the full engine, which has every module.\n\n| Kit | Files | What it plays | Modules | Deep links |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ADVENTURE | [adventure.js](adventure.js), [adventure.html](adventure.html) | a top-down action adventure: a village, ruins and a shrine, a sword, a key and a great door, talk with portraits; Zelda and Secret of Mana | none | #adventure |\n| POINT-AND-CLICK | [pointclick.js](pointclick.js), [pointclick.html](pointclick.html) | an adventure of three rooms: a dock, a tavern, a lighthouse; four puzzles, the nine verbs or a verb coin, an inventory, a riddle contest | click, story | #pointclick |\n| PLATFORMER | [platformer.js](platformer.js), [platformer.html](platformer.html) | a side-scroller: a night approach to a castle in five zones, a sword, a dash, candles, a 2.5D view; Castlevania, Mega Man X, Klonoa | none | #platformer |\n| METROIDVANIA | [metroidvania.js](metroidvania.js), [metroidvania.html](metroidvania.html) | a world of rooms: its map, two abilities that open its gates, a save room, two bosses | rooms | #metroidvania, #metroidvania/miniboss, #metroidvania/boss, #metroidvania/map |\n| SHOOTER | [shooter.js](shooter.js), [shooter.html](shooter.html) | a vertical shoot-'em-up stage, then a horizontal one: waves, power-ups, bosses; 1943 and Raiden, then Gradius and R-Type | arcade | #shooter, #shooter/horizontal |\n| ARCADE | [arcade.js](arcade.js), [arcade.html](arcade.html) | a cabinet of three: maze, formation, rocks | arcade, grid, gen | #arcade, #arcade/maze, #arcade/formation, #arcade/rocks |\n| PINBALL | [pinball.js](pinball.js), [pinball.html](pinball.html) | one table: flippers, bumpers, a ramp, drop targets, a spinner, a kickback, a mission, multiball, tilt, a dot-matrix display | physics, arcade | #pinball |\n| ARTILLERY | [artillery.js](artillery.js), [artillery.html](artillery.html) | two teams of three on an island every blast carves: turns, aim and power, wind, a rocket, a bouncing grenade, a cluster bomb, falls and the sea, a CPU that tries its shots in simulation | physics, terrain, grid | #artillery, #artillery/cpu |\n| PUZZLE | [puzzle.js](puzzle.js), [puzzle.html](puzzle.html) | falling blocks and falling pairs that chain, versus with a second player or the CPU; Tetris, Puyo Puyo | grid | #puzzle |\n| RHYTHM | [rhythm.js](rhythm.js), [rhythm.html](rhythm.html) | four lanes on the music clock: two songs, notes, jumps, holds, a rapping teacher to answer, a dancer and a crowd on the beat, grades, the audio offset | songs, rhythm | #rhythm, #rhythm/stepup, #rhythm/rapschool, #rhythm/calibrate |\n| RPG | [rpg.js](rpg.js), [rpg.html](rpg.html) | a JRPG's journey: a town, a world map, a cave and its boss, the ATB battle, menus and save slots | story, rpg | #rpg, #rpg/battle |\n| ROGUELIKE | [roguelike.js](roguelike.js), [roguelike.html](roguelike.html) | a turn-based crypt of ten generated floors: fields of view and fog, turns by speed, potions and scrolls; Rogue, Mystery Dungeon | grid, gen | #roguelike |\n| TACTICS | [tactics.js](tactics.js), [tactics.html](tactics.html) | an isometric battle on a map of heights: charge time or phases, facing, arcs, areas, a scoring AI | grid, story | #tactics, #tactics/battle, #tactics/phases |\n| RTS | [rts.js](rts.js), [rts.html](rts.html) | real-time strategy: workers, gold, a barracks and a stable, three kinds to fight, fog, an enemy camp that builds and attacks in waves | grid, strategy | #rts |\n| SIM | [sim.js](sim.js), [sim.html](sim.html) | a theme park: paths, four rides and two shops, guests with needs and money, prices, a rating, day and night, time controls | grid, strategy | #sim |\n| BRAWLER | [brawler.js](brawler.js), [brawler.html](brawler.html) | a beat-'em-up: a city block at dusk, combos, grabs and kicks, waves that lock the screen, a second player who drops in; Final Fight, Streets of Rage | none | #brawler |\n| FIGHTING | [fighting.js](fighting.js), [fighting.html](fighting.html) | one on one: three fighters, versus, arcade, training | fight | #fighting, #fighting/versus, #fighting/arcade, #fighting/training |\n| ANIMATION LAB | [animlab.js](animlab.js), [animlab.html](animlab.html) | every move, pose, skin and view of the character rigs | none | #animlab |\n| KART | [kart.js](kart.js), [kart.html](kart.html) | Mode 7 karts: six on a circuit, three laps, items, two players in a split screen | mode7, drive | #kart, #kart/race, #kart/vs |\n| ROAD | [road.js](road.js), [road.html](road.html) | a pseudo-3D coast road against the clock: curves, hills, a tunnel, traffic, checkpoints, a radio | road | #road, #road/drive |\n| SPORTS | [sports.js](sports.js), [sports.html](sports.html) | two against two in a walled cage, up to four players: a ball that bounces and curls, passes, lobs, shots on a meter, formations, a match clock; Sensible Soccer and NBA Jam | none | #sports, #sports/match, #sports/vs |\n| STEALTH | [stealth.js](stealth.js), [stealth.html](stealth.html) | third-person stealth and parkour: a rooftop district at dusk, guards with sight and suspicion, climbing, takedowns, an HTML HUD; Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear | none | #stealth |\n\n**3D:** the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows, are not in this package. They are on the\nsite (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d, /kits/3d-<genre>) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge:\n`engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, its header the 3D manual; `src/starter3d/`). Here, a 3D game is built on the 2D engine's views,\na world in x, y and z drawn from any angle (`API.md`, Coordinates).\n","numLines":49,"startLine":1,"totalLines":49}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Uw711R8AUQF9JpQuSHzHPq","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj8ykajyBHYXD7SzzWZ3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PGswUXMgCXe8M7b2nAJFXQ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/_research/hub/lib/hub/retrofit.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":33866,"cache_read_input_tokens":29406,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":33866},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"c7f8aae1-c8c4-417b-b65e-12e799df2a9d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:31.299Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj8ykK83NB45vD6u5dMZ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01PGswUXMgCXe8M7b2nAJFXQ":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/_research/hub/lib/hub/retrofit.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PGswUXMgCXe8M7b2nAJFXQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Upgrading an existing game to parallel\n2\t\n3\tThe prompt `scripts/retrofit.mjs` gives a model for each game, after its tool\n4\tstep has put the session client and the parallel metas at the top of the\n5\tpage. The model works in a copy of the game in a folder of its own: `{{file}}`\n6\tis the game's page there, `{{contract}}` the protocol in\n7\t[`README.md`](README.md) from *Modes* to *Rules*, word for word (the same text\n8\tfor every game, so a batch reads it from the cache), `{{budget}}` the most lines a try may change,\n9\tand `{{notes}}` what the last try's check said, if there was one.\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\tYou are upgrading one existing browser game, `{{file}}`, so friends can play it\n14\tin parallel mode in AI Hub sessions: each friend plays their own copy at the same\n15\ttime, and the hub keeps the score. Change as little as possible. Never change\n16\thow the game plays or feels: movement, physics, timing, controls, menus, art,\n17\taudio and difficulty stay exactly as they are.\n18\t\n19\tA tool has already put the session client (`session.js`, which defines\n20\t`window.AIHub`) and the `hub:session` meta tags at the top of `<head>`. This is\n21\tthe protocol; the game is upgraded to its parallel mode, and the hub checks it\n22\tagainst it:\n23\t\n24\t<protocol>\n25\t{{contract}}\n26\t</protocol>\n27\t\n28\tDo only this:\n29\t\n30\t1. Find how the game is won or scored. Use a time (lower wins) if it has a\n31\t   clear finish, otherwise its score (higher wins).\n32\t2. Fill `hub:session-goal` (how to win, at most 60 characters) and\n33\t   `hub:session-howto` (the controls, at most 80 characters), in plain words\n34\t   for someone who has never seen the game. If a round of the game has a\n35\t   fixed length, add `<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"…\">` with it.\n36\t   If the game cannot run at all without WebGPU, also add\n37\t   `<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">`.\n38\t3. In session mode (`AIHub.active` is true): skip the title screen, attract\n39\t   mode and menus; `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'parallel', seats: [1, 4] })`;\n40\t   build the game; call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as it can start, with no \"press\n41\t   start\" prompt; begin play only once `AIHub.time() >= 0`. If `connect()`\n42\t   answers `null`, carry on as when `AIHub.active` is false.\n43\t4. Whenever the score changes, call\n44\t   `AIHub.progress({ score: <number>, label: <short text> })`, where a higher\n45\t   score is further ahead and `label` is short text for the scoreboard.\n46\t5. When the run ends (finished, died, out of lives), call\n47\t   `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })` once. In\n48\t   `AIHub.on('time-up', …)`, report the current score at once if the run is\n49\t   still going.\n50\t6. In `AIHub.on('end', …)`, stop input and sound.\n51\t7. If the game deals its pieces, cards, board or level layout from one\n52\t   function using `Math.random`, make that function draw from\n53\t   `AIHub.rng('deal')` in session mode, so every copy gets the same deal all\n54\t   round. Leave every other random call alone.\n55\t8. When `AIHub.active` is false, the game must behave exactly as before.\n56\t\n57\tDon't add features, refactor, reformat, rename or upgrade anything, and don't\n58\ttouch code these steps don't need: change at most {{budget}} lines. Never edit\n59\tthe session client between its `/*! aihub-session` banner and its last line,\n60\tthe names of the meta tags, or a bundled library (three.js, Rapier and the\n61\tlike). Saved progress is already hidden in a session (the client gives the\n62\tgame an empty storage), and `Math.random` is already seeded: add no code for\n63\teither. You are done when the game would pass the checks the protocol\n64\tdescribes; you need not run them, the hub does.\n65\t\n66\tIf the game has no score and no finish, change nothing and reply with one\n67\tline: `NOT PARALLEL: <why>`.\n68\t\n69\tOtherwise reply with: the score or time used, the end condition, and every line\n70\tyou changed.\n71\t\n72\t{{notes}}\n73\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"69392a87-ff7e-4179-903e-cd9c99eb58b9","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:31.306Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/_research/hub/lib/hub/retrofit.md","content":"# Upgrading an existing game to parallel\n\nThe prompt `scripts/retrofit.mjs` gives a model for each game, after its tool\nstep has put the session client and the parallel metas at the top of the\npage. The model works in a copy of the game in a folder of its own: `{{file}}`\nis the game's page there, `{{contract}}` the protocol in\n[`README.md`](README.md) from *Modes* to *Rules*, word for word (the same text\nfor every game, so a batch reads it from the cache), `{{budget}}` the most lines a try may change,\nand `{{notes}}` what the last try's check said, if there was one.\n\n---\n\nYou are upgrading one existing browser game, `{{file}}`, so friends can play it\nin parallel mode in AI Hub sessions: each friend plays their own copy at the same\ntime, and the hub keeps the score. Change as little as possible. Never change\nhow the game plays or feels: movement, physics, timing, controls, menus, art,\naudio and difficulty stay exactly as they are.\n\nA tool has already put the session client (`session.js`, which defines\n`window.AIHub`) and the `hub:session` meta tags at the top of `<head>`. This is\nthe protocol; the game is upgraded to its parallel mode, and the hub checks it\nagainst it:\n\n<protocol>\n{{contract}}\n</protocol>\n\nDo only this:\n\n1. Find how the game is won or scored. Use a time (lower wins) if it has a\n   clear finish, otherwise its score (higher wins).\n2. Fill `hub:session-goal` (how to win, at most 60 characters) and\n   `hub:session-howto` (the controls, at most 80 characters), in plain words\n   for someone who has never seen the game. If a round of the game has a\n   fixed length, add `<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"…\">` with it.\n   If the game cannot run at all without WebGPU, also add\n   `<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">`.\n3. In session mode (`AIHub.active` is true): skip the title screen, attract\n   mode and menus; `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'parallel', seats: [1, 4] })`;\n   build the game; call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as it can start, with no \"press\n   start\" prompt; begin play only once `AIHub.time() >= 0`. If `connect()`\n   answers `null`, carry on as when `AIHub.active` is false.\n4. Whenever the score changes, call\n   `AIHub.progress({ score: <number>, label: <short text> })`, where a higher\n   score is further ahead and `label` is short text for the scoreboard.\n5. When the run ends (finished, died, out of lives), call\n   `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })` once. In\n   `AIHub.on('time-up', …)`, report the current score at once if the run is\n   still going.\n6. In `AIHub.on('end', …)`, stop input and sound.\n7. If the game deals its pieces, cards, board or level layout from one\n   function using `Math.random`, make that function draw from\n   `AIHub.rng('deal')` in session mode, so every copy gets the same deal all\n   round. Leave every other random call alone.\n8. When `AIHub.active` is false, the game must behave exactly as before.\n\nDon't add features, refactor, reformat, rename or upgrade anything, and don't\ntouch code these steps don't need: change at most {{budget}} lines. Never edit\nthe session client between its `/*! aihub-session` banner and its last line,\nthe names of the meta tags, or a bundled library (three.js, Rapier and the\nlike). Saved progress is already hidden in a session (the client gives the\ngame an empty storage), and `Math.random` is already seeded: add no code for\neither. You are done when the game would pass the checks the protocol\ndescribes; you need not run them, the hub does.\n\nIf the game has no score and no finish, change nothing and reply with one\nline: `NOT PARALLEL: <why>`.\n\nOtherwise reply with: the score or time used, the end condition, and every line\nyou changed.\n\n{{notes}}\n","numLines":73,"startLine":1,"totalLines":73}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PGswUXMgCXe8M7b2nAJFXQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj8z1xhqDmUrQe1QYpjY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KgTP9sYZjbBRA2xtdesSih","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/API.md","offset":205},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":26022,"cache_read_input_tokens":63272,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":26022},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"786e3b4f-1bc9-4d99-92f2-8faad72b11b8","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:33.643Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj8z1jZZWYgKjJygqoGj","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KgTP9sYZjbBRA2xtdesSih":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/API.md","offset":205}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KgTP9sYZjbBRA2xtdesSih","type":"tool_result","content":"205\t**Humanoid** (knights, heroes, soldiers, zombies): `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'chibi' (default) | 'heroic' (tall, SotN) | 'bulky' (brawlers, bosses) | 'skeleton' (rib cage, skull, glowing eye sockets; tint with colors.bone), weapon: 'sword' (the default) | 'gun' | 'staff' | null (fists), outfit: 'shirt' | 'tunic' | 'robe' | 'coat', hair: 'short' | 'spiky' | 'long' | 'ponytail' | 'bald', hat: 'cap' | 'pointed' | 'helmet' | 'band' | 'crown' (or { style, color }), sleeves: 'short' | 'long' | 'none', armor, hood (in colors.hair), cape: { len: 6, width: 5, seg: 2.5 }, eyeGlow, hunch, lean, style: 'hd' (default) | 'classic' (stick figures for huge crowds), colors: { skin, hair, cloth, coat, pants, boot, belt, trim, glove, cape, capeIn, metal, plate, hilt, eye, iris } })` (`metal` colors the weapon, `plate` the armor, defaulting to `metal`).\n206\t- `rig.update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, aim, attack })`. `facing` is an angle (side view: 0 = right, `Math.PI` = left). `air: true` tucks the legs for jumps; `point: true` holds a gun forward; `aim` tilts it (radians, + = up: `aim: .8` shoots diagonally up, `Math.PI / 2` straight up).\n207\t- **Moves** (`E.MOVES`, use them instead of hand-made specs; every one has anticipation, a committed strike with lunge / step / lean / twist, follow-through and a clean return to the stance, on every build and in every view):\n208\t  - blades and clubs: `slash backslash overhead rising thrust spin plunge twohand`;\n209\t  - fists: `jab` (left hand) `cross hook uppercut haymaker elbow`;\n210\t  - kicks: `kick roundhouse sweep flyingkick knee axekick`;\n211\t  - other: `cast` (both hands) `throw bash claw`.\n212\t  Give each character its own set (a knight: `overhead`, `thrust`, `spin`; a mage: `cast`; a monk: `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse`; a boss: `haymaker`, `sweep`). `E.move('uppercut', .5)` is the attack state of any move at any moment (previews, cutscenes).\n213\t- `attack: { spec, phase: 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover', u: 0..1 }`, spec `{ a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, r0, r1, hand, rel, blade, spin, kick, plane, lunge, hop, crouch, lean, twist, hold }`. Angles are relative to facing (+ = right hand side). `rel: true`: heights relative to the shoulder and reach scaled by arm length (kick heights as a share of hip height). `r0`/`r1`: reach that extends (thrusts, jabs). `hand: 'L' | 'both'`. In side views horizontal swings turn into the screen plane (a Castlevania chop) by themselves; `plane: 'side' | 'ground'` (or the rig option `swingPlane`) chooses. `lunge` steps into the strike (body motion in local units, clamped to -4..5; to move the character itself, add speed to your body), `hop` lifts the body, `crouch` dips in the wind-up, `hold` is how long the end pose holds (share of recover). You time the phases.\n214\t- In steep ground views (threequarter) rigs and Blobs are drawn from a lower, sprite-like angle so faces show, like SNES RPG sprites (`charView: false` turns it off for one rig, `E.style.charPitch = false` for all).\n215\t- Faces (eyes with catch-lights, brows, mouth), a cheated 3/4 turn toward the camera in side views (`cheat: .5`, 0 turns it off) and hair layers are automatic. `face: { eyes: 'big' (anime / Mana style), bangs: 0..1 }`.\n216\t- Poses: `rig.update(dt, { ..., pose: 'cheer' | 'cast' | 'guard' | 'kneel' | 'crouch' | 'wave' | 'hips' | 'down' })` (victory with the weapon raised, spell casting, a boxer's guard, hurt or resting, ducking, greeting, a cocky idle, knocked out; they blend in and out), `'block'` (weapon held upright across the body), `'die'` (a stagger, the knees give, then the body topples), `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'` (faces and portraits), `run: 0..1` (run in place), or `down: 0..1` to lie a defeated body flat (centred on its position). `stance: 'guard'` keeps fists up while walking (brawlers; the free hand guards the chin during punches), `stance: 'ready'` holds the weapon forward. `climb: true` with `vz` climbs a ladder hand over hand (`Platformer.rigState()` passes both). Traversal: `crouch` (a sneaking gait), `sprint` (a lean), `hang` with `hangZ` (hands on an edge that high above the feet), `flip: 0..1` (a roll), `land: k` (a heavy landing); `mover.rigState()` sets them. Every wind-up starts from the rig's current pose, so chained hits and hand changes never snap. Idle rigs breathe and shift their weight by themselves.\n217\t- `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, view)` inside `r.actor`, `rig.drawSmear(r)` (a glowing trail along the real blade, fist or foot path, in every view), `rig.debug(r)` (skeleton), `rig.kick(v)` (squash and stretch), `rig.hand()` and `rig.tip()` (world points to spawn bullets from), `rig.worldOffset(p)` (where a point in the rig's own frame, such as a joint in `rig.J`, sits from its feet: to draw or spawn at any joint), `rig.lastView` (the view it last drew with), `rig.solvePose()` (sets `rig.J` from its state each step: wrap it on one rig to move joints).\n218\t**Blob** (slimes, rabbites, bats, imps, round monsters): `new E.Blob({ R: 6, colors: { dk, base, lt, spec, eye, pupil }, ears: 'rabbit' | 'cat', horns: true, wings: true, feet: true, tail: true, mouth: true | 'fangs', face: 'front' })`, `update(dt, { squash: -0.4..0.4, look: [dx, dy], squint, walk: 0..1, flap: 0..1 (wing speed; 0 folds them), hang: true (roost upside down) })`, `kick(v)`, `draw(g, ox, oy, view)`. Mix the parts for a bestiary: `wings` + `mouth: 'fangs'` is a bat, `ears: 'rabbit'` + `feet` a Mana rabbite, `horns` + `tail` an imp.\n219\t\n220\t## Effects and lighting\n221\t- Particles: `game.particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` (fireball, smoke, sparks, debris, ring, shake, sound), `fire(x, y, z, n, { size })`, `smoke(x, y, z, n, { size, color })`, `glints(x, y, z, n, color)`, `dust(x, y, z, n)`, `sparks(x, y, z, n, angle)`, `bits(x, y, z, n, colors)`, `ring(x, y, r0, r1, color, dur)`, `text(x, y, z, '12', color, { scale: 2, bounce: true })` (bouncing damage numbers), `add({ kind: 'dust' | 'spark' | 'bit' | 'ember' | 'fire' | 'smoke' | 'glint', ... })`. `explosion(x, y, z, size, { debris, freeze: false, shake: 0 })` for chains of small blasts. Side-scrollers: `particles.ground = (x, y, z) => level.groundBelow(x, z)` so sparks and debris bounce on platforms. Screen flash: `game.flash('#ffffff', .12, strength 0..1)`.\n222\t- Lighting (step 9; every view, side-scrollers included, no WebGPU needed): `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color: '#ffa050' })` every draw. Dark areas get a smooth cool tint, lights add their own color, and emissive items (flames, sparks, bullets, `emissive: true`) stay bright. `lights.darkness` (0..1) and `lights.tint` tune it; after setting `lights.dark = [r, g, b]`, `levels` or `glow`, call `lights.refresh()`. Props with flames can light the scene: `r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { light: true })`. Lamps, street lamps, lanterns and crystals glow on their own (`haloR`, `haloColor`, `halo: false`); `cone: true` adds a light cone under a street lamp.\n223\t- Hits: `game.hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage: 12, textScale: 2, textZ: 22 })` does hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks, a bouncing damage number (`bounce: false` floats it) and a sound in one call; `particles.impact(x, y, z, size, color)` for the star alone.\n224\t- GPU lighting (optional, ground views with a TileMap): `const gpu = game.enableGPU({ map })`; mark glowing things with `px.glow(g, 1)`; add `lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` and `lights.heat(x, y, z, w)`. Where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `game.lights.ambient` (`gpu.fallback = false`: unlit).\n225\t\n226\t## Genre playbook (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1)\n227\t| Genre (examples) | View | Build it with | Copy slice |\n228\t|---|---|---|---|\n229\t| Platformer (Mario, Mega Man, Sonic, Castlevania) | `side` (+ `brawler` for 2.5D) | PlatformMap, Platformer, Humanoid, sprites, Bullets `'side'` | PLATFORMER |\n230\t| Metroidvania (Metroid, Symphony of the Night) | `side` | an `E.World` of PlatformMaps (the rooms module: doors at open edges, a slide, the explored map, gates opened by abilities, saves), a Platformer given `airJumps` and `dash` as they are found, its further moves, an arm cannon aimed eight ways (`point`, `aim`, `rig.tip()`, Bullets) | METROIDVANIA |\n231\t| Action adventure (Zelda, Secret of Mana) | `threequarter` or `topdown` | TileMap rows, `cam.room`, Body, Humanoid + `E.Attack` sword, Dialog, `E.ui.hearts` | ADVENTURE |\n232\t| Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword) | none: rooms painted in screen pixels (`r.overlay`) | `E.Click` (the click module): rooms with walk areas and holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots, the nine verbs, Sierra's icons or a verb coin, an inventory, puzzles as rules; talk through `E.Story` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n233\t| Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad) | none | `E.Story` nodes and choices over a painted backdrop, `rig.drawPortrait` at size, `story.log` (the backlog), `story.auto`, `story.skip`; `game.saves` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n234\t| Action RPG / dungeon (Diablo, Landstalker) | `iso` | TileMap, FlowField, Body (jump onto blocks), GPU lighting | ADVENTURE |\n235\t| Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage, TMNT) | `brawler` | TileMap street strip, Body, Humanoid (`weapon: null`) + `E.Combo` punches and `kick: true` kicks, `E.knockback`, waves that lock the screen; a second player drops in (`game.allowJoin`, `cam.frame` on both) | BRAWLER |\n236\t| Fighting (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers) | `side` | two `E.Fighter`s in an `E.Match` (the fight module): moves with frame data, guards, throws, specials by motion (`'236'`) or charge, a meter, projectiles; `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`, training | FIGHTING |\n237\t| Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius, R-Type) | `overhead` (vertical, and horizontal: world x scrolls) | sprites, Bullets, `E.pattern`, a timeline of waves; horizontal: rock edges as functions of x, a power bar, options on the ship's past path, `E.Path` loops; `E.Arcade` for the continue and the table | SHOOTER (both stages) |\n238\t| Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet, Devil's Crush) | a steep table view of its own (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`) | `E.Physics`: chains for the walls, `flipper`, `bumper`, `slingshot`, `kicker`, a ramp on a layer of its own, the ball a `bullet`; `input.zones` flip on a phone; `E.Arcade` for the score and table. Breakout and Asteroids with real collisions are its recipes | PINBALL |\n239\t| Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth) | `side` at a whole scale (`new E.View(id, label, 0, 0, 1, 1)`) | `E.Terrain` (`carve` the blasts, `walk` the soldiers, `ray` the shots), grenades as `E.Physics` bodies on `land.collider()`, `E.Turns`; a drag aims (`steer = 'drag'`, `drag()`, `stickOn`); a CPU tries its shots in simulation | ARTILLERY |\n240\t| Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids) | `overhead` (one fixed screen, cabinet art on the sides) | `E.Arcade` (score, lives, extra lives, turns, initials, tables, attract mode); a maze of `E.GridMover`s; moths on `E.Path`s turned by `rot`; `E.wrap` and `wrap: [w, h]` | ARCADE |\n241\t| Twin-stick / run-and-gun (Smash TV, Contra) | `topdown` / `side` | Body or Platformer, Bullets, `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` | SHOOTER + ADVENTURE |\n242\t| JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy, EarthBound) | `threequarter` town, `brawler` battles | TileMap towns and a big world map, the story and rpg modules, Dialog with markup, `game.saves` slots | RPG |\n243\t| Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo, Columns) | `side` (a field in screen pixels) | a cell array, a bag from `E.rng`, `input.repeat` (auto-shift), `grid.groups` (chains), `input.drag()` and `clicked()` for touch; versus: two fields, rows sent across (`game.players`) | PUZZLE |\n244\t| Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren) | `threequarter` / `iso` | `E.gen` floors, a TileMap of its rows, `E.Grid.from(map)` paths and `fov`, `E.Fog`, `E.Turns` by speed, `game.tween` steps | ROGUELIKE |\n245\t| Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem) | `iso`, turned a quarter | TileMap of block heights, `map.pick` under the cursor, `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true })` reach over heights, `grid.shape`, `grid.side`, `grid.threat`, `E.Turns` charge time, `E.Story` | TACTICS |\n246\t| Real-time strategy (Warcraft II, StarCraft, Dune II) | `iso` | `E.Strategy` (the strategy module): kinds as data, selection by click, box and group, orders, gold, building, training, fog, an AI's build order and waves; `cam.free`, `{ pick: unit }` and `r.pick`, buildings as `E.SHAPES`, E.Shell's minimap; bodies drawn `every: 3` | RTS |\n247\t| Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity) | `iso` | paths as floor tags walked on an `E.Grid`, agents with `E.needs` picking by `E.choose`, `E.Clock` days and light, `game.timeScale` 0 to 4, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`; rides as shapes stepped through angles (`params`, `facing`) | SIM |\n248\t| 3D platformer or adventure remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash) | `iso` / `threequarter` / `brawler` | TileMap blocks of different heights + Body `jump()` (z is real height); in real 3D: `E.World3D` (the 3D engine's manual), `camera.lock` | ADVENTURE; 3D ACTION, 3D PLATFORMER |\n249\t| Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark) | the 3D engine's `'zones'` camera | `camera.zones` (fixed cameras by room), `camera.steer` (tank controls), `camera.lock` to aim, `world.through` doors, `world.flashlight`, fog; `E.RPG` inventory, `game.saves` | 3D HORROR |\n250\t| First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder) | the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes | cells of 2 x 2 tiles stepped and turned a quarter at a time (tweened), `E.Grid` paths for the monsters, `E.Turns` `'atb'` (real time) or `'speed'` (turns), `E.Fog` for the automap, `world.thing('door')` gates and `world.unlock`, `rig.drawPortrait` for the party | 3D CRAWLER |\n251\t| Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania, Space Channel 5) | any (a highway on the overlay) | `E.Rhythm` (the rhythm module): a chart of lane strings in parts on a song's steps, judged in ms of the music clock, combo, life, grade, holds, calls a teacher plays and you answer, `rh.draw` (`arrows: true`); the songbook's songs (`E.Songbook`); lanes as `input.zones` on a phone; `audio.onBeat` moves the dancer | RHYTHM |\n252\t| Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam) | `threequarter` / `topdown` | the ball an `E.Body` (`bounce`; curl: turn its velocity across its path), passes led to the teammate, `E.arc` for a lob, a shot's meter, formations by where the ball is, `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` | SPORTS |\n253\t| Third-person stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu) | `iso` / `threequarter` | TileMap roofs, Mover, `E.sees`, `E.Suspicion`, FlowField `jump` | STEALTH |\n254\t| Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero) | Mode 7 (the mode7 module, over any view) | `E.Mode7` over a TileMap track (floor tags: grass, sand, boost), `E.Car` karts (`kart: true`: hop, drift, boost) in an `E.Race` round an `E.Path` (laps, checkpoints, drivers, items), rigs in karts as billboards, `game.splitScreen(2)` for two | KART |\n255\t| 3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.track` (banked, walls, pads, `gaps` for a shortcut), `world.vehicle` karts raced by `E.Race`, split screen through `world.cameras` | 3D RACING |\n256\t| Rail shooters (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.flyer` on rails or free in bounds, rolls (`f.rolling`) turn shots aside, `f.seek` for a charged lock, `Dialog`'s radio | 3D FLIGHT |\n257\t| Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Road Rash) | the pseudo-3D road (the road module) | `E.Road` segments (curves, hills, tunnels), scenery and traffic placed on it, `curveAt` throwing the car outward, a clock and checkpoints | ROAD |\n258\t| Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA) | `topdown` / `iso` | `E.Car` on a TileMap (its floor tags slow it, its walls bounce it), `E.Race` for laps and drivers, cars drawn with `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points | KART |\n259\t| First-person shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007, Apex Legends) | the 3D engine's first person (or third) | a level of text rows or a terrain, `world.ray` hitscan, recoil and reloads, doors (`world.thing('door')`), raiders on `world.flow()` | 3D SHOOTER |\n260\t| Bomberman, Frogger, Sokoban, Gauntlet, Robotron; Sonic, Prince of Persia; light guns | as the recipe says | `AI_GUIDE.md`'s Genre recipes: each on the kit closest to it | (a recipe) |\n261\t\n262\t**Sizes:** heroes 40-60 px tall at 320x240 (`size: 1.2-1.4`), bosses 1.8-2.4. Small things (coins, bullets, ships, pickups) are string sprites designed at 8-16 px, drawn at scale 2 if they are the player's ship. Keep hitboxes smaller than the art.\n263\t\n264\t**Feel numbers** (world units per second, tile = 16): walk 70-90, run 95-140, jump 250-320 with gravity 800, fall cap 330, dash 200-260, bullets 250-320 (player) and 70-120 (enemy), enemy walk 25-45. Hit feedback: `game.hitFx` (Effects).\n265\t\n266\t## More: animation and examples outside this card\n267\tThe repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge; any file at `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`) holds more to take from. Its files are large: search them and take only what the game needs. Real 3D (`E.World3D`: first person, racing, flight) is there: `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`.\n268\t- **Motion clips**: 325 curated and 2,548 motion-capture takes that play on any Humanoid: find one in `src/mocap/catalogs/`, cut a small set with `tools/anim-set.mjs`, then `Mocap.drive(rig, lib)` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Where to find more; `docs/MOCAP.md`).\n269\t- **Objects and materials** (full engine): `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws a still object from any view and facing, `map.solid(...)` makes it solid; `E.MATERIALS` dresses floors (`floorMat`), walls (`face`, `roof`) and platform tiles (`mat`). `docs/ASSETS.md` is the card: the format, the library, writing one and checking it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs`.\n270\t- **Worked examples**: Emberdeep (`src/emberdeep/`), a whole game on this engine: 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies, 3 heroes and 30 skills, registered with `def(kind, id, spec)` (one search lists them); adapt them, they lean on the game's own helpers. `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body.\n271\t- **Modules** (`engine/modules/<name>.js`; `modules/` in the handoff package): general systems in files of their own, each a `<script>` after the engine, its header its manual (read it there, or its section of the index). A kit carries the modules its slice names (`@modules` in its header); the full engine (`dist/my-3d2dge.js`; `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package) carries them all.\n272\t  - `grid`: square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges over heights and past allies, fields of view, `E.Fog`, groups, shapes of effect, threat maps, `E.GridMover`, `E.Turns` (speed, charge time, phases, ATB). ROGUELIKE, PUZZLE, TACTICS, ARCADE, ARTILLERY, RTS, SIM, 3D CRAWLER.\n273\t  - `gen`: generated maps from a seed: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes, Poisson scatter, as ASCII rows a TileMap reads. ROGUELIKE, ARCADE.\n274\t  - `fight` (the full engine only): one-on-one fighting: `E.Fighter` (frame data, hurt boxes from the rig's joints, guards, throws, cancels, juggles, specials by motion or charge, a meter, projectiles), `E.Match` (rounds, KO, the HUD, training), `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`. FIGHTING.\n275\t  - `rooms`: a world of rooms on a grid of screens (`E.World`): walking between them at open edges, the explored map (`drawMap`, or `shellMap()` for `E.Shell`), gates that abilities or keys open, saves in `E.store`. METROIDVANIA.\n276\t  - `mode7`: the SNES's Mode 7 (`E.Mode7`): a camera over a flat floor (a TileMap's, a function's or a canvas), a turning sky, billboards far to near (rigs, sprites, shapes, your drawing), fog, `project` and `toFloor`, split screens. KART.\n277\t  - `road`: the pseudo-3D road (`E.Road`): segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, tunnels, scenery and cars scaled by distance and hidden past a crest, the sky's parallax on curves. ROAD.\n278\t  - `drive`: cars on the ground plane (`E.Car`: grip and slide, a kart's hop and drift boost, floor tags, walls and cars it bounces off) and races (`E.Race`: checkpoints along an `E.Path`, laps, places, drivers that pass and rubber-band, items, the HUD; the 3D engine's `world.vehicle` too). KART.\n279\t  - `story`: dialogue as data (`E.Story`): lines with speakers and portraits, choices with conditions, flags and values, gotos, the game's events, a quest log, balloons over heads, a backlog, auto and skip; its state for a save. RPG, POINT-AND-CLICK, TACTICS.\n280\t  - `rpg`: a role-playing game's rules (`E.RPG`): items, bags (slots or a grid of sizes), members with stats, levels and gear, status effects, a party (items used, rewards shared), shops, damage, random encounters by zone, list and card windows. RPG, 3D HORROR.\n281\t  - `strategy` (the full engine only; needs `grid`): `E.Strategy` for real-time strategy (selection by click, box and group; queued orders; formations; production, resources, placement; fog, the minimap; an AI's build order and waves) and for sims `E.Clock` (days, the light), `E.needs` and `E.choose`, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`. RTS, SIM.\n282\t  - `click` (the full engine only): point-and-click adventures (`E.Click`): painted rooms, walk areas with holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots with verbs, SCUMM's nine verbs, Sierra's icons, a verb coin or two buttons, an inventory, rules as data, exits, `story`'s talk; taps on a phone. POINT-AND-CLICK.\n283\t  - `physics`: 2D rigid bodies (`E.Physics`): circles, boxes, polygons, segments, chains and arcs; bounce, friction, sensors, layers, sleep; fast balls swept, never passing through; the same world at any refresh rate; flippers, bumpers, slingshots, kickers. PINBALL, ARTILLERY.\n284\t  - `terrain`: destructible ground in the side plane (`E.Terrain`): carved and added by circles and polygons, the ground below a point and its normal, rays for shots, walkers on slopes, a collider for `physics`. ARTILLERY.\n285\t  - `songs`: the songbook (`E.Songbook`): songs by mood (field, town, battle, race, horror, chase, dance ...) and jingles (stage clear, game over, level up); `get(id)`, `find(mood)`. RHYTHM.\n286\t  - `rhythm` (the full engine only): `E.Rhythm`: charts on a song's steps, judgments in ms of the music clock, combo, score, a life gauge, grades, holds, call and response, a note highway in any direction. RHYTHM.\n287\t  - `arcade`: the coin-op's frame: scores that float up, extra lives, two players taking turns, credits and a continue, high-score tables with initials on a letter wheel, stage cards, the attract mode (a demo, the table, the title). ARCADE, SHOOTER, PINBALL.\n288\t- **Everything running**: https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs\n289\t\n290\t## Before you hand the game back\n291\t- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge` (between the markers in a single file; `game/game.js` in the handoff package). Only your game: no leftover starter menu or unused slices. No network, no libraries.\n292\t- Title screen and game-over screen as scenes; `pausable: true` on gameplay scenes. No pause, settings, rebinding or full-screen code of your own (they're standard); prompts from `game.input.label(action)`.\n293\t- The camera follows the player (`game.focus` every update) and shows the level; the HUD uses screen pixels, or `E.Shell`'s HTML for a modern game (the STEALTH kit; the handoff package's template).\n294\t- Sound effects on jump, hit, pickup and death; music per scene.\n295\t- The look target list above: backdrop, textured tiles, props, sized and dressed HD characters, effects, title font, lights. Compare your screenshots with Symphony of the Night or Metal Slug, not with NES games.\n296\t- If you can run commands: `node tools/check.mjs game/index.html` (your page; add `#scene` to jump to a scene). It presses start, plays, cycles the views, prints how much of the screen is filled and how many colors it uses, adds look notes (flat areas, checkerboard dithering, low contrast, a thin palette), and fails on any error. Act on every look note. `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html#scene --steps \"wait:1500 press:Enter wait:800 rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records an animation frame by frame into one contact sheet: check every attack with it (a swing must wind up, strike, and settle back without jerks or backward sweeps).\n297\t- Animation variety: every character has its own moves from `E.MOVES` (not one shared swing), enemies telegraph with a visible wind-up, and reactions play out (hurt, knockback, `down`, victory `cheer`).\n298\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"84c2e270-5571-4489-8d2b-be0bdea7da25","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:19:33.649Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/dist/handoff/API.md","content":"**Humanoid** (knights, heroes, soldiers, zombies): `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build: 'chibi' (default) | 'heroic' (tall, SotN) | 'bulky' (brawlers, bosses) | 'skeleton' (rib cage, skull, glowing eye sockets; tint with colors.bone), weapon: 'sword' (the default) | 'gun' | 'staff' | null (fists), outfit: 'shirt' | 'tunic' | 'robe' | 'coat', hair: 'short' | 'spiky' | 'long' | 'ponytail' | 'bald', hat: 'cap' | 'pointed' | 'helmet' | 'band' | 'crown' (or { style, color }), sleeves: 'short' | 'long' | 'none', armor, hood (in colors.hair), cape: { len: 6, width: 5, seg: 2.5 }, eyeGlow, hunch, lean, style: 'hd' (default) | 'classic' (stick figures for huge crowds), colors: { skin, hair, cloth, coat, pants, boot, belt, trim, glove, cape, capeIn, metal, plate, hilt, eye, iris } })` (`metal` colors the weapon, `plate` the armor, defaulting to `metal`).\n- `rig.update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, aim, attack })`. `facing` is an angle (side view: 0 = right, `Math.PI` = left). `air: true` tucks the legs for jumps; `point: true` holds a gun forward; `aim` tilts it (radians, + = up: `aim: .8` shoots diagonally up, `Math.PI / 2` straight up).\n- **Moves** (`E.MOVES`, use them instead of hand-made specs; every one has anticipation, a committed strike with lunge / step / lean / twist, follow-through and a clean return to the stance, on every build and in every view):\n  - blades and clubs: `slash backslash overhead rising thrust spin plunge twohand`;\n  - fists: `jab` (left hand) `cross hook uppercut haymaker elbow`;\n  - kicks: `kick roundhouse sweep flyingkick knee axekick`;\n  - other: `cast` (both hands) `throw bash claw`.\n  Give each character its own set (a knight: `overhead`, `thrust`, `spin`; a mage: `cast`; a monk: `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse`; a boss: `haymaker`, `sweep`). `E.move('uppercut', .5)` is the attack state of any move at any moment (previews, cutscenes).\n- `attack: { spec, phase: 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover', u: 0..1 }`, spec `{ a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, r0, r1, hand, rel, blade, spin, kick, plane, lunge, hop, crouch, lean, twist, hold }`. Angles are relative to facing (+ = right hand side). `rel: true`: heights relative to the shoulder and reach scaled by arm length (kick heights as a share of hip height). `r0`/`r1`: reach that extends (thrusts, jabs). `hand: 'L' | 'both'`. In side views horizontal swings turn into the screen plane (a Castlevania chop) by themselves; `plane: 'side' | 'ground'` (or the rig option `swingPlane`) chooses. `lunge` steps into the strike (body motion in local units, clamped to -4..5; to move the character itself, add speed to your body), `hop` lifts the body, `crouch` dips in the wind-up, `hold` is how long the end pose holds (share of recover). You time the phases.\n- In steep ground views (threequarter) rigs and Blobs are drawn from a lower, sprite-like angle so faces show, like SNES RPG sprites (`charView: false` turns it off for one rig, `E.style.charPitch = false` for all).\n- Faces (eyes with catch-lights, brows, mouth), a cheated 3/4 turn toward the camera in side views (`cheat: .5`, 0 turns it off) and hair layers are automatic. `face: { eyes: 'big' (anime / Mana style), bangs: 0..1 }`.\n- Poses: `rig.update(dt, { ..., pose: 'cheer' | 'cast' | 'guard' | 'kneel' | 'crouch' | 'wave' | 'hips' | 'down' })` (victory with the weapon raised, spell casting, a boxer's guard, hurt or resting, ducking, greeting, a cocky idle, knocked out; they blend in and out), `'block'` (weapon held upright across the body), `'die'` (a stagger, the knees give, then the body topples), `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'` (faces and portraits), `run: 0..1` (run in place), or `down: 0..1` to lie a defeated body flat (centred on its position). `stance: 'guard'` keeps fists up while walking (brawlers; the free hand guards the chin during punches), `stance: 'ready'` holds the weapon forward. `climb: true` with `vz` climbs a ladder hand over hand (`Platformer.rigState()` passes both). Traversal: `crouch` (a sneaking gait), `sprint` (a lean), `hang` with `hangZ` (hands on an edge that high above the feet), `flip: 0..1` (a roll), `land: k` (a heavy landing); `mover.rigState()` sets them. Every wind-up starts from the rig's current pose, so chained hits and hand changes never snap. Idle rigs breathe and shift their weight by themselves.\n- `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, view)` inside `r.actor`, `rig.drawSmear(r)` (a glowing trail along the real blade, fist or foot path, in every view), `rig.debug(r)` (skeleton), `rig.kick(v)` (squash and stretch), `rig.hand()` and `rig.tip()` (world points to spawn bullets from), `rig.worldOffset(p)` (where a point in the rig's own frame, such as a joint in `rig.J`, sits from its feet: to draw or spawn at any joint), `rig.lastView` (the view it last drew with), `rig.solvePose()` (sets `rig.J` from its state each step: wrap it on one rig to move joints).\n**Blob** (slimes, rabbites, bats, imps, round monsters): `new E.Blob({ R: 6, colors: { dk, base, lt, spec, eye, pupil }, ears: 'rabbit' | 'cat', horns: true, wings: true, feet: true, tail: true, mouth: true | 'fangs', face: 'front' })`, `update(dt, { squash: -0.4..0.4, look: [dx, dy], squint, walk: 0..1, flap: 0..1 (wing speed; 0 folds them), hang: true (roost upside down) })`, `kick(v)`, `draw(g, ox, oy, view)`. Mix the parts for a bestiary: `wings` + `mouth: 'fangs'` is a bat, `ears: 'rabbit'` + `feet` a Mana rabbite, `horns` + `tail` an imp.\n\n## Effects and lighting\n- Particles: `game.particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` (fireball, smoke, sparks, debris, ring, shake, sound), `fire(x, y, z, n, { size })`, `smoke(x, y, z, n, { size, color })`, `glints(x, y, z, n, color)`, `dust(x, y, z, n)`, `sparks(x, y, z, n, angle)`, `bits(x, y, z, n, colors)`, `ring(x, y, r0, r1, color, dur)`, `text(x, y, z, '12', color, { scale: 2, bounce: true })` (bouncing damage numbers), `add({ kind: 'dust' | 'spark' | 'bit' | 'ember' | 'fire' | 'smoke' | 'glint', ... })`. `explosion(x, y, z, size, { debris, freeze: false, shake: 0 })` for chains of small blasts. Side-scrollers: `particles.ground = (x, y, z) => level.groundBelow(x, z)` so sparks and debris bounce on platforms. Screen flash: `game.flash('#ffffff', .12, strength 0..1)`.\n- Lighting (step 9; every view, side-scrollers included, no WebGPU needed): `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color: '#ffa050' })` every draw. Dark areas get a smooth cool tint, lights add their own color, and emissive items (flames, sparks, bullets, `emissive: true`) stay bright. `lights.darkness` (0..1) and `lights.tint` tune it; after setting `lights.dark = [r, g, b]`, `levels` or `glow`, call `lights.refresh()`. Props with flames can light the scene: `r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { light: true })`. Lamps, street lamps, lanterns and crystals glow on their own (`haloR`, `haloColor`, `halo: false`); `cone: true` adds a light cone under a street lamp.\n- Hits: `game.hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage: 12, textScale: 2, textZ: 22 })` does hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks, a bouncing damage number (`bounce: false` floats it) and a sound in one call; `particles.impact(x, y, z, size, color)` for the star alone.\n- GPU lighting (optional, ground views with a TileMap): `const gpu = game.enableGPU({ map })`; mark glowing things with `px.glow(g, 1)`; add `lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` and `lights.heat(x, y, z, w)`. Where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `game.lights.ambient` (`gpu.fallback = false`: unlit).\n\n## Genre playbook (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1)\n| Genre (examples) | View | Build it with | Copy slice |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Platformer (Mario, Mega Man, Sonic, Castlevania) | `side` (+ `brawler` for 2.5D) | PlatformMap, Platformer, Humanoid, sprites, Bullets `'side'` | PLATFORMER |\n| Metroidvania (Metroid, Symphony of the Night) | `side` | an `E.World` of PlatformMaps (the rooms module: doors at open edges, a slide, the explored map, gates opened by abilities, saves), a Platformer given `airJumps` and `dash` as they are found, its further moves, an arm cannon aimed eight ways (`point`, `aim`, `rig.tip()`, Bullets) | METROIDVANIA |\n| Action adventure (Zelda, Secret of Mana) | `threequarter` or `topdown` | TileMap rows, `cam.room`, Body, Humanoid + `E.Attack` sword, Dialog, `E.ui.hearts` | ADVENTURE |\n| Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword) | none: rooms painted in screen pixels (`r.overlay`) | `E.Click` (the click module): rooms with walk areas and holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots, the nine verbs, Sierra's icons or a verb coin, an inventory, puzzles as rules; talk through `E.Story` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n| Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad) | none | `E.Story` nodes and choices over a painted backdrop, `rig.drawPortrait` at size, `story.log` (the backlog), `story.auto`, `story.skip`; `game.saves` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n| Action RPG / dungeon (Diablo, Landstalker) | `iso` | TileMap, FlowField, Body (jump onto blocks), GPU lighting | ADVENTURE |\n| Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage, TMNT) | `brawler` | TileMap street strip, Body, Humanoid (`weapon: null`) + `E.Combo` punches and `kick: true` kicks, `E.knockback`, waves that lock the screen; a second player drops in (`game.allowJoin`, `cam.frame` on both) | BRAWLER |\n| Fighting (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers) | `side` | two `E.Fighter`s in an `E.Match` (the fight module): moves with frame data, guards, throws, specials by motion (`'236'`) or charge, a meter, projectiles; `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`, training | FIGHTING |\n| Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius, R-Type) | `overhead` (vertical, and horizontal: world x scrolls) | sprites, Bullets, `E.pattern`, a timeline of waves; horizontal: rock edges as functions of x, a power bar, options on the ship's past path, `E.Path` loops; `E.Arcade` for the continue and the table | SHOOTER (both stages) |\n| Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet, Devil's Crush) | a steep table view of its own (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`) | `E.Physics`: chains for the walls, `flipper`, `bumper`, `slingshot`, `kicker`, a ramp on a layer of its own, the ball a `bullet`; `input.zones` flip on a phone; `E.Arcade` for the score and table. Breakout and Asteroids with real collisions are its recipes | PINBALL |\n| Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth) | `side` at a whole scale (`new E.View(id, label, 0, 0, 1, 1)`) | `E.Terrain` (`carve` the blasts, `walk` the soldiers, `ray` the shots), grenades as `E.Physics` bodies on `land.collider()`, `E.Turns`; a drag aims (`steer = 'drag'`, `drag()`, `stickOn`); a CPU tries its shots in simulation | ARTILLERY |\n| Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids) | `overhead` (one fixed screen, cabinet art on the sides) | `E.Arcade` (score, lives, extra lives, turns, initials, tables, attract mode); a maze of `E.GridMover`s; moths on `E.Path`s turned by `rot`; `E.wrap` and `wrap: [w, h]` | ARCADE |\n| Twin-stick / run-and-gun (Smash TV, Contra) | `topdown` / `side` | Body or Platformer, Bullets, `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` | SHOOTER + ADVENTURE |\n| JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy, EarthBound) | `threequarter` town, `brawler` battles | TileMap towns and a big world map, the story and rpg modules, Dialog with markup, `game.saves` slots | RPG |\n| Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo, Columns) | `side` (a field in screen pixels) | a cell array, a bag from `E.rng`, `input.repeat` (auto-shift), `grid.groups` (chains), `input.drag()` and `clicked()` for touch; versus: two fields, rows sent across (`game.players`) | PUZZLE |\n| Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren) | `threequarter` / `iso` | `E.gen` floors, a TileMap of its rows, `E.Grid.from(map)` paths and `fov`, `E.Fog`, `E.Turns` by speed, `game.tween` steps | ROGUELIKE |\n| Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem) | `iso`, turned a quarter | TileMap of block heights, `map.pick` under the cursor, `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true })` reach over heights, `grid.shape`, `grid.side`, `grid.threat`, `E.Turns` charge time, `E.Story` | TACTICS |\n| Real-time strategy (Warcraft II, StarCraft, Dune II) | `iso` | `E.Strategy` (the strategy module): kinds as data, selection by click, box and group, orders, gold, building, training, fog, an AI's build order and waves; `cam.free`, `{ pick: unit }` and `r.pick`, buildings as `E.SHAPES`, E.Shell's minimap; bodies drawn `every: 3` | RTS |\n| Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity) | `iso` | paths as floor tags walked on an `E.Grid`, agents with `E.needs` picking by `E.choose`, `E.Clock` days and light, `game.timeScale` 0 to 4, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`; rides as shapes stepped through angles (`params`, `facing`) | SIM |\n| 3D platformer or adventure remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash) | `iso` / `threequarter` / `brawler` | TileMap blocks of different heights + Body `jump()` (z is real height); in real 3D: `E.World3D` (the 3D engine's manual), `camera.lock` | ADVENTURE; 3D ACTION, 3D PLATFORMER |\n| Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark) | the 3D engine's `'zones'` camera | `camera.zones` (fixed cameras by room), `camera.steer` (tank controls), `camera.lock` to aim, `world.through` doors, `world.flashlight`, fog; `E.RPG` inventory, `game.saves` | 3D HORROR |\n| First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder) | the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes | cells of 2 x 2 tiles stepped and turned a quarter at a time (tweened), `E.Grid` paths for the monsters, `E.Turns` `'atb'` (real time) or `'speed'` (turns), `E.Fog` for the automap, `world.thing('door')` gates and `world.unlock`, `rig.drawPortrait` for the party | 3D CRAWLER |\n| Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania, Space Channel 5) | any (a highway on the overlay) | `E.Rhythm` (the rhythm module): a chart of lane strings in parts on a song's steps, judged in ms of the music clock, combo, life, grade, holds, calls a teacher plays and you answer, `rh.draw` (`arrows: true`); the songbook's songs (`E.Songbook`); lanes as `input.zones` on a phone; `audio.onBeat` moves the dancer | RHYTHM |\n| Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam) | `threequarter` / `topdown` | the ball an `E.Body` (`bounce`; curl: turn its velocity across its path), passes led to the teammate, `E.arc` for a lob, a shot's meter, formations by where the ball is, `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` | SPORTS |\n| Third-person stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu) | `iso` / `threequarter` | TileMap roofs, Mover, `E.sees`, `E.Suspicion`, FlowField `jump` | STEALTH |\n| Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero) | Mode 7 (the mode7 module, over any view) | `E.Mode7` over a TileMap track (floor tags: grass, sand, boost), `E.Car` karts (`kart: true`: hop, drift, boost) in an `E.Race` round an `E.Path` (laps, checkpoints, drivers, items), rigs in karts as billboards, `game.splitScreen(2)` for two | KART |\n| 3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.track` (banked, walls, pads, `gaps` for a shortcut), `world.vehicle` karts raced by `E.Race`, split screen through `world.cameras` | 3D RACING |\n| Rail shooters (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.flyer` on rails or free in bounds, rolls (`f.rolling`) turn shots aside, `f.seek` for a charged lock, `Dialog`'s radio | 3D FLIGHT |\n| Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Road Rash) | the pseudo-3D road (the road module) | `E.Road` segments (curves, hills, tunnels), scenery and traffic placed on it, `curveAt` throwing the car outward, a clock and checkpoints | ROAD |\n| Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA) | `topdown` / `iso` | `E.Car` on a TileMap (its floor tags slow it, its walls bounce it), `E.Race` for laps and drivers, cars drawn with `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points | KART |\n| First-person shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007, Apex Legends) | the 3D engine's first person (or third) | a level of text rows or a terrain, `world.ray` hitscan, recoil and reloads, doors (`world.thing('door')`), raiders on `world.flow()` | 3D SHOOTER |\n| Bomberman, Frogger, Sokoban, Gauntlet, Robotron; Sonic, Prince of Persia; light guns | as the recipe says | `AI_GUIDE.md`'s Genre recipes: each on the kit closest to it | (a recipe) |\n\n**Sizes:** heroes 40-60 px tall at 320x240 (`size: 1.2-1.4`), bosses 1.8-2.4. Small things (coins, bullets, ships, pickups) are string sprites designed at 8-16 px, drawn at scale 2 if they are the player's ship. Keep hitboxes smaller than the art.\n\n**Feel numbers** (world units per second, tile = 16): walk 70-90, run 95-140, jump 250-320 with gravity 800, fall cap 330, dash 200-260, bullets 250-320 (player) and 70-120 (enemy), enemy walk 25-45. Hit feedback: `game.hitFx` (Effects).\n\n## More: animation and examples outside this card\nThe repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge; any file at `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`) holds more to take from. Its files are large: search them and take only what the game needs. Real 3D (`E.World3D`: first person, racing, flight) is there: `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`.\n- **Motion clips**: 325 curated and 2,548 motion-capture takes that play on any Humanoid: find one in `src/mocap/catalogs/`, cut a small set with `tools/anim-set.mjs`, then `Mocap.drive(rig, lib)` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Where to find more; `docs/MOCAP.md`).\n- **Objects and materials** (full engine): `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws a still object from any view and facing, `map.solid(...)` makes it solid; `E.MATERIALS` dresses floors (`floorMat`), walls (`face`, `roof`) and platform tiles (`mat`). `docs/ASSETS.md` is the card: the format, the library, writing one and checking it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs`.\n- **Worked examples**: Emberdeep (`src/emberdeep/`), a whole game on this engine: 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies, 3 heroes and 30 skills, registered with `def(kind, id, spec)` (one search lists them); adapt them, they lean on the game's own helpers. `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body.\n- **Modules** (`engine/modules/<name>.js`; `modules/` in the handoff package): general systems in files of their own, each a `<script>` after the engine, its header its manual (read it there, or its section of the index). A kit carries the modules its slice names (`@modules` in its header); the full engine (`dist/my-3d2dge.js`; `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package) carries them all.\n  - `grid`: square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges over heights and past allies, fields of view, `E.Fog`, groups, shapes of effect, threat maps, `E.GridMover`, `E.Turns` (speed, charge time, phases, ATB). ROGUELIKE, PUZZLE, TACTICS, ARCADE, ARTILLERY, RTS, SIM, 3D CRAWLER.\n  - `gen`: generated maps from a seed: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes, Poisson scatter, as ASCII rows a TileMap reads. ROGUELIKE, ARCADE.\n  - `fight` (the full engine only): one-on-one fighting: `E.Fighter` (frame data, hurt boxes from the rig's joints, guards, throws, cancels, juggles, specials by motion or charge, a meter, projectiles), `E.Match` (rounds, KO, the HUD, training), `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`. FIGHTING.\n  - `rooms`: a world of rooms on a grid of screens (`E.World`): walking between them at open edges, the explored map (`drawMap`, or `shellMap()` for `E.Shell`), gates that abilities or keys open, saves in `E.store`. METROIDVANIA.\n  - `mode7`: the SNES's Mode 7 (`E.Mode7`): a camera over a flat floor (a TileMap's, a function's or a canvas), a turning sky, billboards far to near (rigs, sprites, shapes, your drawing), fog, `project` and `toFloor`, split screens. KART.\n  - `road`: the pseudo-3D road (`E.Road`): segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, tunnels, scenery and cars scaled by distance and hidden past a crest, the sky's parallax on curves. ROAD.\n  - `drive`: cars on the ground plane (`E.Car`: grip and slide, a kart's hop and drift boost, floor tags, walls and cars it bounces off) and races (`E.Race`: checkpoints along an `E.Path`, laps, places, drivers that pass and rubber-band, items, the HUD; the 3D engine's `world.vehicle` too). KART.\n  - `story`: dialogue as data (`E.Story`): lines with speakers and portraits, choices with conditions, flags and values, gotos, the game's events, a quest log, balloons over heads, a backlog, auto and skip; its state for a save. RPG, POINT-AND-CLICK, TACTICS.\n  - `rpg`: a role-playing game's rules (`E.RPG`): items, bags (slots or a grid of sizes), members with stats, levels and gear, status effects, a party (items used, rewards shared), shops, damage, random encounters by zone, list and card windows. RPG, 3D HORROR.\n  - `strategy` (the full engine only; needs `grid`): `E.Strategy` for real-time strategy (selection by click, box and group; queued orders; formations; production, resources, placement; fog, the minimap; an AI's build order and waves) and for sims `E.Clock` (days, the light), `E.needs` and `E.choose`, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`. RTS, SIM.\n  - `click` (the full engine only): point-and-click adventures (`E.Click`): painted rooms, walk areas with holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots with verbs, SCUMM's nine verbs, Sierra's icons, a verb coin or two buttons, an inventory, rules as data, exits, `story`'s talk; taps on a phone. POINT-AND-CLICK.\n  - `physics`: 2D rigid bodies (`E.Physics`): circles, boxes, polygons, segments, chains and arcs; bounce, friction, sensors, layers, sleep; fast balls swept, never passing through; the same world at any refresh rate; flippers, bumpers, slingshots, kickers. PINBALL, ARTILLERY.\n  - `terrain`: destructible ground in the side plane (`E.Terrain`): carved and added by circles and polygons, the ground below a point and its normal, rays for shots, walkers on slopes, a collider for `physics`. ARTILLERY.\n  - `songs`: the songbook (`E.Songbook`): songs by mood (field, town, battle, race, horror, chase, dance ...) and jingles (stage clear, game over, level up); `get(id)`, `find(mood)`. RHYTHM.\n  - `rhythm` (the full engine only): `E.Rhythm`: charts on a song's steps, judgments in ms of the music clock, combo, score, a life gauge, grades, holds, call and response, a note highway in any direction. RHYTHM.\n  - `arcade`: the coin-op's frame: scores that float up, extra lives, two players taking turns, credits and a continue, high-score tables with initials on a letter wheel, stage cards, the attract mode (a demo, the table, the title). ARCADE, SHOOTER, PINBALL.\n- **Everything running**: https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs\n\n## Before you hand the game back\n- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge` (between the markers in a single file; `game/game.js` in the handoff package). Only your game: no leftover starter menu or unused slices. No network, no libraries.\n- Title screen and game-over screen as scenes; `pausable: true` on gameplay scenes. No pause, settings, rebinding or full-screen code of your own (they're standard); prompts from `game.input.label(action)`.\n- The camera follows the player (`game.focus` every update) and shows the level; the HUD uses screen pixels, or `E.Shell`'s HTML for a modern game (the STEALTH kit; the handoff package's template).\n- Sound effects on jump, hit, pickup and death; music per scene.\n- The look target list above: backdrop, textured tiles, props, sized and dressed HD characters, effects, title font, lights. Compare your screenshots with Symphony of the Night or Metal Slug, not with NES games.\n- If you can run commands: `node tools/check.mjs game/index.html` (your page; add `#scene` to jump to a scene). It presses start, plays, cycles the views, prints how much of the screen is filled and how many colors it uses, adds look notes (flat areas, checkerboard dithering, low contrast, a thin palette), and fails on any error. Act on every look note. `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html#scene --steps \"wait:1500 press:Enter wait:800 rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records an animation frame by frame into one contact sheet: check every attack with it (a swing must wind up, strike, and settle back without jerks or backward sweeps).\n- Animation variety: every character has its own moves from `E.MOVES` (not one shared swing), enemies telegraph with a visible wind-up, and reactions play out (hurt, knockback, `down`, victory `cheer`).\n","numLines":94,"startLine":205,"totalLines":298}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KgTP9sYZjbBRA2xtdesSih","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
