{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9aiCLiAuKDZjgFbzr1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TTeJYadWRw3iVDEYDhuQZ2","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/_research/hub; cat CLAUDE.md | cut -c1-400; grep -n -i \"on its own\\|hub:players\\|hub:type\\|local multiplayer\\|multiplayer\\|hub:genre\\|hub:tags\\|session-\\|meta name\" README.md | cut -c1-240 | head -40","description":"Search hub repo docs for catalogue tags and multiplayer notes"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4139,"cache_read_input_tokens":260349,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4139},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"b08a3bcd-4b36-41da-a712-156a49289e12","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:27:32.928Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9ahwDCxwuYoWSgxhuK","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01TTeJYadWRw3iVDEYDhuQZ2":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/_research/hub; cat CLAUDE.md | cut -c1-400; grep -n -i \"on its own\\|hub:players\\|hub:type\\|local multiplayer\\|multiplayer\\|hub:genre\\|hub:tags\\|session-\\|meta name\" README.md | cut -c1-240 | head -40","description":"Search hub repo docs for catalogue tags and multiplayer notes"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TTeJYadWRw3iVDEYDhuQZ2","type":"tool_result","content":"# Working in this repo\n\n## Finish every change on main\n\n- When a `/goal` is complete, or a prompt has changed the repo, commit and\n  push to `origin/main` right away.\n- Pushing does not deploy. Vercel usage costs too much to build every\n  change, so Git deploys are off (`\"git\": { \"deploymentEnabled\": false }`\n  in `vercel.json`) and the site runs locally: `pnpm dev`, then\n  http://127.0.0.1:7417. Deploy to Vercel only when Michael explicitly asks,\n  for a major update: push with `deploymentEnabled` set to `true` (that push\n  deploys; check it reaches READY), then set it back to `false` in the next\n  commit, which does not deploy.\n- Work on `main`. If a branch is ever needed, merge it into main and delete\n  it (locally and on GitHub) in the same session. Never leave a local\n  branch, an unpushed commit, a stash, or an open PR behind.\n- Before starting work, run `git fetch --prune` and look at\n  `gh pr list`. Anything open gets resolved first.\n\n## Pull requests from others\n\nDecide each one; do not let it sit. Accept it (merge to main, delete the\nbranch), reject it (close it with a one-line reason), or replace it (close\nit, and land a better version on main that says which PR it replaces).\n\n## Keep it simple and clean\n\n- No dependencies, no build step, no generated files committed\n  (`data/games.json` is gitignored and rebuilt by `scripts/scan.mjs`).\n- Run `pnpm test` (or `node --test \"scripts/**/*.test.mjs\"`) before\n  committing code.\n- Delete what is no longer used instead of leaving it around.\n\n## When something goes wrong\n\nRoll back rather than leave half-finished work. Revert the commit\n(`git revert`) or restore the last good state and push that, so main is\nalways a working wall. Say what was rolled back and why.\n37:| `<meta name=\"description\">` | The blurb in the drawer |\n38:| `<meta name=\"theme-color\">` | That cabinet's marquee colour and the light it throws |\n267:If a game misbehaves in a frame, *Open on its own page* in its Details drawer\n287:Players is *Single player* or *Multiplayer*, and Multiplayer opens head counts\n422:record, **F** favourites, **O** opens a game on its own page, **/** writes a\n494:[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\n495:why, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n500:not a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n518:each with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\n564:  on its own compared with the original.\n654:- `node scripts/session-browser.mjs` plays a whole session in four tabs: a\n734:own, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\n750:| `players` | `1–2`, `1–4`… only when a game has co-op or versus | the Players rail: *Single player*, or *Multiplayer*, which drops a menu of head counts (*4 players* finds every game that seats four). A game with no `players` is single\n757:checks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n841:  session-browser.mjs  a whole session in four tabs, end to end","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"4b19b995-2d46-4551-90c9-e779a1e8373c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:27:32.967Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Working in this repo\n\n## Finish every change on main\n\n- When a `/goal` is complete, or a prompt has changed the repo, commit and\n  push to `origin/main` right away.\n- Pushing does not deploy. Vercel usage costs too much to build every\n  change, so Git deploys are off (`\"git\": { \"deploymentEnabled\": false }`\n  in `vercel.json`) and the site runs locally: `pnpm dev`, then\n  http://127.0.0.1:7417. Deploy to Vercel only when Michael explicitly asks,\n  for a major update: push with `deploymentEnabled` set to `true` (that push\n  deploys; check it reaches READY), then set it back to `false` in the next\n  commit, which does not deploy.\n- Work on `main`. If a branch is ever needed, merge it into main and delete\n  it (locally and on GitHub) in the same session. Never leave a local\n  branch, an unpushed commit, a stash, or an open PR behind.\n- Before starting work, run `git fetch --prune` and look at\n  `gh pr list`. Anything open gets resolved first.\n\n## Pull requests from others\n\nDecide each one; do not let it sit. Accept it (merge to main, delete the\nbranch), reject it (close it with a one-line reason), or replace it (close\nit, and land a better version on main that says which PR it replaces).\n\n## Keep it simple and clean\n\n- No dependencies, no build step, no generated files committed\n  (`data/games.json` is gitignored and rebuilt by `scripts/scan.mjs`).\n- Run `pnpm test` (or `node --test \"scripts/**/*.test.mjs\"`) before\n  committing code.\n- Delete what is no longer used instead of leaving it around.\n\n## When something goes wrong\n\nRoll back rather than leave half-finished work. Revert the commit\n(`git revert`) or restore the last good state and push that, so main is\nalways a working wall. Say what was rolled back and why.\n37:| `<meta name=\"description\">` | The blurb in the drawer |\n38:| `<meta name=\"theme-color\">` | That cabinet's marquee colour and the light it throws |\n267:If a game misbehaves in a frame, *Open on its own page* in its Details drawer\n287:Players is *Single player* or *Multiplayer*, and Multiplayer opens head counts\n422:record, **F** favourites, **O** opens a game on its own page, **/** writes a\n494:[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\n495:why, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n500:not a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n518:each with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\n564:  on its own compared with the original.\n654:- `node scripts/session-browser.mjs` plays a whole session in four tabs: a\n734:own, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\n750:| `players` | `1–2`, `1–4`… only when a game has co-op or versus | the Players rail: *Single player*, or *Multiplayer*, which drops a menu of head counts (*4 players* finds every game that seats four). A game with no `players` is single\n757:checks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n841:  session-browser.mjs  a whole session in four tabs, end to end","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TTeJYadWRw3iVDEYDhuQZ2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9bWyRv6mhTqdSGsRSw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AyTmHwHPvDCy5UGHWLYC9N","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/_research/hub; sed -n 25,48p README.md | cut -c1-260; sed -n 495,525p README.md | cut -c1-300; sed -n 728,765p README.md | cut -c1-320","description":"Read hub README sections on tags and sessions"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2257,"cache_read_input_tokens":264488,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2257},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"b4031d79-db06-48f4-b464-43102c1c4ed0","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:27:35.998Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9bWiJgY4i1XJvZFodz","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01AyTmHwHPvDCy5UGHWLYC9N":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211853__x57f432/work/_research/engine/_research/hub; sed -n 25,48p README.md | cut -c1-260; sed -n 495,525p README.md | cut -c1-300; sed -n 728,765p README.md | cut -c1-320","description":"Read hub README sections on tags and sessions"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AyTmHwHPvDCy5UGHWLYC9N","type":"tool_result","content":"```bash\ngit add games/ && git commit -m \"Add Whatever You Called It\" && git push\n```\n\n### Where the game's details come from\n\nNothing is typed in by hand. On every build, `scripts/scan.mjs` reads just the\n`<head>` of each file for catalogue metadata (the audio inventory also reads source text and asset paths):\n\n| From the game | Becomes |\n|---|---|\n| `<title>Undertow — Seven Guardians Below</title>` | Name **Undertow**, tagline **Seven Guardians Below** |\n| `<meta name=\"description\">` | The blurb in the drawer |\n| `<meta name=\"theme-color\">` | That cabinet's marquee colour and the light it throws |\n| `<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:…\">` | The emblem on its screen (a relative path works in a project folder) |\n| `<meta property=\"og:image\">` | Its cover art, in a project folder that ships the image |\n| file size, the date first added to the hub | The File size rail, the Newest sort |\n\nA game with none of these still works — it gets its filename as a name and a\ncolour derived from its slug.\n\n### Names on the wall\n\nNo two entries show the same name. When names collide (case and accents\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n```\n\nHere is the part worth knowing: **the filter rails are not declared anywhere.**\nThey are read off whatever the games actually carry. Fill in `model` on a few\ngames and a Model rail appears on the wall by itself; remove them and it goes.\nAny field works, including ones invented on the spot. A game can also carry its\nown, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\nvalues are nearly all different (a build id, a URL) is searchable but never\nbecomes a rail. Every field is searchable. `harness` is searched and shown in\nthe drawer but never gets a rail, and neither do `source` and `series`, which\nhave Original, Inspired and the stacks.\n\n`collection` comes from the folder a game sits in, but it is an ordinary field:\nan override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\n\nThe library's own catalogue uses these fields, all in `data/overrides.json`:\n\n| Field | What it is | On the wall |\n|---|---|---|\n| `genre` | one of a fixed list (Racing, Platformer, Life Sim, Tech Demo…) | the Genre rail, and the cabinet's panel |\n| `type` | `Game`, `Tech demo` or `Tool` | the shelf: games, or tech demos & tools |\n| `tags` | 2–5 from a fixed vocabulary (`3d`, `retro`, `co-op`, `touch`…) | the Tags rail |\n| `players` | `1–2`, `1–4`… only when a game has co-op or versus | the Players rail: *Single player*, or *Multiplayer*, which drops a menu of head counts (*4 players* finds every game that seats four). A game with no `players` is single player |\n| `source` | the classic a game riffs on, as it is titled (`Dragon Quest IX`); none for an original | Original or Inspired, the stacks, the drawer, and search |\n| `series` | the franchise, only when it is not the source itself (`Dragon Quest` for `Dragon Quest IX`) | the stacks |\n| `controls` | a one-line summary | the drawer |\n| `description` | one or two plain sentences | the drawer |\n\nEvery game with the same `source` must have the same `series`; `pnpm test`\nchecks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\nGenre, Collection, Type and Players follow it when they exist; the\nrest follow by how many games carry them. `docs/curation-2026-09-24.md`\nrecords how the library was curated: which file of each game was kept, and\nwhich older iterations it replaced. `docs/curation-2026-09-28.md` records the\nimport from the source-named GPT-games folder and maps the old slugs to the\ncurrent ones; `docs/curation-2026-09-28-new-ai-stuff.md` records the smaller\nimport that followed it.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c21bae92-846f-4b71-8d0d-098b4791d47c","uuid":"32a45bc0-99cf-4f3a-9a71-a985ca3d3c0a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:27:36.035Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"```bash\ngit add games/ && git commit -m \"Add Whatever You Called It\" && git push\n```\n\n### Where the game's details come from\n\nNothing is typed in by hand. On every build, `scripts/scan.mjs` reads just the\n`<head>` of each file for catalogue metadata (the audio inventory also reads source text and asset paths):\n\n| From the game | Becomes |\n|---|---|\n| `<title>Undertow — Seven Guardians Below</title>` | Name **Undertow**, tagline **Seven Guardians Below** |\n| `<meta name=\"description\">` | The blurb in the drawer |\n| `<meta name=\"theme-color\">` | That cabinet's marquee colour and the light it throws |\n| `<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:…\">` | The emblem on its screen (a relative path works in a project folder) |\n| `<meta property=\"og:image\">` | Its cover art, in a project folder that ships the image |\n| file size, the date first added to the hub | The File size rail, the Newest sort |\n\nA game with none of these still works — it gets its filename as a name and a\ncolour derived from its slug.\n\n### Names on the wall\n\nNo two entries show the same name. When names collide (case and accents\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n```\n\nHere is the part worth knowing: **the filter rails are not declared anywhere.**\nThey are read off whatever the games actually carry. Fill in `model` on a few\ngames and a Model rail appears on the wall by itself; remove them and it goes.\nAny field works, including ones invented on the spot. A game can also carry its\nown, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\nvalues are nearly all different (a build id, a URL) is searchable but never\nbecomes a rail. Every field is searchable. `harness` is searched and shown in\nthe drawer but never gets a rail, and neither do `source` and `series`, which\nhave Original, Inspired and the stacks.\n\n`collection` comes from the folder a game sits in, but it is an ordinary field:\nan override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\n\nThe library's own catalogue uses these fields, all in `data/overrides.json`:\n\n| Field | What it is | On the wall |\n|---|---|---|\n| `genre` | one of a fixed list (Racing, Platformer, Life Sim, Tech Demo…) | the Genre rail, and the cabinet's panel |\n| `type` | `Game`, `Tech demo` or `Tool` | the shelf: games, or tech demos & tools |\n| `tags` | 2–5 from a fixed vocabulary (`3d`, `retro`, `co-op`, `touch`…) | the Tags rail |\n| `players` | `1–2`, `1–4`… only when a game has co-op or versus | the Players rail: *Single player*, or *Multiplayer*, which drops a menu of head counts (*4 players* finds every game that seats four). A game with no `players` is single player |\n| `source` | the classic a game riffs on, as it is titled (`Dragon Quest IX`); none for an original | Original or Inspired, the stacks, the drawer, and search |\n| `series` | the franchise, only when it is not the source itself (`Dragon Quest` for `Dragon Quest IX`) | the stacks |\n| `controls` | a one-line summary | the drawer |\n| `description` | one or two plain sentences | the drawer |\n\nEvery game with the same `source` must have the same `series`; `pnpm test`\nchecks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\nGenre, Collection, Type and Players follow it when they exist; the\nrest follow by how many games carry them. `docs/curation-2026-09-24.md`\nrecords how the library was curated: which file of each game was kept, and\nwhich older iterations it replaced. `docs/curation-2026-09-28.md` records the\nimport from the source-named GPT-games folder and maps the old slugs to the\ncurrent ones; `docs/curation-2026-09-28-new-ai-stuff.md` records the smaller\nimport that followed it.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AyTmHwHPvDCy5UGHWLYC9N","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
